<h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Verbal noun (neuter), formed on root.</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Worthy of</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>going</li></ol><p>From the root to go. When used as the second element of an upapada tatpurusha, the vowel and final verbal roots ending in a nasal are reduced to their zero grade, /a/, which is then reinterpreted as the thematic vowel.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Adjective in -ī&#47;-inī&#47;-i, meaning possessing what is denoted by the base.</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>being like, being of, having</li><li>descended from, being of rank of, belonging to the class of</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>jāti</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Creating the thing or performing the action implied by the noun the suffix is attached to.</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Adjectival suffix meaning wanting to have or wanting to do what is denoted by the first element.</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who works with the object denoted by the prefix</li><li>one who makes the object or state denoted by the prefix</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>-कार</i> (-kāra); synchronically, as from the root of the verb karoti.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Abounding in thing identified by the noun the suffix is added to</li><li>Possessing the quality of state indicated by the noun the suffix is added to</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>-मत्</i> (-mat).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go [to]</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>This suffix forms a class of neuter abstract nouns from adjectives and nouns, and express the state, nature or quality of being that which is denoted by the adjective or noun.</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Abounding in thing identified by the noun the suffix is added to</li><li>Possessing the quality of state indicated by the noun the suffix is added to</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>-वत्</i> (-vat).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in combination</i>) Having a specified degree of age.</li></ol><p><i>āyu</i> +&lrm; <i>-ka</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li><i>Devanagari script form of</i> -kara</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li><i>Thai script form of</i> -jātika (“descended from, like”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li><i>Thai script form of</i> -āyuka (“having a specified degree of age”)</li></ol><p><i>āyu</i> +&lrm; <i>-ka</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li><i>Tai Tham script form of</i> -kara</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li><i>Tai Tham script form of</i> -āyuka (“having a specified degree of age”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cloud</li><li>sky</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अभ्र</i> (abhra), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;abʰrás</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;n̥bʰrós</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having arrived</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guest</li></ol><p>As though inherited Sanskrit <i>अभ्यागत</i> (abhyāgata), but can be <i>abhi-</i> +&lrm; <i>āgata</i>. The noun is derived from the adjective.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>safe, fearless</li></ol><p><i>a-</i> +&lrm; <i>bhaya</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>Predominantly verbal prefix:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>towards</li><li>over</li></ol></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अभि-</i> (abhi-).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>name, appellation, expression</li></ol><p>Probably borrowed from Sanskrit <i>अभिधान</i> (abhidhāna); arguably a calque composed as <i>abhi-</i> +&lrm; <i>dhāna</i> (“receptacle”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>last evening</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not breaking or separating</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>&#42;अभिद्यमान</i> (&#42;abhidyamāna) or more simply <i>a-</i> (“not”) +&lrm; <i>bhijjamāna</i> (“being broken”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>covetousness</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to know, to learn, to ascertain</li><li>to perceive</li><li>to admit, to acknowledge</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to create, to cause to appear by supernatural power</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend towards, to turn or direct to</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring into existence</li><li>to produce</li><li>to effect</li><li>to work</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to concentrate on</li><li>to pay respect to</li></ol><p><i>abhi-</i> +&lrm; <i>ropeti</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>beautiful</li><li>lovely</li></ol><p><i>abhi-</i> +&lrm; <i>rūpa</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be angry; to scold</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drench, to rain upon</li></ol><p><i>abhi-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;sandeti</i> (“hypothetical causative of <i>sandati</i> (“to flow”)”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anointing, consecration, inauguration</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अभिषेक</i> (abhiṣeká). By surface analysis, <i>abhi-</i> +&lrm; <i>seka</i>. Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀅𑀪𑀺𑀲𑁂𑀅</i> (abhisea).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand out supreme, to excel, surpass</li></ol><p><i>abhi-</i> +&lrm; <i>tiṭṭhati</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>To make haste, be quick.</li></ol><p>(<i>abhi-</i> +&lrm; <i>tarati</i>. According to PED wrong for abhittarati) to make haste.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to conquer</li></ol><p><i>abhi-</i> +&lrm; <i>vijināti</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to salute</li><li>to greet</li></ol><p>causative of <i>abhivadati</i> (“to declare”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to contend with</li></ol><p><i>abhi-</i> +&lrm; <i>yujjhati</i> (“to fight”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>well-known; recognised</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>recollection</li></ol><p>Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀅𑀳𑀺𑀡𑁆𑀡𑀸𑀡</i> (ahiṇṇāṇa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not real</li><li>untrue, false</li></ol><p><i>a-</i> +&lrm; <i>bhūta</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ati-</i> (“over”), used before vowels; see there for further details</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>continuous</li><li>thorough</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>passing away, (<i>time</i>) lapse</li><li>death</li></ol><p>Formed from <i>acceti</i> (“to elapse”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to elapse</li><li>to overcome</li><li>to honour</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bear</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>marvelous</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>आश्चर्य</i> (āścarya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mythology</i>) celestial nymph</li><li>the act of snapping one's fingers; the act of bringing together the fingertips</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अप्सराः</i> (apsarā́ḥ), nominative singular of <i>अप्सरस्</i> (apsaras).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a ray of light, a beam, flame</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अर्चि</i> (arcí), <i>अर्चिस्</i> (arcís). Compare Sauraseni Prakrit <i>𑀅𑀘𑁆𑀘𑀺</i> (acci).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>everlasting, eternal (<i>also as an epithet for nirvana</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अच्युत</i> (acyuta). See also <i>a-</i>, <i>cuta</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>moist</li><li>green</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>आर्द्र</i> (ārdra).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>path</li><li>time</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अध्वन्</i> (ádhvan), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;Hádʰwan</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a long path, high road</li></ol><p>Compound of addhāna and magga. Also found in Sanskrit.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mountain</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eat</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अत्ति</i> (átti).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>up to</li><li>over</li><li>on</li><li>above</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>अधि</i> (adhi).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get to</li><li>to acquire</li><li>to attain</li><li>to understand</li></ol><p><i>adhi-</i> +&lrm; <i>gacchati</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to surpass</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>exceeding; superior</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>अधिक</i> (adhika).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>supervision, administration</li><li>relation, cause, consequence</li><li>question, dispute, lawsuit</li></ol><p><i>adhi-</i> +&lrm; <i>karaṇa</i>. Related to Sanskrit <i>अधिकरण</i> (adhikaraṇa), though the exact relationship is unclear. It could be a common inheritance or a loan in either direction, possibly varying by sense.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>management</li><li>office</li><li>aspiration</li><li>chapter, section</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अधिकार</i> (adhikāra); analysable as <i>adhi-</i> +&lrm; <i>kāra</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lord, ruler, master</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Sanskrit <i>अधिपति</i> (adhipati). By surface analysis, <i>adhi</i> +&lrm; <i>pati</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>higher wisdom</li></ol><p><i>adhi-</i> +&lrm; <i>paññā</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intention</li></ol><p>From <i>adhi</i> + a component <i>ppāya</i> reflected in the latter two syllables of Sanskrit <i>अभिप्राय</i> (abhiprāya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superb</li><li>excellent</li><li>surpassing</li></ol><p><i>adhi-</i> +&lrm; <i>vara</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Buddhism</i>) will, determination, resolution: one of the Ten Perfections.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अधिष्ठान</i> (adhiṣṭhāna).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand on</li><li>to remain firm in</li><li>to devote oneself to</li><li>to perform</li></ol><p>Semantically as thouɡh <i>adhi-</i> +&lrm; <i>tiṭṭhati</i>, but with a different stem formation, which Geiger attributes to analogy with <i>yāti</i>. There is a parallel stem in <i>ṭhaha</i>, which stem Geiger attributes in general to analogy with <i>dahati</i> from root <i>dhā</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>below</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अधः</i> (adhaḥ), from Proto-Indo-European.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>standing below</li><li>(in a book) preceding</li></ol><p><i>adho</i> (“below”) +&lrm; <i>ṭhita</i> (“stood”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>now</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अधुना</i> (adhunā́). Cognate to Ashokan Prakrit <i>𑀅𑀥𑀼𑀦𑀸</i> (adhunā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dependent</li></ol><p>Compare Sanskrit <i>अधीन</i> (adhīna).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not given or offered</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>medicine</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>healthy</li></ol><p><i>a-</i> (“not”) +&lrm; <i>gada</i> (“disease”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wrong course</li><li>prejudice</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>highest</li><li>top-most</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अग्र</i> (ágra). Cognate with Ashokan Prakrit <i>𑀅𑀕</i> (aga /⁠agga⁠/).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seizure, taking</li></ol><p><i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>gah</i> (“seize”) +&lrm; <i>-ana</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>aggaḷa</i> (“securing device”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pre-eminence, prominence, superiority</li></ol><p><i>agga</i> +&lrm; <i>-tā</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a wooden bolt or pin for fastening a door</li><li>(<i>clothing</i>) gusset</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>value, worth</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अर्घ</i> (arghá), from Proto-Indo-Aryan <i>&#42;Hargʰás</i>, from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;Hargʰás</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂elgʷʰ-ós</i>, from <i>&#42;h₂elgʷʰ-</i> (“to cost, have value”). Cognate with Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀅𑀕𑁆𑀖</i> (aggha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fire</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>flame, conflagration</li><li>sparks</li></ol></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अग्नि</i> (agni), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;Hagnís</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁n̥gʷnís</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gun</li></ol><p><i>aggi</i> (“fire”) +&lrm; <i>nāḷī</i> (“tube”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fireplace</li></ol><p><i>aggi</i> +&lrm; <i>ṭhāna</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) voiceless</li></ol><p>From <i>a-</i> +&lrm; <i>ghosa</i> (“voiced”), calque of Sanskrit <i>अघोष</i> (aghoṣa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>home, house, dwelling</li></ol><p>Compare Sanskrit <i>अगार</i> (agāra​, “house, dwelling”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>householder</li></ol><p>From <i>agāra</i> +&lrm; <i>-in</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>I (1st person singular personal pronoun)</li><li>we (1st person plural personal pronoun)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अहम्</i> (aham).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>egotism, arrogance, ahamkara</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अहंकार</i> (ahaṃkāra).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snake, serpent, dragon</li></ol><p>Inherited from Vedic Sanskrit <i>अहि</i> (ahi). Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁ógʷʰis</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mushroom</li></ol><p><i>ahi</i> (“snake”) +&lrm; <i>chattaka</i> (“sunshade”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nonviolence</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अहिंसा</i> (ahiṃsā). By surface analysis, <i>a</i> +&lrm; <i>hiṃsā</i>. Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀅𑀳𑀺𑀁𑀲𑀸</i> (ahiṃsā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goat</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अज</i> (aja), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;Haȷ́ás</i>, from Proto-Indo-European.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>python</li><li>boa constrictor</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अजगर</i> (ajagara, literally “goat-swallower”).</p><p>The meaning “boa constrictor” seems to arise from its use as the English translations in Monier Williams’ Sanskrit-English dictionary and Childers' Pali-English dictionary; the translation now occurs in dictionaries of Asian origin (e.g. A.P. Buddhadatta’s) and affects modern usage.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cumin seed</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अजमोद</i> (ajamoda).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the hide of the black antelope, worn as a garment by ascetics</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अजिन</i> (ajína, “antelope skin”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>today, now</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अद्य</i> (adya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of today; recent, present, modern</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अद्यतन</i> (adyatana).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>straight, upright</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>आर्जव</i> (ārjava). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀅𑀚𑁆𑀚𑀯</i> (ajjava).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>relating to the individual</li><li>within the individual</li><li>internally</li><li>subjectively</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अध्यात्मम्</i> (adhyātmam). By surface analysis, <i>adhi</i> +&lrm; <i>attaṃ</i> (self).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>study (i.e. learning) of the Vedas</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अध्ययन</i> (adhyayana). By surface analysis, <i>adhi-</i> +&lrm; <i>ayana</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inhabit, to live in</li></ol><p><i>adhi-</i> +&lrm; <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>vasati</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>engaged in learning the Veda</li></ol><p><i>adhi-</i> +&lrm; <i>i</i> (“to go”) +&lrm; <i>-aka</i>, in the same manner as <i>ajjhayana</i> (“study of the Veda”) = <i>adhi-</i> +&lrm; <i>i</i> +&lrm; <i>-ana</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li><i>Pentaptera arjuna</i></li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>अर्जुन</i> (arjuna), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂erǵ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ajja</i> (“today”)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अद्या</i> (adyā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>she-goat</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अजा</i> (ajā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pleasant</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inaction, not doing</li></ol><p><i>a-</i> +&lrm; <i>karaṇa</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not dug</li></ol><p><i>a-</i> +&lrm; <i>khāta</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not doing one's duty</li><li>doing what ought not to be done</li><li>ineffective</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having nothing</li></ol><p><i>a-</i> (“not”) +&lrm; <i>kiñcana</i> (“something”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>impatient</li><li>intolerant</li></ol><p><i>a-</i> (“not”) +&lrm; <i>khama</i> (“patient”). The gemination results from the adjective's descent from a root which once had an initial consonant cluster.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>letter</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अक्षर</i> (akṣara).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Distinction of letters, etymological science</li></ol><p><i>akkhara</i> +&lrm; <i>pabheda</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>science of writing and reading</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bad luck, misfortune</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अक्षण</i> (akṣaṇa, “innoportune”). More at <i>a-</i>, <i>khaṇa</i>. Partial cognate of Prakrit <i>अक्खणिआ</i> (akkhaṇiā, “perverse copulation”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eye</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अक्षि</i> (akṣi), from Proto-Indo-Aryan <i>&#42;Hákṣi</i>, from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;Hákši</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃ókʷs</i> (“eye”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃ekʷ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>announced, proclaimed, told, said</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>आख्यात</i> (ākhyāta). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀆𑀖𑀸𑀬</i> (āghāya), <i>𑀅𑀓𑁆𑀔𑀸𑀬</i> (akkhāya), <i>𑀆𑀥𑀸𑀬</i> (ādhāya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scold, to abuse</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>आक्रोशति</i> (ākrośati). By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>kus</i> (root).</p><p>The consonants of the aorist in -cch- go back to Sanskrit <i>अक्रुक्षत्</i> (akrukṣat) for the simplex.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unskilful</li><li>bad</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>demerit, sin, bad action</li></ol><p><i>a-</i> (“not”) +&lrm; <i>kusala</i> (“skilful”). The noun is a specialised application of the adjective.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not delayed, immediate</li><li>of this world</li><li>timeless</li></ol><p><i>a-</i> +&lrm; <i>kālika</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the letter 'a'</li></ol><p>Possibly from Sanskrit <i>अकार</i> (akāra). By surface analysis, <i>अ</i> (a, the letter) +&lrm; <i>kāra</i> (term designating a letter or sound) etc.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ornament, decoration</li></ol><p>Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀅𑀮𑀁𑀓𑀸𑀭</i> (alaṃkāra).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doing up, fitting out, ornamentation</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>अलङ्करण</i> (alaṅkaraṇa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bosom friend, companion, advisor</li><li>minister, confidant</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अमात्य</i> (amātya). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀅𑀫𑀘𑁆𑀘</i> (amacca).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>immortal</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>everlasting</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nirvana, freedom from rebirth and death</li><li>ambrosia</li><li>water</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अमृत</i> (amṛta, “immortal”), in both its primary adjective sense and as nouns. By surface analysis, <i>a-</i> +&lrm; <i>mata</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the mango tree, <i>Mangifera indica</i></li><li>the mango fruit</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>आम्र</i> (āmra).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mango grove</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>hey, oi, you there</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अम्बु</i> (ambu, “water”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>family</i>) mummy</li></ol><p>Compare synonymous <i>ammā</i>. Either inherited or borrowed from Sanskrit <i>अम्बा</i> (ambā).</p><p>See under <i>amba</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>impure</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अमेध्य</i> (amedhya). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀅𑀫𑁂𑀚𑁆𑀛</i> (amĕjjha), <i>𑀅𑀫𑀺𑀚𑁆𑀛</i> (amijjha). By surface analysis, <i>a-</i> +&lrm; <i>mejjha</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>familiar term of address to a woman</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mother</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>a-</i> used before words beginning with vowels</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cart</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>अनस्</i> (ánas).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wrath</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unsurpassed</li><li>unrivalled</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Blessed One, i.e. the Buddha</li></ol><p><i>a-</i> +&lrm; <i>adhi-</i> +&lrm; <i>vara</i>. Note that <i>a-</i> (“not”) has the effect of making a superlative for <i>adhivara</i> (“excellent”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>homeless</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Homeless one; used originally for any member of the sangha</li><li>(<i>Buddhism</i>) A white-robed student in some modern forms of the Theravada tradition who, for a few months, awaits being considered for ordination as a samanera.</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>infinite, endless</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अनन्त</i> (ananta). By surface analysis, <i>an-</i> +&lrm; <i>anta</i> (“end”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to breathe</li><li>to have life</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अनति</i> (anati, “to breathe”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>displeased</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>complete, not lacking</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misfortune, distress</li></ol><p><i>a-</i> +&lrm; <i>naya</i> (“method”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>passionless, blameless</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blind</li><li>mentally blinded, foolish</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अन्ध</i> (andha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mentally blind, ignorant</li></ol><p><i>andha</i> +&lrm; <i>bhūta</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>darkness; blindness</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अन्धकार</i> (andhakāra). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀅𑀁𑀥𑀬𑀸𑀭</i> (aṃdhayāra), <i>𑀅𑀁𑀥𑀸𑀭</i> (aṃdhāra). By surface analysis, <i>andha</i> +&lrm; <i>-kāra</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>free from lust</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not one</li><li>many, various</li><li>countless</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>अनेक</i> (aneka), or otherwise from <i>a-</i> +&lrm; <i>eka</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not deaf and dumb</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>aṅga</i></li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>impermanent</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>impermanence</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अनित्य</i> (anitya). Morphologically, <i>a-</i> +&lrm; <i>nicca</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wind</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Sanskrit <i>अनिल</i> (anila).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having no power or influence</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unpleasant, disagreeable</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अनिष्ट</i> (aniṣṭa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>food, especially boiled rice</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अन्न</i> (anna, “food”).</p><p>See annā</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>family</i>) mummy</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>end</li><li>goal</li><li>top</li><li>intestine, tharm</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lowest, inferior</li><li>worst</li><li>last, final</li><li>extreme</li><li>interior</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अन्त</i> (anta).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>final, at the end</li></ol><p><i>anta</i> (“end”) +&lrm; <i>bhūta</i> (“being”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mesentery</li></ol><p><i>anta</i> (“intestine”) +&lrm; <i>guṇa</i> (“string”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>between</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chance conversation</li></ol><p><i>antarā</i> +&lrm; <i>kathā</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>danger; obstacle</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Sanskrit <i>अन्तराय</i> (antarāya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>student, pupil, disciple</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pupil who resides with his instructor</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>+ accusative</i>) into</li><li>(<i>+ locative</i>) inside</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अन्तः</i> (antaḥ).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>Verbal prefix:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>after, behind, according to</li><li>along, near to</li><li>less than, in consequence of, beneath</li></ol></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अनु-</i> (anu-), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;ánu</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>following</li><li>connected to</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Not pointed out; not uttered.</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>following after</li></ol><p>Verbal noun of <i>anugacchati</i>, composed as <i>anu-</i> +&lrm; <i>gam</i> +&lrm; <i>-ana</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>favour, help, benefit</li><li>compassion, kindness</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अनुग्रह</i> (anugraha). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀅𑀡𑀼𑀕𑁆𑀕𑀳</i> (aṇuggaha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give permission, to grant, to allow</li><li>to advise, to prescribe</li></ol><p><i>anu-</i> +&lrm; <i>jānāti</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have pity for; to have compassion for</li></ol><p><i>anu-</i> +&lrm; <i>kampati</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>consent, permission, agreement, assent, approval</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अनुमति</i> (anumati). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀅𑀡𑀼𑀫𑀇</i> (aṇumaï). By surface analysis, <i>anu-</i> +&lrm; <i>mati</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rejoice in</li><li>to appreciate</li></ol><p><i>anu-</i> +&lrm; <i>modati</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to follow</li></ol><p><i>anu-</i> +&lrm; <i>-pajjati</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall upon</li><li>to befall</li></ol><p><i>anu-</i> +&lrm; <i>patati</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>To give, to give over to</li></ol><p><i>anu-</i> +&lrm; <i>pavecchati</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>anupādāaparitassanā</i> (“non-agitation through non-clinging”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>anupādāaparitassanā</i> (“non-agitation through non-clinging”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>non-agitation through non-clinging</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>To reach, to attain</li></ol><p><i>anu-</i> +&lrm; <i>pāpuṇāti</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>recollection, memory, mindfulness</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>See <i>anussāveti</i> for forms and usage.</li></ol><p>By-form of <i>anussāveti</i>, formally <i>anu-</i> +&lrm; <i>sāvayati</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to announce</li><li>to proclaim</li></ol><p><i>anu-</i> +&lrm; <i>sāveti</i> (“to announce”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unbettered, without superior</li></ol><p><i>an-</i> +&lrm; <i>uttara</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>permitted, allowed</li><li>sanctioned, given leave, ordained</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अनुज्ञात</i> (anujñāta), synchronically analyzed as the past participle of <i>anujānāti</i> (“to grant, allow”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>anvāssavati</i> (“to stream into”)</li></ol><p>Either simply <i>anu-</i> +&lrm; <i>assavati</i> (“to flow towards”), or a later modification of anvāssavati (“to stream into”) to accord with the rule that vowels before consonant clusters are short.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stream into, attack, befall</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>anu-</i> +&lrm; <i>āsavati</i> (“to flow”) and <i>anu-</i> +&lrm; <i>assavati</i> (“to flow towards”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misconduct, misbehavior, immorality</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अनाचार</i> (anācāra). Analysable as <i>an-</i> +&lrm; <i>ācāra</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disregard</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not yet come</li><li>future</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>non-returner, anāgāmi, one who will not be reborn in this physical world.</li></ol><p><i>an-</i> (“not”) +&lrm; <i>āgāmin</i> (“returner”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>healthy, free from illness, not decaying</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अनामय</i> (anāmaya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>free from intoxicants</li><li>passionless</li></ol><p><i>a-</i> +&lrm; <i>āsava</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>helpless</li><li>destitute</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अनाथ</i> (anātha). By surface analysis, <i>a-</i> +&lrm; <i>nātha</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>male nickname</li></ol><p>From <i>anātha</i> (“destitute”) + <i>piṇḍa</i> (“lump of food”) + <i>-ika</i>, "one who gives alms to the poor".</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Pali root <i>ap</i></li></ol><p>Extracted from <i>appoti</i> and its compound <i>pāpuṇāti</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>offspring</li></ol><p>Compare Sanskrit <i>अपत्य</i> (apatya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gone, gone away from [<i>with</i> ablative]</li><li>removed; deceased, departed</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अपगत</i> (apagata). By surface analysis, <i>apa-</i> +&lrm; <i>gata</i>. Cognate with Sauraseni Prakrit <i>𑀅𑀯𑀕𑀤</i> (avagada), Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀅𑀯𑀕𑀅</i> (avagaa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>offence, guilt, crime, sin</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अपराध</i> (aparādha). Cognate with Ashokan Prakrit <i>𑀅𑀧𑀭𑀥</i> (aparadha, Girnar), <i>𑀅𑀧𑀮𑀸𑀥</i> (apalādha, Kalsi, Dhauli), <i>𐨀𐨤𐨪𐨢</i> (aparadha, Shahbazgarhi).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>an emphatic particle</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>also, even so</li><li>even</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अपि</i> (api).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>few, a small amount</li></ol><p>Derived from Sanskrit <i>अल्प</i> (alpa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>discontent, dissatisfaction</li><li>sulkiness</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>causeless</li></ol><p><i>a-</i> +&lrm; <i>paccaya</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>small, little, trifling</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a trifle</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carefulness; vigilance</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अप्रमाद</i> (apramāda), equivalent to <i>a-</i> +&lrm; <i>pamāda</i> (“carelessness”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thought, reasoning, resolve</li></ol><p>Either derived from inherited form <i>*appaṇa</i> from Sanskrit <i>अर्पण</i> (arpaṇa) by irregular decerebralisation and change of gender, or formed anew as <i>app</i> (root) +&lrm; <i>-anā</i> from Pali <i>appeti</i> (“to fix”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Only slightly defiled</li><li>Of only slightly blemished character</li></ol><p><i>appa</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;rajakkha</i>; <i>*rajakkha</i> is <i>rajas</i> (“dust, dirt”) +&lrm; <i>-ka</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not docile；rebellious</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>free from danger, safe</li></ol><p><i>a-</i> +&lrm; <i>paṭibhaya</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move forwards, to rush forwards</li><li>to affix</li><li>to hand over</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अर्पयति</i> (arpayati), causative of Sanskrit <i>ऋ</i> (ṛ, root).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unpleasant; disliked</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अप्रिय</i> (apriya). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀅𑀧𑁆𑀧𑀺𑀬</i> (appiya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fasting, going short on food</li></ol><p><i>appa</i> +&lrm; <i>āhāra</i> (“food”) +&lrm; <i>-tā</i> (“-ness”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>worthless</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the near shore</li></ol><p><i>a-</i> (“not”) +&lrm; <i>pāra</i> (“the other side”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>worthy</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Buddhism</i>) honorific for an ascetic</li><li>His Majesty</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>majestic, honorable</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अर्हत्</i> (arhat, “deserving, worthy, entitled to”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be worthy, to deserve</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अर्हति</i> (arhati).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lotus</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अरविन्द</i> (aravinda).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forest</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) any place except a village</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अरण्य</i> (araṇya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>wow, whoa</li><li>yay</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a noble man; one who has attained higher knowledge.</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>noble, distinguished</li><li>Aryan</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>आर्य</i> (ārya). Doublet of <i>ayya</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>healthy; free from disease</li></ol><p>Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀅𑀭𑁄𑀕</i> (aroga).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enemy</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unequal</li><li>incomparable</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stone, rock</li></ol><p><i>a-</i> +&lrm; <i>sama</i></p><p>Derived from <i>asman</i> by epenthesis and complete loss of consonantal declension.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not composed</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unmixed</li></ol><p><i>a-</i> +&lrm; <i>saṃsaṭṭha</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unrestrained</li></ol><p><i>a-</i> (“not”) +&lrm; <i>saṃvuta</i> (“restrained”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sword</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>असि</i> (asi).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sword and shield</li></ol><p><i>asi</i> +&lrm; <i>camma</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eaten</li><li>having eaten</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>food</li><li>sickle</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stone, rock</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अश्मन्</i> (aśman); see there for more.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>free from sorrow</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ashoka tree</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Indian male name (<i>commonly rendered Ashoka</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अशोक</i> (aśoka).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a horse</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अश्व</i> (aśva), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁éḱwos</i>.</p><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अस्य</i> (asya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>faithless</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disbelief</li></ol><p>Related to <i>saddhā</i> (“faith, belief”), incorporating the prefix <i>a-</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cavalry</li></ol><p><i>assa</i> +&lrm; <i>senā</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flow towards</li></ol><p><i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>savati</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shelter, resting place, refuge</li></ol><p>Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀆𑀲𑀬</i> (āsaya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(~māsa) the month of Aswayuja, corresponding to September–October</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tear (clear, salty fluid from the eye)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corpse</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unpleasant</li><li>ugly</li><li>impure</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>अशुभ</i> (aśubha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unclean</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dirt, excrement; dung; semen</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अशुचि</i> (aśuci) or simply <i>a-</i> +&lrm; <i>suci</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a class of malevolent beings, often described as demons, as opposed to the devas</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>असुर</i> (asura).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eighty</li></ol><p>Cognate with Sanskrit <i>अशीति</i> (aśīti), which see for wider connections. Ultimately from the same base as <i>aṭṭha</i> (“eight”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>being unhurried</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>and, and then, and also, moreover</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अथ</i> (atha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>and yet</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>very</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अति</i> (ati).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>over, beyond, exceedingly</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go beyond</li><li>to overcome, to surpass</li></ol><p><i>ati-</i> +&lrm; <i>kamati</i> (“to travel”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>went beyond; passed over; overcame; surpassed</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>too much, in excess</li><li>extremely intoxicated</li></ol><p><i>ati-</i> +&lrm; <i>mattā</i> (“measure”)</p><p><i>ati-</i> +&lrm; <i>matta</i> (“intoxicated”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pass time</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>killing</li><li>destruction</li></ol><p><i>ati-</i> +&lrm; <i>pāta</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>exceeding, in excess</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shine magnificently</li><li>to outshine</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guest</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अतिथि</i> (atithi, “guest”) or Sanskrit <i>अतिथिन्</i> (atithin, “travelling”); the initial 'at' is the root, not part of <i>ati-</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one's own nature</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>person, personality, individual</li><li>body, form</li></ol><li>life, rebirth</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self, ego</li><li>oneself, i.e. a reflexive pronoun</li><li>Also used as an emphatic pronoun</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>आत्मन्</i> (ātman).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) middle ending</li></ol><p>Calque of Sanskrit <i>आत्मनेपद</i> (ātmanepada), composed as <i>attano</i> (“for oneself”) +&lrm; <i>pada</i> (“word”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>welfare, gain, wealth</li><li>need, want, use</li><li>meaning</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अर्थ</i> (artha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spread out, stretch, lay out, cover</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>&#42;आस्तरति</i> (&#42;āstarati) (compare <i>आस्तरण</i> (āstaraṇa)).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>setting down</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be</li><li>to exist</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अस्ति</i> (asti), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁es-</i>. Compare Latin <i>est</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of the past</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>past (before now)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be left over, to remain</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to abide</li><li>to linger</li><li>to stand still</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strew, cover over or up</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>&#42;अवस्तरति</i> (&#42;avastarati) (compare <i>अवस्तृणोति</i> (avastṛṇoti)).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blame</li><li>dispraise</li></ol><p><i>a-</i> +&lrm; <i>vaṇṇa</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>peacable</li><li>friendly</li></ol><p>From <i>a-</i> +&lrm; <i>vera</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>near</li><li>not far</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ignorance (lack of knowledge)</li><li>(<i>Buddhism</i>) avidya, ignorance</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>a-</i> +&lrm; <i>vijjā</i>. Cognate with Sanskrit <i>अविद्या</i> (avidyā). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not released</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>free from thought</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>path</li><li>going to</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अयन</i> (áyana). By surface analysis, <i>i</i> (go) +&lrm; <i>-ana</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not to be conquered or subdued</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अयोध्य</i> (ayodhyá).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>noble</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Indo-Aryan <i>&#42;Áryas</i>, from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;Áryas</i> (“Aryan”); cognate with Sanskrit <i>आर्य</i> (ārya), whence <i>ariya</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lady</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the wai or namaste gesture, which is a mark of respect</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अञ्जलि</i> (añjali).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>worthy of reverential salutation</li></ol><p><i>añjali</i> (“wai”) +&lrm; <i>karanīya</i> (“to be done”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pull</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>ऋञ्जति</i> (ṛñjáti, “to make straight”). Related to <i>uju</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>other, another, different</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अन्य</i> (anya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>one another</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>a certain</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>otherwise</li><li>differently</li><li>in another way, in a different manner</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अन्यथा</i> (anyathā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>elsewhere</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अन्यत्र</i> (anyatra). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀅𑀡𑁆𑀡𑀢𑁆𑀢</i> (aṇṇatta), <i>𑀅𑀡𑁆𑀡𑀢𑁆𑀣</i> (aṇṇattha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>aññatra</i> (“elsewhere”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unknown, not known</li></ol><p><i>a-</i> +&lrm; <i>ñāta</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ignorance</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rich, opulent, wealthy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>आढ्य</i> (āḍhyá). Doublet of <i>āḷhiya</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>half-</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अर्ध</i> (ardhá, “half-”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a fortnight</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अर्धमास</i> (ardhamāsá). By surface analysis, karmadhāraya compound of <i>aḍḍha</i> (“half”) +&lrm; <i>māsa</i> (“month”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shoulder</li><li>a part, a side</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अंस</i> (aṃsa), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂óms-o-s</i> (“shoulder”).</p><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अंश</i> (aṃśa), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;Hánćas</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁ónḱos</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>member, body part</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>अङ्ग</i> (aṅga, “limb”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>finger</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thumb</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अङ्गुष्ठ</i> (aṅgúṣṭha), from Proto-Indo-Aryan <i>&#42;Hangúṣṭʰas</i>, from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;Hangúštʰas</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eng-</i> (“joint”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>charcoal</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अङ्गार</i> (áṅgāra). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀅𑀁𑀕𑀸𑀭</i> (aṃgāra), <i>𑀅𑀁𑀕𑀸𑀮</i> (aṃgāla).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>egg</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ocean</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अर्णव</i> (arṇava).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eight</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अष्ट</i> (aṣṭá), from Proto-Indo-Aryan <i>&#42;aṣṭā́</i>, from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;aštā́</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;oḱtṓw</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>forty-eight</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अष्टाचत्वारिंशत्</i> (aṣṭā́catvāriṃśat).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eighth</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अष्टम</i> (aṣṭama).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>sixty-eight</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अष्टाषष्टि</i> (aṣṭāṣaṣṭi). By surface analysis, <i>aṭṭha</i> +&lrm; <i>saṭṭhi</i>. Cognate with Prakrit <i>अट्ठासट्ठि</i> (aṭṭhāsaṭṭhi), <i>अट्ठसट्ठि</i> (aṭṭhasaṭṭhi), <i>अडसट्ठि</i> (aḍasaṭṭhi).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>octagonal</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>composed of eight parts, eightfold</li></ol><p><i>aṭṭha</i> +&lrm; <i>aṅga</i> +&lrm; <i>-ika</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) bone</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>&#42;अष्ठि</i> (&#42;aṣṭhi). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀅𑀝𑁆𑀞𑀺</i> (aṭṭhi) and obscurely related to Sanskrit <i>अस्थि</i> (asthi).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bone marrow</li></ol><p><i>aṭṭhi</i> +&lrm; <i>miñja</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cat</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>बभ्रु</i> (babhru, “mongoose”). See there for more.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boundary stones</li></ol><p><i>baddha</i> (“put together”) +&lrm; <i>sīmā</i> (“boundary”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>much, many</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>बहु</i> (bahu).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having much to do; busy</li></ol><p><i>bahu</i> +&lrm; <i>karaṇīya</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very learned</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>बहुश्रुत</i> (bahuśruta, literally “having heard much”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very useful</li></ol><p><i>bahu</i> (“much”) +&lrm; <i>upakāra</i> (“help”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crane</li><li>heron</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strength</li><li>power</li><li>force</li><li>army, military force</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>बल</i> (bala).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>religious offering</li><li>revenue</li><li>tax</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>strong</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tie, bond, fetter</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>halter</li></ol><li>a person who binds</li><li>a think that binds</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Sanskrit <i>बन्ध</i> (bandha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>binding, bond, fetter</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prison</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bind, tie</li></ol><p>Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀩𑀁𑀥𑀇</i> (baṃdhaï).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kinsman</li><li>relative</li><li>relation</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>बन्धु</i> (bandhu).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>luck, fortune</li><li>female organ</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>भग</i> (bhaga).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>'the fortunate one', epithet for Buddha</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>भगवत्</i> (bhagavat).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sister</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>भगिनी</i> (bhaginī).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eating, feeding on</li><li>edible</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>भक्ष</i> (bhakṣa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eat</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>भक्षति</i> (bhakṣati).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>frown</li><li>superciliousness</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>भृकुटि</i> (bhṛkuṭi), <i>भृकुटी</i> (bhṛkuṭī). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀪𑀺𑀉𑀟𑀺</i> (bhiuḍi), <i>𑀪𑀺𑀉𑀟𑀻</i> (bhiuḍī).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bee</li></ol><p>Probably inherited from Sanskrit <i>भ्रमर</i> (bhramara).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spin, whirl about</li><li>to roam</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>भ्रमति</i> (bhrámati). Cognate with Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀪𑀫𑀇</i> (bhamaï), Sauraseni Prakrit <i>𑀪𑀫𑀤𑀺</i> (bhamadi).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eyebrow</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Indo-Aryan <i>&#42;HbʰrúHs</i> (“eyebrow”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃bʰrúHs</i>. Compare Sanskrit <i>भ्रू</i> (bhrū́), Old Marathi <i>भवै</i> (bhavai).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vocative</i>) Sir, venerable sir</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Name of the Pali root <i>bhar</i></li></ol><p>Extracted from <i>bharati</i> etc.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bear, support, feed</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>भरति</i> (bhárati). Cognate with Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀪𑀭𑀇</i> (bharaï), Sauraseni Prakrit <i>𑀪𑀭𑀤𑀺</i> (bharadi). Related to <i>bharaṇa</i>, <i>bharita</i>. Possibly partly a doublet of <i>harati</i>, which is far commoner, and compare the reduction of <i>bhavati</i> to <i>hoti</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bearing, supporting, maintenance</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>भरण</i> (bharaṇa). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀪𑀭𑀡</i> (bharaṇa). By surface analysis, <i>bhar</i> (root) +&lrm; <i>-ana</i>. Related to <i>bharati</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wife</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>भार्या</i> (bhāryā). Cognate with Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀪𑀚𑁆𑀚𑀸</i> (bhajjā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boiled rice</li><li>food, meal</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>भक्त</i> (bhaktá), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;bʰaktás</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰh₂g-tó-s</i>, from the root <i>&#42;bʰeh₂g-</i> (“to divide, distribute”). Cognate to Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀪𑀢𑁆𑀢</i> (bhatta), Sauraseni Prakrit <i>𑀪𑀢𑁆𑀢</i> (bhatta).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>service, devotion</li><li>attachment, fondness</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>भक्ति</i> (bhakti). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀪𑀢𑁆𑀢𑀺</i> (bhatti).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>One whose duty it is to regulate the distribution of food to the monks</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>state of existence</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>भव</i> (bhava). Doublet of <i>bhāva</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sir, your honour</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>भवत्</i> (bhavat).</p><p>Surmised to be a contraction of <i>bhagavant</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become, exist</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>भवति</i> (bhávati), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰuH-</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fear, fright</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>भय</i> (bhaya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak</li><li>to tell</li><li>to preach</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>भनति</i> (bhanati). Cognate with Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀪𑀡𑀇</i> (bhaṇaï).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>good; wares; implements; articles</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>भाण्ड</i> (bhāṇḍa, “pot; vessel”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to quarrel</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>warehouse, storehouse, treasury</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>भाण्डागार</i> (bhāṇḍāgāra). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀪𑀁𑀟𑀸𑀕𑀸𑀭</i> (bhaṃḍāgāra), <i>𑀪𑀁𑀟𑀸𑀆𑀭</i> (bhaṃḍāāra), <i>𑀪𑀁𑀟𑀸𑀭</i> (bhaṃḍāra). By surface analysis, <i>bhaṇḍa</i> +&lrm; <i>agāra</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breaking, disunion, dissension</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>भेद</i> (bheda).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drum</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sound of a drum</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>medicine, remedy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>भैषज्य</i> (bhaiṣajya), from <i>भिषज्</i> (bhiṣaj), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;bʰiš-</i> (“medicine, healing”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a beggar (one who begs)</li><li>a Buddhist monk</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>भिक्षु</i> (bhikṣu). Related to <i>bhikkhati</i>, <i>bhikkhā</i>. Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀪𑀺𑀓𑁆𑀔𑀼</i> (bhikkhu).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Buddhist nun</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>भिक्षुणी</i> (bhikṣuṇī).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alms, food obtained by begging, begging, mendicancy</li></ol><p>Related to <i>bhikkhati</i>, <i>bhikkhu</i>. Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀪𑀺𑀓𑁆𑀔𑀸</i> (bhikkhā), <i>𑀪𑀺𑀘𑁆𑀙𑀸</i> (bhicchā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break</li><li>to cut, to sever</li><li>to destroy, to ruin</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>भिनत्ति</i> (bhinatti, “to break”) with generalisation of the weak stem. The passive is a continuation of the Sanskrit passive, Sanskrit <i>भिद्यते</i> (bhidyate) with the usual change of voice from middle to mostly active.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lotus plant root</li></ol><p>Possibly related to Bhojpuri <i>भसींड़</i> (bhasīṇṛ)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>physician, medical doctor</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>भिषक्</i> (bhiṣak), nominative singular of <i>भिषज्</i> (bhiṣaj), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;bʰišáȷ́</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cushion, pad</li><li>bolster</li></ol><p>Compare Sanskrit <i>बृसि</i> (bṛsi).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wall</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>भित्ति</i> (bhitti). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀪𑀺𑀢𑁆𑀢𑀺</i> (bhitti).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having one or more walls</li></ol><p>From <i>bhitti</i> +&lrm; <i>-ka</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vocative</i>) friend</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>possession</li><li>wealth</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>भोग</i> (bhoga).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>meal, food</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>भोजन</i> (bhojana). By surface analysis, <i>bhuj</i> (root) +&lrm; <i>-ana</i>. Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀪𑁄𑀅𑀡</i> (bhoaṇa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bending</li><li>crooked</li><li>clean, pure, bright</li><li>beautiful</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arm</li></ol><p>Formed from the root bhuj (“to bend”), with the noun form inherited from Sanskrit <i>भुज</i> (bhuja). In Pali, the noun is a specific instance of the adjective, while Sanskrit appears to lack the adjective.</p><p>Formed from the root bhuj (“to cleanse”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snake</li></ol><p>Kenning <i>bhuja</i> (“crooked”) +&lrm; <i>-ga</i> (“going”) 'crooked goer'.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>freeman, a freed slave</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>terrestrial</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ground</li><li>floor</li><li>soil</li><li>earth god</li></ol><p>Probably <i>bhūmi</i> (“ground”) +&lrm; <i>-ya</i>; see Sanskrit <i>भूम्य</i> (bhūmya) for discussion of formation.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eat</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>भुञ्जते</i> (bhuñjate).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a part, share, portion</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>भाग</i> (bhāgá).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>speak, say</li><li>to shine, glow, fill with light</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>भासति</i> (bhā́sati).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>language</li><li>dialect</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>भाषा</i> (bhāṣā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brother</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>भ्रातृ</i> (bhrātṛ), ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰréh₂tēr</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>state, condition</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>भाव</i> (bhāva). Doublet of <i>bhava</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>meditation</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cultivate, to develop (an idea etc.)</li></ol><p>Causative of <i>bhavati</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be afraid</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dreadful, horrible, cruel, awful</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>भीम</i> (bhīma).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>frightened, afraid, terrified</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>भीत</i> (bhītá). Cognate with Gandhari <i>𐨧𐨁𐨡</i> (bhida) and Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀪𑀻𑀅</i> (bhīa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the earth</li><li>eyebrow</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>भू</i> (bhū, “the earth”).</p><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>भ्रू</i> (bhrū, “eyebrow”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ground, earth</li><li>Earth</li><li>region</li><li>stage</li><li>plane</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>भूमि</i> (bhūmi), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;bʰúHmiš</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ornament, decoration</li></ol><p>Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀪𑀽𑀲𑀡</i> (bhūsaṇa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>natural</li><li>genuine</li><li>true</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beings</li><li>living beings</li><li>animate nature</li><li>city spirit guardians</li><li>inanimate nature</li><li>supernatural being, ghost</li><li>nature, creation</li><li>truth</li><li>(as second element of a bahuvrihi, with respect to the first element)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>what has been or happened</li><li>having become, acting as, being</li></ol></ol><p>This past participle of <i>bhavati</i> (“to be”) can be traced back to Sanskrit <i>भूत</i> (bhūta) and beyond. Note that the past participle naturally has an active sense.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>formerly</li></ol><p><i>bhūta</i> +&lrm; <i>pubbaṃ</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>den</li><li>hole</li><li>portion</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marmelos tree</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>image</li><li>figure</li><li>disk (of the sun or moon)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drop</li><li>dot</li><li>trifle</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cat</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>supreme knowledge</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Buddhism</i>) the Bodhi Tree, i.e. the specific fig tree under which the Buddha sat when he attained enlightenment; the descendant of that tree on the same spot at Bodh Gaya.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>बोधि</i> (bodhi), from Proto-Indo-Aryan <i>&#42;bawdʰíṣ</i>, from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;bawdʰíš</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰewdʰ-</i> (“to be awake”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Buddhism</i>) bodhisattva (one destined to reach enlightenment)</li></ol><p>From <i>bodhi</i> (“enlightenment”) +&lrm; <i>satta</i> (“being”). Compare Sanskrit <i>बोधिसत्त्व</i> (bodhisattva).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>body</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a royal gift</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a brahma world</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Brahma</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>ब्रह्मन्</i> (brahman).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brahmin (a man of the Brahman caste)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Sanskrit <i>ब्राह्मण</i> (brāhmaṇa), replacing inherited <i>&#42;bamhaṇa</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>say</li><li>speak</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>desiderative of <i>bhuñjati</i>: to want food, be hungry</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>बुभुक्षति</i> (bubhukṣati). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀩𑀼𑀳𑀼𑀓𑁆𑀔𑀇</i> (buhukkhaï).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hungry, desirous of food</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>बुभुक्षित</i> (bubhukṣita). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀩𑀼𑀳𑀼𑀓𑁆𑀔𑀺𑀅</i> (buhukkhia), <i>𑀪𑀼𑀓𑁆𑀔𑀺𑀅</i> (bhukkhia).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Buddhism</i>) Buddha, the enlightened one</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Buddhism</i>) buddha</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>बुद्ध</i> (buddha, “enlightened, awakened”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Pali (Capitalized: Buddha)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Buddhism</i>) Buddha (the enlightened one)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>बुद्ध</i> (buddha, “enlightened, awakened”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>image of the Buddha</li></ol><p><i>buddha</i> +&lrm; <i>rūpa</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the act of consecrating an image of the Buddha</li></ol><p><i>buddha</i> +&lrm; <i>abhiseka</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to awaken</li><li>to be awake</li><li>to know</li><li>to understand</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>बुध्यते</i> (budhyate), from the root <i>बुध्</i> (budh) from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰewdʰ-</i>. Cognate with Russian <i>буди́ть</i> (budítʹ, “to awaken”), Gothic <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰𐌱𐌹𐌿𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (anabiudan), English <i>bid</i>, German <i>bieten</i> (“to bid, to offer”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tiger</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>व्याघ्र</i> (vyāghrá). Doublet of <i>vyaggha</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sign</li><li>mark</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) letter</li><li>letter (as opposed to spirit)</li></ol><p>Alternative form of <i>vyañjana</i>, which see for further information.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>vyūha</i></li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hindrance</li><li>prevention</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>बाधा</i> (bādhā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arm</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>combining form of <i>bahu</i> (“many”)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>बाहु</i> (bāhu), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰeh₂ǵʰús</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>combining form of <i>bahu</i> (“many”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arm</li></ol><p>Doublet of <i>bāhu</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>child</li><li>fool</li><li>alternative form of <i>vāla</i> (“hair”)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>young</li><li>ignorant</li><li>foolish</li><li>newly-risen (<i>of the sun</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>बाल</i> (bāla).</p><p>Byform of <i>vāla</i> (“hair”). The same occasional development is also seen in the byform Sanskrit <i>बाल</i> (bāla) of Sanskrit <i>वाल</i> (vāla, “hair”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boy, kid</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>बालक</i> (bālaka). Cognate with Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀩𑀸𑀮𑀅</i> (bālaa), Paisaci Prakrit <i>𑀧𑀸𑀴𑀓</i> (pāḷaka).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>girl</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fisherman</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arrow</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>बाण</i> (bāṇa). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀩𑀸𑀡</i> (bāṇa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seed, germ</li><li>generating element</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>बीज</i> (bī́ja).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>and (copulative particle)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>च</i> (ca).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to abandon, give up</li><li>to let loose</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>त्यजति</i> (tyajati, “abandons”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tyégʷeti</i>. Cognate with Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀘𑀬𑀇</i> (cayaï). Related to <i>cāga</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wheel</li><li>circle</li><li>disc</li><li>cycle</li><li>command</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>चक्र</i> (cakra). Cognate with Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀘𑀓𑁆𑀓</i> (cakka).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>cakkavāḷa</i> (“circle, sphere”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>circle</li><li>sphere</li><li>(<i>buddhist</i>) world, solar-system, universe.</li></ol><p>From or cognate with Sanskrit <i>चक्रवाड</i> (cakravāḍa), formed from <i>चक्र</i> (cakra, “circle, wheel”) + <i>वल</i> (vala, “ring”), found in <i>वलय</i> (valaya, “ring, armlet”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eye</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>चक्षु</i> (cakṣu).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sense of sight</li><li>eye</li></ol><p><i>cakkhu</i> (“eye”) +&lrm; <i>indriya</i> (“sense”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>good for the eyes</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>organ of sight</li><li>sense of sight</li></ol><p><i>cakkhu</i> +&lrm; <i>āyatana</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>partridge</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>चकोर</i> (cakora).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the letter 'c'</li></ol><p>Possibly from Sanskrit <i>चकार</i> (cakāra). By surface analysis, translingual <i>ච</i> (the letter) +&lrm; <i>kāra</i> (term designating a letter or sound) etc.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>चलति</i> (cálati, “to walk, move”), <i>चरति</i> (cárati). Related to <i>carati</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yak, ox</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hide, skin, leather</li><li>shield</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>चर्मन्</i> (carman). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀘𑀫𑁆𑀫</i> (camma).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a leatherworker, a tanner</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>चर्मकार</i> (carmakāra). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀘𑀫𑁆𑀫𑀆𑀭</i> (cammaāra).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moon</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>चन्द्र</i> (candra).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moonlight</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>चन्द्रिका</i> (candrikā). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀘𑀁𑀤𑀺𑀆</i> (caṃdiā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the moon</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>चन्द्रमाः</i> (candramāḥ), nominative singular of <i>चन्द्रमस्</i> (candramas).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>act of walking about, act of frequenting</li><li>one who walks about, one who frequents</li><li>messenger, spy</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to walk; move about</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>चरति</i> (cárati), from Proto-Indo-Aryan <i>&#42;čálHati</i>, from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;čálHati</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷel-</i> (“to turn”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>walking about</li><li>foot (part of body)</li><li>conduct, behaviour (especially good behaviour)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>चरण</i> (caraṇa). By surface analysis, <i>car</i> +&lrm; <i>-ana</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wandering</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>and [there] were (<i>optative mood</i>)</li></ol><p>Contraction of <i>ca</i> +&lrm; <i>assa</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>forty</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>catur</i> (“four”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>quadruple, fourfold</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>चतुर्गुण</i> (caturguṇa). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀘𑀉𑀕𑁆𑀕𑀼𑀡</i> (caügguṇa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tetrad, foursome (set of four)</li><li>square; junction of four roads</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>चतुष्क</i> (catuṣka). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀘𑀤𑀼𑀓𑁆𑀓</i> (cadukka), <i>𑀘𑀉𑀓𑁆𑀓</i> (caükka).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>secret (<i>of counsel</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>चतुष्कर्ण</i> (catuṣkarṇa, “heard only by four ears”). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀘𑀉𑀓𑁆𑀓𑀡𑁆𑀡</i> (caükkaṇṇa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>four</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>चतुर्</i> (catúr).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having four edges</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a type of chess</li><li>a set of four members</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eighty four</li></ol><p><i>catur</i> (four) +&lrm; <i>asīti</i> (eighty)</p><p>Synchronically, the lengthening of the third vowel of the word is anomalous, but is supported by the presence of a long vowel in similar numbers, such as <i>pañcāsīti</i> (“85”) (= <i>pañca</i> + <i>asīti</i>).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>fourth</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>चतुर्थ</i> (caturtha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>falling away; shifting</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to walk about</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fierce，violent; quick-tempered，uncontrolled，passionate</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>चण्ड</i> (caṇḍa), probably a corruption of <i>चन्द्र</i> (candra).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cloth</li><li>garment</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intention</li><li>volition</li></ol><p>Sanskrit <i>चेतना</i> (cetanā)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>memorial</li><li>pagoda</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>चैत्य</i> (caitya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fallowness</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nominative singular of <i>cetokhila</i> (“fallowness”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>understanding the ways of others' thought</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>calmness of mind</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>six</li></ol><p>Possibly from Sanskrit <i>&#42;ट्षष्</i> (&#42;ṭṣaṣ), variant of <i>षष्</i> (ṣaṣ); see there for further etymology.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in&#32;compounds</i>) veil</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roof</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roof; covering</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dung</li></ol><p>From late Old Indo-Aryan <i>&#42;śakana- ~ &#42;chakana-</i>, a colloquial formation from Sanskrit <i>शकन्</i> (śakán), the oblique base of <i>शकृत्</i> (śákṛt, “dung”); see there for further etymology. Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀙𑀕𑀡</i> (chagaṇa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a group of six; sextet; hexad</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>षट्क</i> (ṣáṭka). Cognate to Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀙𑀓𑁆𑀓</i> (chakka).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fraud, pretext</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>impulse, will, wish</li></ol><p>Cognate with Sanskrit <i>छन्द</i> (chanda); see the root <i>छन्द्</i> (chand) for more.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sunshade, parasol</li><li>sovereignty</li><li>canopy</li><li>pupil, student</li><li>corpse, body</li></ol><p>From the root <i>chad</i> (“to cover over”) +&lrm; <i>-tta</i> (“instrument suffix”), if not simply Sanskrit <i>छत्त्र</i> (chattra), which is not necessarily an old formation.</p><p>From the 'sunshade' meaning, though the semantic route is debated. Possibly inherited from Sanskrit <i>छात्त्र</i> (chāttra), and certainly related.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sunshade</li><li>sunshade-maker</li><li>mushroom</li><li>kingfisher</li></ol><p><i>chatta</i> (“sunshade”) +&lrm; <i>-ka</i>. It is possible that the meaning 'kingfisher' does not belong here.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corpse</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bad; evil</li></ol><p>Compare Sanskrit <i>शव</i> (śava).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skin</li><li>tegument</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>छवि</i> (chavi).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>sixth</li></ol><p>Cognate with Sanskrit <i>षष्ठ</i> (ṣaṣṭha), but from a Proto-Indic doublet thereof.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>severance</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>clever</li><li>skillful</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cutter, destroyer</li></ol><p>Ultimately <i>chid</i> (“to cut”) +&lrm; <i>-tar</i>, but this composition predates Pali, being seen in Sanskrit <i>छेत्तृ</i> (chettṛ).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut off</li><li>to sever</li><li>to destroy</li></ol><p>Cognate with Sanskrit <i>छिनत्ति</i> (chinátti).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cut off, destroyed</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>छिन्न</i> (chinna). Cognate to Prakrit <i>छिण्ण</i> (chiṇṇa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dagger</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>क्षुरिका</i> (kṣurikā).. Doublet of <i>khura</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shade; shadow</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>छाया</i> (chāyā). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀙𑀸𑀬𑀸</i> (chāyā), <i>𑀙𑀸𑀆</i> (chāā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mark</li><li>sign</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parrot</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to think</li><li>to reflect</li><li>to consider, to ponder</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having long since become a monk</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figurative</i>) mind, heart, feeling</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>चित्त</i> (citta).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Pali (Capitalized: Citta)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the month of Chaitra, corresponding to March–April</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an orator, a brilliant speaker</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thought; honour</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>चित्ति</i> (cítti).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tamarind tree</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>चिञ्चा</i> (ciñcā). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀘𑀺𑀁𑀘𑀸</i> (ciṃcā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cloth</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thief, robber, bandit</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>चौर</i> (caura), चोर (cora).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>passing away</li></ol><p>Compare Sanskrit <i>च्युति</i> (cyuti).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>powder</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>चूर्ण</i> (cūrṇa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abandonment, forsaking</li><li>generosity</li><li>gift</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>त्याग</i> (tyāga), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tyegʷ-</i>. Related to the verb <i>cajati</i>. Cognate with Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀘𑀸𑀬</i> (cāya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fickleness</li></ol><p>Compare Sanskrit <i>चपलिता</i> (capalitā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>motion</li><li>action, behaviour</li></ol><p>Formed on the root <i>car</i> of <i>carati</i> (“to move about”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>practice</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>and [there] were (<i>optative mood</i>)</li></ol><p>Contraction of <i>ca</i> +&lrm; <i>assa</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>belonging to the four directions</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Four Heavenly Kings</li></ol><p><i>cātu-</i> +&lrm; <i>mahārājan</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of the Four Heavenly Kings</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(member of) the retinue of the Four Heavenly Kings</li></ol><p>Variant of <i>cātumahārājika</i>, which is <i>cātumahārājā</i> (“the four Heavenly Kings”) +&lrm; <i>-ika</i>; gemination following cātu- is variable.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yellow robe</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a single lock or tuft left on the crown of the head after tonsure</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>चूडा</i> (cūḍā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sour milk, curd</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>दधि</i> (dadhi). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀤𑀳𑀺</i> (dahi), <i>𑀤𑀥𑀺</i> (dadhi).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give</li><li>to offer</li><li>to allow</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>ददाति</i> (dadāti).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lake</li></ol><p>Possibly from Sanskrit <i>द्रह</i> (draha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>skilled; able; clever</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ability; skill</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>दक्ष</i> (dákṣa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>right, righthand</li><li>southern</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the south; southern direction</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>दक्षिण</i> (dakṣiṇa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>worthy of an offering</li></ol><p><i>dakkhiṇā</i> +&lrm; <i>-eyya</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>donation</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>दक्षिणा</i> (dakṣiṇā, “donation”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the letter 'd'</li></ol><p>Possibly from Sanskrit <i>दकार</i> (dakāra). By surface analysis, translingual <i>ද</i> (the letter) +&lrm; <i>kāra</i> (term designating a letter or sound) etc.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dameti</i> (“to tame”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tame, to punish, to master, to convert</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Tamil; Dravidian</li></ol><p>From Old Tamil <i>𑀢𑀫𑀺𑀵𑁰</i> (tamiḻ).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>in need of taming, in need of restraint</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tooth, fang</li><li>tusk (<i>especially of an elephant</i>)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of ivory</li><li>restrained</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>दन्त</i> (dánta). Cognate with Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀤𑀁𑀢</i> (daṃta).</p><p>From Sanskrit <i>दान्त</i> (dānta).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ivorysmith (an artisan in ivory)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>दन्तकार</i> (dantakāra). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀤𑀁𑀢𑀸𑀭</i> (daṃtāra).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>ten</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>दश</i> (daśa), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;déḱm̥</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>tenth</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>दशम</i> (daśama).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seeing, looking, sight; intuition; insight</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>दर्शन</i> (darśana). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀤𑀲𑁆𑀲𑀡</i> (dassaṇa), <i>𑀤𑀁𑀲𑀡</i> (daṃsaṇa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fair, beautiful</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poverty</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stick, handle, staff, rod</li><li>stem of tree, wood; wood worked into something</li><li>punishment, thrashing</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>दण्ड</i> (daṇḍa). Cognate with Niya Prakrit <i>𐨡𐨎𐨜</i> (daṃḍa), Prakrit <i>𑀤𑀁𑀟</i> (daṃḍa), <i>𑀟𑀁𑀟</i> (ḍaṃḍa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>point</li><li>place, region</li><li>country</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>देश</i> (deśa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to point out</li><li>to teach</li><li>to preach</li><li>to expound</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>god, deity, angel</li><li>the sky, heaven</li><li>a rain cloud</li><li>a king</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>देव</i> (devá, “deity, god”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Devadatta, cousin and former disciple of the Buddha.</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the divine realm, devaloka, heaven</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>देवलोक</i> (devaloká). By surface analysis, tatpuruṣa compound of <i>deva</i> +&lrm; <i>loka</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>son of a god</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>husband's brother</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>देवर</i> (devara). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀤𑁂𑀯𑀭</i> (devara), <i>𑀤𑁂𑀅𑀭</i> (deara), <i>𑀤𑀺𑀅𑀭</i> (diara).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deity</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goddess, female deity</li><li>queen</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>देवी</i> (devī́, “goddess; queen”). By surface analysis, <i>deva</i> (“god”) +&lrm; <i>-ī</i> (feminine-forming suffix). Cognate to Gandhari <i>𐨡𐨅𐨬𐨁</i> (devi), Prakrit <i>𑀤𑁂𑀯𑀻</i> (devī).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>to be given</li><li>deserving a gift, worthy of receiving alms</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a gift, offering</li></ol><p>Derived from Sanskrit <i>देय</i> (deya), the gerundive from the root&nbsp;<i>दा</i> (dā). See <i>dadāti</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the letter 'dh'</li></ol><p>Possibly from Sanskrit <i>धकार</i> (dhakāra). By surface analysis, translingual <i>ධ</i> (the letter) +&lrm; <i>kāra</i> (term designating a letter or sound) etc.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>truth, righteousness</li><li>doctrine, teachings</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>Buddhism</i>) teachings of the Buddha</li></ol><li>morality, goodness</li><li>causal antecedent</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>धर्म</i> (dhárma), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;dʰármas</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰérmos</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the eye of wisdom</li></ol><p>dhamma + cakkhu</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>section of the dharma</li></ol><p><i>dhamma</i> +&lrm; <i>khandha</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>general rule; nature</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>धर्मता</i> (dharmatā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>investigation of doctrine.</li></ol><p><i>dhamma</i> +&lrm; <i>vicaya</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wealth, money, riches, treasure</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>धन</i> (dhána). Cognate with Gandhari <i>𐨢𐨣</i> (dhana), Prakrit <i>𑀥𑀡</i> (dhaṇa).</p></html>:/tʰɛ̤ʔnṵ/
<h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bow</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>धनुः</i> (dhanuḥ).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>white</li><li>clean</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>धवल</i> (dhavala).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>realm</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an ascetic practice</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stable, permanent, durable</li><li>absolute</li><li>unfailing, imperishable</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>ध्रुव</i> (dhruva), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;dʰruwás</i>. Cognate with Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀥𑀼𑀯</i> (dhuva).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wearing</li><li>sustaining</li><li>bearing in mind</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>धारण</i> (dhāraṇa). By surface analysis, <i>dhar</i> +&lrm; <i>-ana</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A torrent, stream, flow, shower</li><li>the edge of a weapon</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>धारा</i> (dhārā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a fountain</li></ol><p><i>dhārā</i> (“bearing, holding”) +&lrm; <i>yanta</i> (“machine”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>element</li><li>natural condition</li><li>(<i>religion</i>) relic</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) root of a word</li><li>humour of the body</li><li>a faculty of the senses</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>धातु</i> (dhātu). The grammatical sense is a semantic loan from Sanskrit <i>धातु</i> (dhātu).</p><p>Borrowed from Sanskrit <i>धातु</i> (dhātu).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wet nurse</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>धावति</i> (dhāvati).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wise</li><li>firm, resolute, brave</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>धीर</i> (dhīra, “firm”) and Sanskrit <i>धीर</i> (dhīra, “wise”). In Pali, the two words merged into a single polysemous word, at least in the understanding of the commentaries.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>dhītā</i> (“daughter”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>daughter</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>धयति</i> (dhayati, “to suck”), cf. Latin <i>fīlia</i> (“daughter”) from the same root. More immediately, compare Magadhi Prakrit and Sauraseni Prakrit <i>𑀥𑀻𑀤𑀸</i> (dhīdā).</p><p>It might also be a contraction of Sanskrit <i>दुहितृ</i> (duhitṛ, “daughter”), possibly before the loss of the laryngeals; it has certainly blended with this word or its Pali reflex <i>duhitar</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smoke, fumes</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>धूम</i> (dhūma).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>incense</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>divine, heavenly</li><li>celestial</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>दिव्य</i> (divyá), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;diwyós</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Buddhism</i>) the divine eye</li></ol><p>From <i>dibba</i> +&lrm; <i>cakkhu</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>divine ear, supernatural hearing. One of the six abhiññas, the faculty of hearing every sound in all the universe.</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to amuse oneself, to sport</li><li>to play (<i>especially dice</i>)</li></ol><p>Sanskrit <i>दीव्यति</i> (dīvyati).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>double</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>द्विगुण</i> (dviguṇa). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀤𑀺𑀉𑀡</i> (diuṇa), Niya Prakrit <i>𐨡𐨁𐨒𐨂𐨣</i> (diguna).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>day</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a musical instrument，a small drum</li></ol><p>Compare Sanskrit <i>डिण्डिम</i> (ḍiṇḍima).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enemy</li><li>The root <i>dis</i> (as in Pali <i>deseti</i></li><li>The root <i>dis</i> (as in Pali <i>dissati</i> and parts of <i>passati</i>)</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>द्विष</i> (dviṣa)</p><p>Extracted from the verb <i>deseti</i></p><p>Extracted from the verb <i>dissati</i> (“to seem”) and forms such as <i>diṭṭha</i> of <i>passati</i> (“to see”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to point out, to show</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>दिशति</i> (diśati).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quarter</li><li>point of the compass</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>दिशा</i> (diśā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>day</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>दिवस</i> (divasa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>seen</li><li>found</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>this world</li></ol><p><i>diṭṭha</i> +&lrm; <i>dhamma</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>view</li><li>belief</li><li>dogma</li><li>theory</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>दृष्टि</i> (dṛṣṭi).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dejectedness</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>depravity</li><li>fault, defect, flaw, sin</li><li>anger</li><li>hatred</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>दोष</i> (doṣa). The Pali root is <i>dus</i>.</p><p>From Sanskrit <i>द्वेष</i> (dveṣa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a clear night</li><li>moonlight</li></ol><p>From a derivative of Sanskrit <i>दोषा</i> (doṣā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>obstinate</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>famine</li></ol><p>Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀤𑀼𑀩𑁆𑀪𑀺𑀓𑁆𑀔</i> (dubbhikkha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>milk</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>दुग्ध</i> (dugdhá). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀤𑀼𑀤𑁆𑀥</i> (duddha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a difficult road</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>दुर्ग</i> (durgá, “an impassable route”), from Proto-Indo-Aryan <i>&#42;duẓgás</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Buddhism</i>) suffering, discontent, dissatisfaction</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>दुःख</i> (duḥkha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Buddhism</i>) destruction of suffering</li></ol><p><i>dukkha</i> +&lrm; <i>nirodha</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Buddhism</i>) the origin of suffering</li></ol><p><i>dukkha</i> +&lrm; <i>samudaya</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>afflicted, grieved, unhappy, dejected, ailing</li></ol><p>Sanskrit <i>दुःखित</i> (duḥkhita)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tree</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>द्रुम</i> (druma, “tree”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wrong drawing out</li><li>misunderstanding</li></ol><p><i><i>dus-</i> &#43;‎ <i>naya</i></i> +&lrm; <i>-tā</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>badly or wrongly placed.</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Pali root <i>dus</i></li></ol><p>Extracted from the prefix <i>dus-</i> and verbs such as <i>dussati</i> and <i>dūseti</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>textile, fabric, cloth</li><li>garment, clothes</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>दूर्श</i> (dūrśá).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>second</li><li>companion, friend, partner</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>द्वितीय</i> (dvitīya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>for the second time</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>spoilt</li><li>bad</li><li>corrupt</li><li>malignant</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>दुष्ट</i> (duṣṭa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>thirty two</li></ol><p><i>dve</i> +&lrm; <i>tiṃsa</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>twofoldness</li></ol><p><i>dve</i> +&lrm; <i>bhāva</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>two months old</li></ol><p><i>dve</i> +&lrm; <i>māsika</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>two</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>द्वि</i> (dvi).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>twelve</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>द्वादश</i> (dvādaśa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>twelfth</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>द्वादशम</i> (dvādaśama).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>door, entrance, gate</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>द्वार</i> (dvā́ra). Cognate with Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀤𑀸𑀭</i> (dāra).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gift</li><li>charity</li><li>alms</li><li>giving</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>दान</i> (dā́na) or re-formed from <i>dā</i> +&lrm; <i>-ana</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>young woman</li><li>wife, married woman</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>दार</i> (dāra).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>maintenance of a wife</li></ol><p><i>dāra</i> +&lrm; <i>bharaṇa</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>child</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wood (natural hard substance from tree)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>दारु</i> (dāru). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀤𑀸𑀭𑀼</i> (dāru).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>strong, firm, severe</li><li>harsh, cruel</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>दारुण</i> (dāruṇa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wife, married woman</li></ol><p>Conversion of dāra to feminine form to accord with its meaning</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(male) slave</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>दास</i> (dāsa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slavery</li></ol><p><i>dāsa</i> +&lrm; <i>-bya</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slavery</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>female slave</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>दासी</i> (dāsī).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>giver</li><li>generous person</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>दातृ</i> (dātṛ, “giver”), agent noun of <i>ददाति</i> (<i>dadāti</i>, “to give”), an analysis synchronically applicable to Pali.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sickle, scythe</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>दात्र</i> (dā́tra), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;dáHtram</i> (“sickle”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pomegranate tree</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>दाडिम</i> (dāḍima). Doublet of <i>dāḷima</i>, the inherited form, and <i>dālima</i>, a by-form of the inherited form.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pomegranate tree</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>दाडिम</i> (dāḍima). Doublet of <i>dāḍima</i> and <i>dālima</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>long</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>दीर्घ</i> (dīrgha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>island</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>द्वीप</i> (dvīpa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leopard</li><li>tiger</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>द्वीपिन्</i> (dvīpin). Although formally and etymologically synchronically analysable as <i>dīpa</i> (“island”) +&lrm; <i>-in</i>, this may not have affected the sense.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inflection of <i>dīpin</i> (“leopard”):## nominative singular## nominative/vocative/accusative plural</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a messenger, envoy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>दूत</i> (dūta).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inviting everyone to come and see it, open to all.</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>one</li><li>alone</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>एक</i> (eka).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>forty one</li></ol><p><i>eka</i> (“one”) +&lrm; <i>cattālīsā</i> (“forty”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>on one side, apart, aside</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>twenty one</li></ol><p><i>eka</i> +&lrm; <i>vīsati</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in all probability</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eleven</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>एकादश</i> (ekādaśa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eleventh</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>एकादशम</i> (ekādaśama).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thief</li><li>robber</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the letter 'e'</li></ol><p>Possibly from Sanskrit <i>एकार</i> (ekāra). By surface analysis, translingual <i>එ</i> (the letter) +&lrm; <i>kāra</i> (term designating a letter or sound) etc.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cardamom seeds</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>this</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>this</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative stem of eta</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative stem of eta</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>this</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>this one</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>एतद्</i> (etad).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>now</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>एति</i> (eti).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>here</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>emphatic</i>) so, even, just</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>एव</i> (eva). Doublet of <i>evaṃ</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li><i>Form of</i> evaṃ <i>before vowels</i></li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>such</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>thus, so</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>एवम्</i> (evam).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pumpkin</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>womb, the swelling of the womb (<i>during pregnancy</i>)</li><li>embryo, fetus</li><li>interior, cavity</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>गर्भ</i> (gárbha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pregnant</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>गर्भिणी</i> (garbhíṇī).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go</li><li>to go to</li><li>to proceed</li><li>to depart</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>गच्छति</i> (gacchati), see there for more. Cognate with Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀕𑀘𑁆𑀙𑀇</i> (gacchaï).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a vulture</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>गृध्र</i> (gṛdhra). Cognate with Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀕𑀺𑀤𑁆𑀥</i> (giddha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>donkey</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>गर्दभ</i> (gardabha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>name of the Pali verb root <i>gah</i></li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>householder; head of household</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>गृहपति</i> (gṛhapati). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀕𑀺𑀳𑀯𑀇</i> (gihavaï), <i>𑀕𑀳𑀯𑀇</i> (gahavaï). By surface analysis, tatpuruṣa compound of <i>gaha</i> +&lrm; <i>pati</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cause to take</li><li>to include, to embrace, to hold, to contain</li><li>to take, to seize</li><li>to obtain, to receive, to acquire, to buy</li><li>to inform, to teach, to undergo</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elephant</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>गज</i> (gaja). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀕𑀚</i> (gaja), <i>𑀕𑀬</i> (gaya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dribble, drip, trickle</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>गलति</i> (galati).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>going (<i>verbal noun</i>)</li></ol><p>From the root <i>gam</i> (“to go”) +&lrm; <i>-ana</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>deep</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) profound, difficult to comprehend</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>गम्भीर</i> (gambhīra).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send; to cause to go</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>गमयति</i> (gamayati).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Pali root <i>gam</i></li></ol><p>Extracted from forms of <i>gacchati</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>odor, scent, perfume</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>गन्ध</i> (gandha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a gandharva (one of a class of male heavenly beings, famed singers)</li><li>a musician, a singer</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>गन्धर्व</i> (gandharva).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scented room</li><li>private room once occupied by the Buddha</li></ol><p><i>gandha</i> +&lrm; <i>kuṭi</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>should be gone.</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figurative</i>) bond, fetter</li><li>composition, text</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>ग्रन्थ</i> (grantha, “binding”). From the date of appearance, the sense of 'composition' may be a semantic loan from Sanskrit <i>ग्रन्थ</i> (grantha, “composition”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reproach, to blame, scold, censure</li><li>to find fault with</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>गर्हति</i> (garhati).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>heavy</li><li>venerable</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teacher</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>गुरु</i> (gurú, “heavy”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>heavy</li><li>severe, serious</li><li>important</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>course</li><li>destiny</li><li>career</li><li>behavior</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>गति</i> (gati).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ganges</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gang, crowd</li><li>sect</li><li>(<i>Buddhism</i>) chapter of monks</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>गण</i> (gaṇa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>gaṇhāti</i> (“to seize”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take</li><li>to obtain</li><li>to comprehend</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>गृह्णाति</i> (gṛhṇāti, “to seize”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prostitute</li><li>courtesan, geisha</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nominative singular of <i>gaṇa</i> (“group of people”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knot, tie, joint</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>ग्रन्थि</i> (granthí). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀕𑀁𑀞𑀺</i> (gaṃṭhi).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>house</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>गेह</i> (geha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Pali root <i>ghamm</i></li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>overpowered by heat.</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>house</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>गृह</i> (gṛha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rat snake</li></ol><p><i>ghara</i> (“house”) +&lrm; <i>sappa</i> (“snake”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>frightful</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>घोर</i> (ghorá).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sound</li><li>shouting, wailing (as of ghosts)</li><li>voice</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) voiced</li></ol><p>The noun is inherited from Sanskrit <i>घोष</i> (ghoṣa). The grammatical term is a semantic loan from Sanskrit <i>घोष</i> (ghoṣa, “voiced”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fame; renown; praise</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grass</li></ol><p>Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀖𑀸𑀲</i> (ghāsa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rush</li><li>bow</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>घूठि</i> (ghūṭhi).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>greedy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>गृध्र</i> (gṛ́dhra).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sick</li></ol><p>From <i>gilāna</i> + <i>ka</i>, from Sanskrit <i>ग्लान</i> (glāna).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>summer</li><li>heat</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>ग्रीष्म</i> (grīṣma).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mountain</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>गिरि</i> (giri).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a mountain cleft</li><li>a gorge or cave</li></ol><p><i>giri</i> +&lrm; <i>guhā</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>word； utterance.</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cow, ox, bull</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>गो</i> (go).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of a large lizard</li><li>monitor lizard</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>गोधा</i> (godhā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wheat</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>गोधूम</i> (godhūma), from Proto-Indo-Iranian. Cognate with Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀕𑁄𑀳𑀽𑀫</i> (gohūma).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elk</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to guard</li><li>to protect</li><li>to watch</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>गोपयति</i> (gopayati).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a cowherd</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>गोपाल</i> (gopāla).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rafter (<i>an A-shaped frame supporting the roof of a hut</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cowshed</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>गोशाला</i> (gośālā). By surface analysis, tatpuruṣa compound of <i>go</i> +&lrm; <i>sālā</i>. Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀕𑁄𑀲𑀸𑀮𑀸</i> (gosālā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ox, bullock</li></ol><p>Obscurely derived from <i>go</i>. Compare Ardhamagadhi Prakrit <i>𑀕𑁄𑀡</i> (goṇa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cowshed</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>गोष्ठ</i> (goṣṭha). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀕𑁄𑀝𑁆𑀞</i> (gŏṭṭha), <i>𑀕𑀼𑀝𑁆𑀞</i> (guṭṭha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anus</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Pali root <i>guh</i></li></ol><p>In principle extracted from verbs such as <i>gūhati</i>, but probably also partly borrowed from Sanskrit <i>गुह्</i> (guh).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hiding place; cave</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) the heart</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>गुहा</i> (gúhā). The Pali root is <i>guh</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a bush, a thicket</li><li>a troop or swarm</li></ol><p>Variant of <i>&#42;gumma</i>, from Sanskrit <i>गुल्म</i> (gulma). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀕𑀼𑀫𑁆𑀫</i> (gumma).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>heavy</li><li>venerable</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teacher</li></ol><p>Cognate with Sanskrit <i>गुरु</i> (guru). It is an assimilatory modification of <i>garu</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with well-guarded senses</li></ol><p><i>gutta</i> (guarded) +&lrm; <i>dvāra</i> (door)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>protection, defence, guard</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>गुप्ति</i> (gúpti). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀕𑀼𑀢𑁆𑀢𑀺</i> (gutti).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>string, cord</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a thread of cloth</li><li>(<i>music</i>) string of an instrument</li><li>bowstring</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) part, constituent</li></ol><li>quality, attribute; especially a positive trait</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>गुण</i> (guṇa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>village</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>ग्राम</i> (grā́ma), from Proto-Indo-Aryan <i>&#42;grā́mas</i>, from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;grā́mas</i>. Cognate with Ashokan Prakrit <i>𑀕𑀸𑀫</i> (gāma).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>village</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>verse, stanza</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>गाथा</i> (gāthā), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;gaHtHa-</i>. Cognate with Avestan <i>𐬔𐬁𐬚𐬁</i> (gāθā, “form of strophe, metre, namely the collections of Zarathustra's songs”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cow</li></ol><p><i>go</i> +&lrm; <i>-ī</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>tightly</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>song</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>गीत</i> (gīta).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>necklace</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) neck</li></ol><p>Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀕𑀻𑀯𑀸</i> (gīvā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to conceal</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>गूहति</i> (gūhati)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>faeces, dung</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>गूथ</i> (gūtha), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;g(ʷ)uH-</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hidden</li><li>secret</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>गूळ्ह</i> (gūḷhá). By surface analysis, <i>guh</i> (root) +&lrm; <i>-ta</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) heart</li><li>feeling</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;ḱérd</td></tr></table></p><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>हृदय</i> (hṛ́daya). Cognate with Gandhari <i>𐨱𐨁𐨡𐨩</i> (hidaya), Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀳𑀺𑀅𑀅</i> (hiaa), Paisaci Prakrit <i>𑀳𑀺𑀢𑀅</i> (hitaa), Magadhi Prakrit <i>𑀳𑀮𑀅</i> (halaa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the letter 'h'</li></ol><p>Possibly from Sanskrit <i>हकार</i> (hakāra). By surface analysis, translingual <i>හ</i> (ha, “the letter”) +&lrm; <i>kāra</i> (“letter or sound”) etc.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a striker, a killer</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>हन्तर्</i> (hantar), which see for more. By surface analysis, <i>han</i> (root) +&lrm; <i>-tar</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Name of the Pali root <i>har</i></li></ol><p>Extracted from <i>harati</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carry, to take away</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>हरति</i> (hárati).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>removal, taking, stealing, seizing</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>हरण</i> (haraṇa). By surface analysis, <i>har</i> (root) +&lrm; <i>-ana</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a deer</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>हरिण</i> (hariṇa, “deer”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to laugh, to be merry</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>हसति</i> (hasati, “laughs”); distantly related to <i>jagghati</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hand</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>हस्त</i> (hásta), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;ȷ́ʰástas</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰes-</i>. Cognate with Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀳𑀢𑁆𑀣</i> (hattha), Sauraseni Prakrit <i>𑀳𑀢𑁆𑀣</i> (hattha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an elephant</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>हस्तिन्</i> (hastín).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swan, goose</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>हंस</i> (haṃsa, “swan”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>winter</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>हेमन्त</i> (hemanta).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neigh, neighing (<i>of a horse</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>हेषा</i> (heṣā). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀳𑁂𑀲𑀸</i> (hesā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cause, reason</li><li>condition</li><li>motive</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>हेतु</i> (hetu).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>for, because</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>indeed, certainly</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hiccup</li></ol><p>Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀳𑀺𑀓𑁆𑀓𑀸</i> (hikkā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snow</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>हिम</i> (hima, “snow”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>snowy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>हिमवत्</i> (himavat).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gold</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>हिरण्य</i> (híraṇya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>useful</li><li>beneficial</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>welfare, blessing, good</li><li>friend</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>हित</i> (hita).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>yesterday</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>ह्यः</i> (hyáḥ). Cognate with Sauraseni Prakrit <i>𑀳𑀺𑀚𑁆𑀚𑁄</i> (hijjo).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>violence, killing, injuring</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>हिंसा</i> (hiṃsā). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀳𑀺𑀁𑀲𑀸</i> (hiṃsā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>asafetida</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be, to exist</li></ol><p>Reduction of <i>bhavati</i> (“to be”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>laughter</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>हास</i> (hāsa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminution, decay, decrease</li><li>year</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>हायन</i> (hāyana, “passing away; leaving; quitting”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>menial</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>person</i>) retainer</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>इभ्य</i> (ibhya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>iti</i> (“thus”), used before vowels</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wish, desire, ask for</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>इच्छति</i> (icchati).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>desire, wish</li><li>longing</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>इच्छा</i> (icchā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prosperity</li><li>psychic power</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>ऋद्धि</i> (ṛddhi). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀅𑀤𑁆𑀥𑀺</i> (addhi), <i>𑀇𑀤𑁆𑀥𑀺</i> (iddhi), <i>𑀇𑀟𑁆𑀠𑀺</i> (iḍḍhi), <i>𑀭𑀺𑀤𑁆𑀥𑀺</i> (riddhi).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>here</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-Aryan <i>&#42;Hidʰá</i>, from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;Hidʰá</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁i-dʰe-</i> (“here”). Compare Sanskrit <i>इह</i> (ihá), Avestan <i>𐬌𐬛𐬁</i> (idā), <i>𐬌𐬜𐬀</i> (iδa), Ashokan Prakrit <i>𑀇𑀥</i> (idha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>To prosper</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>&#42;इध्यति</i> (*ídhyati), from the root <i>एध्</i> (edh).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>this (very close by)</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>demonstrative</i>) this</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lord, king</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>इन्द्र</i> (indra).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>directive force</li><li>faculty</li><li>sense</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Rigveda</li></ol><p>Adapted from Tamil <i>இருக்கு வேதம்</i> (irukku vētam), ultimatey from Sanskrit <i>ऋग्वेद</i> (ṛgveda).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sage, seer</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>ऋषि</i> (ṛṣi), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;r̥šíš</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bear</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>ऋक्ष</i> (ṛkṣa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a species of antelope</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>ऋश्य</i> (ṛ́śya, “antelope”) + <i>miga</i> (from Sanskrit <i>मृग</i> (mṛgá)), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;Hŕ̥ćyas</i> + <i>&#42;mr̥gás</i>, the first component from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁el-</i> (“deer”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to envy</li></ol><p>Denominative from <i>issā</i> (“jealousy, envy”), from Sanskrit <i>ईर्ष्या</i> (īrṣyā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jealousy</li><li>ill-will</li><li>envy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>ई॒र्ष्या</i> (īrṣyā́).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the northeast; northeastern direction</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>ईशान</i> (īśāna).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>any</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>thus</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>इति</i> (iti).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>history, tradition</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>इतिहास</i> (itihāsa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>surely</li><li>indeed</li><li>also</li></ol><p>Contraction of <i>iti</i> + <i>api</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woman, female</li><li>wife</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>स्त्री</i> (strī). The phonetically regular descendant is the rare <i>thī</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>like, as</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vowel 'i' or 'ī' or its sound</li></ol><p><i>i</i> (“the vowel sound /i/ or its letter”) +&lrm; <i>vaṇṇa</i> (“vowel”), probably a calque of Sanskrit <i>इवर्ण</i> (ivarṇa), but note the subtly different meaning.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>debt</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>ऋण</i> (ṛṇa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>debtor</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pleasant, pleasing, agreeable</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>इष्ट</i> (iṣṭa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brick</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>इष्टका</i> (iṣṭakā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to laugh</li><li>to deride, ridicule</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Indo-Aryan <i>&#42;ȷ́ʰágẓʰati</i> (“laughs”), from <i>&#42;ȷ́ʰagẓʰ-</i>, reduplication of the root <i>&#42;ȷ́ʰas-</i> (“to laugh”); compare Sanskrit <i>जक्षत्</i> (jákṣat, “laughing”) and <i>हसति</i> (hasati, “laughs”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leave</li><li>to abandon</li><li>to give up</li><li>to forsake</li></ol><p>Cognate with Sanskrit <i>जिहीते</i> (jihīte).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>जल</i> (jalá).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>genitive/dative singular of <i>jala</i> (“water”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burn</li><li>to shine</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>ज्वलति</i> (jválati). Cognate with Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀚𑀮𑀇</i> (jalaï).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wet dirt</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the rose apple tree</li><li>rose apple (the fruit)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>जम्बु</i> (jambu).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>India</li><li>(<i>Buddhism</i>) The 'continent' (<i>mahādīpa</i>) where men reside</li></ol><p><i>jambu</i> +&lrm; <i>dīpa</i>, itself ultimately from Sanskrit <i>जम्बुद्वीप</i> (jambudvīpa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the rose apple tree</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>jambudīpa</i> (“India”)</li></ol><p>As with the alternative form <i>jambudīpa</i>, <i>jambū</i> +&lrm; <i>dīpa</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>vulgar; contemptible</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>person; man; people</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>जन</i> (jana).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>producing, causing, generating</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>father</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>जनक</i> (janaka). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀚𑀡𑀬</i> (jaṇaya). By surface analysis, <i>jan</i> (root) +&lrm; <i>-aka</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>countryside</li><li>country, province</li><li>inhabited region, continent</li></ol><p><i>jana</i> +&lrm; <i>pada</i>. Compare Sanskrit <i>जनपद</i> (janapada). The term might not date back to the common ancestor of Pali and Sanskrit; the Sanskrit term has no attestation earlier than c. 600 BC.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>populace</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knee</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>creature; living being</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>old, decrepit</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>old age; decrepitude; decay</li></ol><p>See jara.</p><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>जरा</i> (jarā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>subject to growing old or decaying</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shoulder</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>speed</li><li>strength</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>जव</i> (java).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>victory</li><li>triumph</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>जय</i> (jaya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>jeti</i></li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>moving, movable</li></ol><p>Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀚𑀁𑀕𑀫</i> (jaṃgama).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the belly</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>जठर</i> (jaṭhara).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of ascetic with matted hair.</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jeta, a sponsor of the Buddha, former owner of the site of the Jetavana monastery.</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>jayati</i> 'to conquer'.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conqueror</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>जेतृ</i> (jetṛ).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to win</li><li>to conquer, to subdue</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>जयति</i> (jayati).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the month of Jyeshta, corresponding to May-June</li></ol></html>:/d͡ʑʱɑː.n̪ɐ/
<h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Buddhism</i>) dhyana, meditation, a state of collected, full-body awareness</li><li>contemplation</li></ol><p>Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀛𑀸𑀡</i> (jhāṇa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>setting fire to</li><li>funeral, cremation</li></ol><p>Nominal formation related to <i>jhāpeti</i> (“to set fire to”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burn, be on fire</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to waste away; to dry up</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to be consumed</li><li>to contemplate, think upon</li><li>to search for, hunt after</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>क्षायति</i> (kṣā́yati), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰgʷʰ-éh₁ye-ti</i>.</p><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>ध्यायति</i> (dhyāyati), from the root <i>ध्यै</i> (dhyai, “to think of”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>jeti</i></li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tongue</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>जिह्वा</i> (jihvā, “tongue”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to perform a sacrifice</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moonlight</li><li>a moonlit night</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>ज्योत्स्ना</i> (jyotsnā). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀚𑀼𑀡𑁆𑀳𑀸</i> (juṇhā), <i>𑀚𑁄𑀡𑁆𑀳𑀸</i> (jŏṇhā), <i>𑀚𑁄𑀇𑀲𑀺𑀡𑀸</i> (joisiṇā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>net</li><li>entanglement</li><li>snare; deception</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>जाल</i> (jālá).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knee</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>जानु</i> (jānu).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to know</li><li>to understand, comprehend</li><li>to notice</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>जानाति</i> (jānā́ti), from Proto-Indo-Aryan <i>&#42;ȷ́ānáHti</i>, from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;ȷ́ānáHti</i> (compare Avestan <i>𐬰𐬀𐬥𐬄𐬥</i> (zanąn), Old Persian <i>𐎧𐏁𐎴𐎠𐎿𐎠𐏃𐎹</i> (x-š-n-a-s-a-h-y /⁠xšnāsāhiy⁠/) (Persian شناختن (šenâxtan)), Northern Kurdish <i>zanîn</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵn̥néh₃ti</i> (“to know”) (compare Old Church Slavonic <i>знати</i> (znati), English <i>know</i>).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>born, produced, arisen</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>जात</i> (jāta).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>birth</li><li>rebirth</li><li>lineage, family, caste</li><li>variety</li></ol><p>Derived from Sanskrit <i>जाति</i> (jāti).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>circle of rebirths</li></ol><p><i>jāti</i> +&lrm; <i>saṃsāra</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>discussion about the parentage</li></ol><p><i>jāti</i> +&lrm; <i>vāda</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knee</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>जानु</i> (jānu). Doublet of <i>jānu</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>living</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>life</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>जीव</i> (jīvá).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to live, be alive</li><li>to subsist on</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>जीवति</i> (jīvati), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷeih₃w-</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>life, lifetime</li><li>livelihood</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>जीवित</i> (jīvitá).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the pride of life</li></ol><p><i>jīvita</i> +&lrm; <i>mada</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gambling</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>द्यूत</i> (dyūta), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;dyuH-</i>. Cognate with Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀚𑀽𑀯</i> (jūva).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>interrogative</i>) who</li><li>(<i>interrogative</i>) what</li><li>(<i>interrogative</i>) which</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Pali letter 'k'</li></ol><p>From the masculine and neuter respectively of Sanskrit&#32;<i>क</i>&#32;(<i>ká</i>), from Proto-Indo-Iranian&#32;<i>&#42;kás</i>, from Proto-Indo-European&#32;<i>&#42;kʷós</i>.</p><p>Probably from the pronunciation of a syllable consisting only of the letter.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poem, poetical composition</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tortoise, turtle</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>कच्छप</i> (kacchapa). Cognate with Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀓𑀘𑁆𑀙𑀯</i> (kacchava), Ardhamagadhi Prakrit <i>𑀓𑀘𑁆𑀙𑀪</i> (kacchabha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>indefinite</i>) whoever</li><li>(<i>indefinite</i>) whatever</li><li>anyone (chiefly in negative statements)</li><li>anything (chiefly in negative statements)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>किंचिद्</i> (kiṃcid).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>niggardliness</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chameleon</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crab</li></ol><p>Formally as <i>kakkaṭa</i> +&lrm; <i>-ka</i>, but whether <i>kakkaṭa</i> continued into Pali from Sanskrit <i>कर्कट</i> (karkaṭa, “crab”), as opposed to being restored by borrowing, is unclear. This word is clearly related to the Sanskrit word.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the letter 'k'</li></ol><p>Possibly from Sanskrit <i>ककार</i> (kakāra). By surface analysis, translingual <i>ක</i> (ka, “letter”) +&lrm; <i>kāra</i> (“letter or sound”) etc.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>young elephant</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>कलभ</i> (kalabha). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀓𑀮𑀪</i> (kalabha), <i>𑀓𑀮𑀳</i> (kalaha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quarrel</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>squirrel</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wave, surge, billow</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>कल्लोल</i> (kallola), of Dravidian origin. Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀓𑀮𑁆𑀮𑁄𑀮</i> (kallola).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a series of billows or waves</li></ol><p>Tatpuruṣa compound of <i>kallola</i> (“wave, billow”) +&lrm; <i>mālā</i> (“row, line, series”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sin</li><li>impurity</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>impure</li><li>dirty</li></ol><p>Compare Sanskrit <i>कलुष</i> (kaluṣa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>beautiful, charming</li><li>auspicious</li><li>helpful</li><li>good</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Sanskrit <i>कल्याण</i> (kalyāṇa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>virtuous, good</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beautiful girl</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lotus</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>कमल</i> (kamala).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to walk</li><li>to travel, go</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kamboja</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit Kamboja.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doing, deed, action</li><li>work, occupation</li><li>(Buddhism,&#32;Hinduism) karma</li><li>ceremony</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) building; weaving (<i>action of</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>कर्मन्</i> (kárman), from Proto-Indo-Aryan <i>&#42;kárma</i>, from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;kárma</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷer-</i> (“to do, make”). Cognate with Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀓𑀫𑁆𑀫</i> (kamma), Sauraseni Prakrit <i>𑀓𑀫𑁆𑀫</i> (kamma).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shake, to tremble</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>कम्पते</i> (kampate).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bulb, tuber</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>कन्द</i> (kanda).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>act of crying, lamentation</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>क्रन्दन</i> (krandana). Related to <i>kandati</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cry, lament</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>क्रन्दति</i> (krándati). Cognate with Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀓𑀁𑀤𑀇</i> (kaṃdaï), Sauraseni Prakrit <i>𑀓𑀁𑀤𑀤𑀺</i> (kaṃdadi). Related to <i>kandana</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>youngest</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>कनिष्ठ</i> (kaniṣṭha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lovely</li><li>enjoyable</li><li>pleasant</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>husband</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>कान्त</i> (kānta, “desired”). Doublet of <i>kāmita</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut, to cut off</li><li>to spin, to twist threads</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>कृन्तति</i> (kṛntati, “to cut”).</p><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>कृणत्ति</i> (kṛṇatti, “to spin”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beauty</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>कान्ति</i> (kānti). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀓𑀁𑀢𑀺</i> (kaṃti).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beloved woman</li><li>woman</li></ol><p>Absolute use of <i>kanta</i> (“lovely”, adjective)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>difficult to pass</li></ol><p>Uncertain, either from a hypothetical <i>kad-tarati</i>, from <i>kad</i> (“what”) +&lrm; <i>tarati</i> (“to cross over”), or from a formation related to Sanskrit <i>कान्तार</i> (kāntāra, “large forest”), itself possibly related to <i>कानन</i> (kānana, “forest”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>monkey</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>कपि</i> (kapi).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>brown, tawny</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kapilavastu</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) cheek</li></ol><p>Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀓𑀯𑁄𑀮</i> (kavola).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fit, suitable, worthy</li><li>nearly equal to, rivalling, resembling, like</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>time</li><li>a short time, moment</li><li>precept, rule, ordinance</li><li>rules concerning rites, one of the Vedaṅgas</li><li>practice, usage, mode</li><li>alternative, permission</li><li>a chapter or section of a book</li><li>thought</li><li>whole</li><li>a vast period, age, world cycle</li><li>Kappabindu</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>कल्प</i> (kalpa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elbow</li></ol><p>Probably inherited from Sanskrit <i>कूर्पर</i> (kūrpara), with the first vowel assimilating to the second. In this case, the intermediate stage is represented by Prakrit <i>𑀓𑀼𑀧𑁆𑀧𑀭</i> (kuppara).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cotton</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>कर्पास</i> (karpāsa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>camphor</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Pali root <i>kar</i></li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doing, action, making</li><li>production</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>करण</i> (karaṇa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>to be done, made</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>करणीय</i> (karaṇīya). By surface analysis, <i>kar</i> +&lrm; <i>-anīya</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>basket</li><li>the cast skin，slough of a serpent</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to build, create</li><li>to do, act</li><li>to produce</li><li>to compose (as a literary or musical work)</li><li>to impose</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>करोति</i> (karoti).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pity, compassion</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>करुणा</i> (karuṇā). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀓𑀭𑀼𑀡𑀸</i> (karuṇā), <i>𑀓𑀮𑀼𑀡𑀸</i> (kaluṇā), Gandhari <i>𐨐𐨪𐨂𐨣</i> (karuna).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>refuse</li><li>excrement, dung</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plough</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>कर्षति</i> (karṣati).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>katañjalin</i> (“waiing”)</li></ol><p>Romanisation of <i>กัตอัญชะลิน</i> (katañjalin), a Thai script form of katañjalin.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>waiing, in the namaste posture</li></ol><p><i>kata</i> (done) +&lrm; <i>añjali</i> (namaste) +&lrm; <i>-in</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>how?</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tell, narrate, speak, converse</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>कथयति</i> (kathayati, “to tell, narrate”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>speaking</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>speaker, preacher</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>speech, story, talk</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>friendly talk</li></ol><p><i>kathā</i> +&lrm; <i>sallāpa</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agreement, talk, conversation</li></ol><p>Likely derived either from <i>katheti</i> (“to speak, tell”) or <i>karoti</i> (“to build, do”), though which one exactly is unclear.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>to be done, made</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>कर्तव्य</i> (kartavya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doer, actor; maker</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) the subject, taking on the nominative case</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>कर्तृ</i> (kartṛ). Grammatically, the agent noun of <i>karoti</i> (“to do”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>where, whither?</li></ol><p>Compare Sanskrit <i>कुत्र</i> (kutra, “where?”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>musk</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>कस्तूरिका</i> (kastūrikā). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀓𑀢𑁆𑀣𑀽𑀭𑀺𑀬𑀸</i> (katthūriyā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(~māsa) the month of Kartika, corresponding to October-November</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Wanting to do</li></ol><p><i>kattuṃ</i> (“to do”) +&lrm; <i>-kāma</i> (“desirous”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a sacrifice, oblation</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mail, armour</li><li>coat of mail</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>कवच</i> (kavaca).. Doublet of <i>koja</i>?</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a poet</li><li>alternative form of <i>kapi</i> (“monkey”)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>कवि</i> (kavi).</p><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>कपि</i> (kapi). Doublet of <i>kapi</i> with lenited intervocalic stop.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>maiden; girl</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>कन्या</i> (kanyā), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;kanyáH</i> (“maiden, virgin, unmarried girl”). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀓𑀡𑁆𑀡𑀸</i> (kaṇṇā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw, to drag</li></ol><p>From the Sanskrit past participle <i>कृष्ट</i> (kṛṣṭa, “drawn, ploughed”) + Pali <i>-ati</i>. See the cognate Prakrit <i>𑀓𑀟𑁆𑀠𑀇</i> (kaḍḍhaï) for more discussion.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>top sprout of a plant or tree, (<i>especially of palm trees like coconut, which is edible</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bronze</li><li>bronze dish</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>कंस</i> (kaṃsa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of bronze</li></ol><p>From <i>kaṃsa</i> +&lrm; <i>-maya</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>black</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>कृष्ण</i> (kṛṣṇá), from Proto-Indo-Aryan <i>&#42;kr̥ṣnás</i>, from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;kr̥šnás</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kr̥snós</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kanhagotamaka, Black Gotamaka (the name of a royal snake lineage, associated with cobras)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cobra</li></ol><p><i>kaṇha</i> (“black”) +&lrm; <i>gotama</i> (“Gautama”) +&lrm; <i>-ka</i>. The common noun is derived from the proper noun.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>angle, corner</li><li>external ear, earflap</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>कर्ण</i> (karṇa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hard, firm, stiff</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) hard, harsh, cruel</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the cotton cloth which was annually supplied by the laity to the bhikkhus for the purpose of making robes</li><li>a wooden frame used by the bhikkhus in sewing aforementioned robes</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>कठिन</i> (kaṭhina​, “hard, difficult, stiff, firm”). The "monk's cotton cloth" sense is probably due to the stiffness of said cloth.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stick, a piece of wood</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>काष्ठ</i> (kāṣṭha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hair (of the head)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>केश</i> (keśa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hair relic</li></ol><p><i>kesa</i> +&lrm; <i>dhātu</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hair relic</li></ol><p><i>kesā</i> +&lrm; <i>dhātu</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>distinguishing sign</li><li>banner</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>केतु</i> (ketu).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>whole, entire</li></ol><p><i>kevala</i> (“complete”) + <i>kappa</i> (“suitable”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>for sale; purchasable</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>क्रय्य</i> (kráyya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sword</li><li>rhinoceros</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>खड्ग</i> (khaḍga).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rhinoceros</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>solid food (usually in compounds)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>edible, eatable</li></ol><p>From Vedic Sanskrit <i>खाद्य</i> (khādya). Also the gerund of <i>khajjati</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>firefly</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>ख</i> (kha, “sky; emptiness”) + <i>&#42;द्योपन</i> (&#42;dyopana) (compare <i>द्योतन</i> (dyotana, “shining, illuminating”)) + <i>-क</i> (-ka); compare <i>खद्योत</i> (khadyota).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the act of itching; an itch</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>खर्जु</i> (kharju). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀔𑀚𑁆𑀚𑀽</i> (khajjū).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>khajjopanaka</i> (“firefly”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stumble</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>patient, forgiving</li><li>enduring, hardened to</li></ol><p>Formed from the root kham, possibly in a stage earlier than Pali.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be patient with</li><li>to endure</li><li>to forgive</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>क्षमते</i> (kṣámate).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>patience, endurance</li><li>earth</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>क्षमा</i> (kṣamā). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀔𑀫𑀸</i> (khamā).</p><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>क्षमा</i> (kṣamā́, instrumental of <i>क्षम्</i> (kṣám)). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀔𑀫𑀸</i> (khamā), <i>𑀙𑀫𑀸</i> (chamā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>khaṇati</i> (“to dig”), which see for forms and usage</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the shoulders</li><li>trunk of a tree</li><li>a multitude</li><li>one of the five elements of being</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>स्कन्ध</i> (skandha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>camp</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nominative singular of <i>khandhāvāra</i> (“camp”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Buddhism</i>) patience, forbearance and forgiveness: one of the Ten Perfections.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>क्षान्ति</i> (kṣānti). Synchronically associated with the root kham.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>khanti</i> (“patience”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>donkey</li><li>water</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>खर</i> (khara).</p><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>क्षर</i>&nbsp;n (kṣara, “water”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water-lily</li></ol><p><i>khara</i> (“water”) +&lrm; <i>khaṇḍa</i> (“to_break”, root)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a member of the warrior or ruling class</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>क्षत्रिय</i> (kṣatriya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>decline, decadence</li><li>destruction</li><li>consumption (disease)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>क्षय</i> (kṣaya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A bier or bed on which the corpse is carried.</li></ol><p>Compare Sanskrit <i>खडु</i> (khaḍu).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moment</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>क्षण</i> (kṣaṇa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>act of digging</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>खनन</i> (khanana). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀔𑀡𑀡</i> (khaṇaṇa). By surface analysis, <i>khaṇ</i> (root) +&lrm; <i>-ana</i>. Related to <i>khaṇati</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dig</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>खनति</i> (khanati).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>safe</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>क्षेम</i> (kṣema).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>field</li><li>place of production</li></ol><p>Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀔𑁂𑀢𑁆𑀢</i> (khĕtta), <i>𑀙𑁂𑀢𑁆𑀢</i> (chĕtta).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spittle</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>क्ष्वेड</i> (kṣveḍa). The meaning of Sanskrit <i>क्ष्वेड</i> (kṣveḍa, “venom”) may represent a later specialisation.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>क्षिपति</i> (kṣipati).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the sound of sneezing</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>indeed</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shake</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>small</li><li>inferior</li><li>insignificant, trifling</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>क्षुद्रक</i> (kṣudraka).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hunger, appetite</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>क्षुधा</i> (kṣudhā), ultimately from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;ćšudʰ-</i> (“hunger”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>razor</li><li>animal hoof</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>क्षुर</i> (kṣurá), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;kšurás</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ksurós</i>.</p><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>खुर</i> (khura).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the act of eating</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>खादन</i> (khādana, “eating”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eat, devour</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>खादति</i> (khā́dati). Cognate with Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀔𑀸𑀅𑀇</i> (khāaï).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dug</li></ol><p>From Vedic Sanskrit <i>खात</i> (khāta), past participle of <i>खन्</i> (khan).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>milk, milky liquid</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>क्षीर</i> (kṣīra), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;kšiHrám</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coconut milk</li></ol><p><i>khīraṃ</i> (“milk”) +&lrm; <i>nāḷī</i> (“tube”). This is an unusual kind of tatpurusha, an 'alutta tappurisa', in which the first element retains its case ending, which is accusative in this case. The compound may also be supported by the word <i>nāḷikera</i> (“coconut palm”), though there is no formal connection.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that which ought to be done</li><li>duty, obligation</li><li>work</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>कृत्य</i> (kṛtya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be weary</li><li>to be in want of</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pleura</li></ol><p>Derived from Sanskrit <i>क्लोमन्</i> (kloman, “lung”) by addition of <i>-ka</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>worm</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>कृमि</i> (kṛ́mi), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷŕ̥mis</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gaunt, haggard</li><li>slender</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>कृश</i> (kṛśa), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;kr̥ćás</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kr̥ḱós</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fame</li><li>renown</li></ol><p>Cognate with Sanskrit <i>कीर्ति</i> (kīrti)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reputation</li></ol><p><i>kitti</i> +&lrm; <i>sadda</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>kitti</i> (“fame”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>anything, something</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>black</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anger</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>क्रोध</i> (krodha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pepper</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shouting</li><li>uproar</li><li>excitement</li><li>warning about something</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Sanskrit <i>कोलाहल</i> (kolāhala, “uproar”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>container, wrapper</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>storehouse, storeroom</li><li>sheath</li><li>foreskin</li></ol></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>कोश</i> (kośa, “container”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inhabitant of Kosala</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) Kosala</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>proficiency</li></ol><p>Compare Gandhari <i>𐨐𐨆𐨭𐨫</i> (kośala).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>silken</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>silk</li><li>silk cloth</li><li>owl</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Buddhism</i>) the name of a Brahmin clan, whom the deva Sakka is believed to originate from.</li><li>(<i>Buddhism</i>) the name of a monk.</li><li>A Literature, art festival event during Chhath on Hanumannagar.</li><li>A Kind of Pots which may used in Chhath Festival.</li></ol><p>From <i>kosika</i>, from Sanskrit <i>कौशिक</i> (kauśika).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>keeper of a treasury</li></ol><p><i>kosa</i> +&lrm; <i>ārakkha</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sheath</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>excitement</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abdomen; stomach</li><li>closet; monk's cell; storeroom</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>कोष्ठ</i> (koṣṭha). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀓𑁄𑀝𑁆𑀞</i> (kŏṭṭha), <i>𑀓𑀼𑀝𑁆𑀞</i> (kuṭṭha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>angry</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>क्रुद्ध</i> (kruddha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>interrogative</i>) when</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at any time</li></ol><p><i>kudā</i> +&lrm; <i>-canaṃ</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become angry</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>क्रुध्यति</i> (krudhyati), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;krawdʰ-</i> (“to be angry”). Cognate with Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀓𑀼𑀚𑁆𑀛𑀇</i> (kujjhaï).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dog</li></ol><p>Cognate with Sanskrit <i>कुर्कुर</i> (kurkurá).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cock, rooster</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>कुक्कुट</i> (kukkuṭá). Cognate with Niya Prakrit <i>𐨐𐨂𐨐𐨂𐨜</i> (kukuḍa), Prakrit <i>𑀓𑀼𑀓𑁆𑀓𑀼𑀟</i> (kukkuḍa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cockfight</li></ol><p>Tatpuruṣa compound of <i>kukkuṭa</i> +&lrm; <i>yuddha</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clan, caste, family</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>कुल</i> (kula).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lineage</li></ol><p><i>kula</i> +&lrm; <i>vaṃsa</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a winnowing basket</li><li>a raft (<i>woven</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>कुल्य</i>&nbsp;n (kulya, “bone, winnowing basket”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nest</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water pot, pitcher</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>कुम्भ</i> (kumbha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>potter</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>कुम्भकार</i> (kumbhakāra). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀓𑀼𑀁𑀪𑀆𑀭</i> (kuṃbhaāra), <i>𑀓𑀼𑀁𑀪𑀸𑀭</i> (kuṃbhāra). By surface analysis, <i>kumbha</i> +&lrm; <i>-kāra</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crocodile</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a tortoise</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>कूर्म</i> (kūrmá).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barley bread</li><li>a porridge or dumpling made from grain (probably a type of wheat or barley)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>angry</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>कुपित</i> (kupita).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>clever</li><li>(morally) correct</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>good action</li><li>merit</li><li>virtue</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>कुशल</i> (kuśala, adjective).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>whence</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>interrogative</i>) where</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>क्व</i> (kva, “where”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>interrogative</i>) where</li></ol><p>Apparently from synonymous <i>kuva</i> by analogical addition of the final nasal.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>key (object designed to open and close a lock)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>कुञ्चिका</i> (kuñcikā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cuckoo; Indian cuckoo</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>कुणाल</i> (kuṇāla, kind of bird).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hut, single-roomed dwelling</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>interrogative</i>) where</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>क्व</i> (kva, “where”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>somewhere, in some places</li><li>sometimes</li></ol><p>Univerbation of Sanskrit <i>क्व</i> (kva) <i>चिद्</i> (cid), synchronically yielding <i>kva</i> (“where?”) +&lrm; <i>-ci</i> (“some, any”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>interrogative</i>) who</li><li>(<i>interrogative</i>) what</li><li>(<i>interrogative</i>) which</li></ol><p>Synchronically, as 'thematic' feminine formed on masculine <i>ka</i> (“who”). This feminine formation goes back at least to Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;káH</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crow</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>काक</i> (kāka). Cognate with Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀓𑀸𑀅</i> (kāa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>full to the brim</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>time</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>काल</i> (kāla).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from time to time</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>temporal</li><li>timely</li></ol><p><i>kāla</i> (time) +&lrm; <i>-ika</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pleasure, lust, love, passion, enjoyment</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>काम</i> (kāma).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wretch</li></ol><p><i>ka</i> +&lrm; <i>purisa</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deed</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>service</li><li>act of mercy</li><li>worship, homage</li></ol><li>doing, manner</li><li>production, application (esp. in compounds)</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) item in question</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>letter</li><li>sound</li><li>word</li></ol></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>कार</i> (kāra); synchronically, as from the root of the verb karoti. The grammatical senses are likely to be borrowings of the Sanskrit cognate.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>final sound or letter of a word</li></ol><p><i>kāra</i> (“word”) +&lrm; <i>anta</i> (“end”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) causative</li></ol><p>The adjective is the regular past participle of the causative verb, and is inherited from Sanskrit <i>कारित</i> (kārita), where it is a bound form. The noun is borrowed from Sanskrit <i>कारित</i> (kārita), but may be interpreted a specific application of the adjective.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>to be done, fit to be done</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>कार्य</i> (kāryà).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>karuṇā</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>कारुण्य</i> (kāruṇya). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀓𑀸𑀭𑀼𑀡𑁆𑀡</i> (kāruṇṇa), <i>𑀓𑀸𑀮𑀼𑀡𑁆𑀡</i> (kāluṇṇa), <i>𑀓𑀸𑀮𑀼𑀡</i> (kāluṇa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jail</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Wanting to do</li></ol><p><i>kātuṃ</i> (“to do”) +&lrm; <i>-kāma</i> (“desirous”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>group, collection</li><li>body</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>काय</i> (kāya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>black</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>काल</i> (kāla).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bought, purchased</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>क्रीत</i> (krītá).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to play, sport, enjoy or amuse oneself</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>क्रीळति</i> (krīḷati).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>play, sport, enjoyment</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>क्रीडा</i> (krīḍā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trap, snare</li><li>deceit</li><li>prominence, top</li><li>heap</li><li>hammer</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dehorned</li></ol><p>Sanskrit <i>कूट</i> (kūṭa, “trap”).</p><p>Sanskrit <i>कूट</i> (kūṭa, “horn”).</p><p>Sanskrit <i>कूट</i> (kūṭa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a pinnacle building，or such a temporary construction； a catafalque．</li></ol><p><i>kūṭa</i> +&lrm; <i>āgāra</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>taken, obtained, received</li></ol><p>Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀮𑀤𑁆𑀥</i> (laddha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to adhere, to stick, to cling</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>लगति</i> (lagati).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>easily offended, touchy</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>quickly</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>auspicious</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sign</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>लक्षण</i> (lakṣaṇa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fortune, luck</li><li>splendor</li></ol><p>Sanskrit <i>लक्ष्मी</i> (lakṣmī).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synovial fluid</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>creeper</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>लता</i> (latā). Cognate with Gandhari <i>𐨫𐨡</i> (lada), Niya Prakrit <i>𐨫𐨡</i> (lada), Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀮𑀆</i> (laā), <i>𑀮𑀬𑀸</i> (layā), and Sauraseni Prakrit <i>𑀮𑀤𑀸</i> (ladā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clove</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>लवङ्ग</i> (lavaṅga), from Malayo-Polynesian. Cognate with Prakrit <i>लवंग</i> / <i>𑀮𑀯𑀁𑀕</i> (lavaṃga).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brief measure of time</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>लय</i> (laya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lanka</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Sanskrit <i>लङ्का</i> (laṅkā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sri Lanka</li></ol><p><i>laṅkā</i> +&lrm; <i>dīpa</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Indian quail</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>writer</li><li>scribe; copyist</li><li>clerk; secretary</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>streak</li><li>scratch, line</li><li>writing, missive</li><li>art of writing</li></ol><p>Inherited from synonymous लेखा (lekhā). A derivative of the root <i>likh</i>. Doublet of <i>rekhā</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smearing</li><li>plastering</li><li>anointing</li></ol><p><i>lip</i> (to smear) +&lrm; <i>-ana</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a cave (in a rock), mountain cave, esp. used by ascetics (or bhikkhus) as a hermitage or place of shelter</li><li>refuge, shelter</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) salvation</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>लयन</i> (layana, “lying; place of repose”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sign; mark; attribute; feature</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>लिङ्ग</i> (liṅga).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>greed, covetousness</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>लोभ</i> (lobha). Related to <i>luddha</i>, <i>lubbhati</i>. Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀮𑁄𑀳</i> (loha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>metal, especially copper, brass, or bronze</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>लोह</i> (loha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>red</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blood</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>लोहित</i> (lóhita), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;Hráwdʰitas</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁rewdʰ-</i> (“red”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>world</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>लोक</i> (loka). Cognate with Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀮𑁄𑀅</i> (loa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Knowing the universe．A common epithet of a Buddha</li></ol><p><i>loka</i> +&lrm; <i>vidū</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wavering, unsteady, agitated</li><li>longing, eager, greedy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>लोल</i> (lola).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>greed, longing, eagerness</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>लोलता</i> (lolatā). By surface analysis, <i>lola</i> +&lrm; <i>-tā</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hair (of the body)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>लोमन्</i> (lóman), a variant of <i>रोमन्</i> (róman). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀮𑁄𑀫</i> (loma).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goose bumps, horripilation</li></ol><p><i>loma</i> +&lrm; <i>haṃsa</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nominative singular of <i>lomahaṃsa</i> (“goose bumps”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>instrumental singular of <i>loma</i> (“hair”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cutting off</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) elision</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) apocope</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>लोप</i> (lopa); the Pali root is <i>lup</i>. The grammatical terms may be borrowings or semantic loans from Sanskrit.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>salt (sodium chloride)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>लवण</i> (lavaṇa). Doublet of <i>lavaṇa</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>salty, alkaline</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>लावणिक</i> (lāvaṇika).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>salty, alkaline</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>लवणित</i> (lavaṇita). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀮𑁄𑀡𑀺𑀬</i> (loṇiya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>broken up</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>रुग्ण</i> (ruɡṇá), with the eastern tendency of <i>r</i> &gt; <i>l</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ruj</i> (root) +&lrm; <i>-na</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be broken up, to break up</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>रुज्यते</i> (rujyate), with eastern tendency for <i>r</i> to become <i>l</i> and the usual change of voice from middle to active.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break, to harm, to injure</li><li>to attack, to plunder</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>लुम्पति</i> (lumpati).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>broken, cut off</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) elided</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>लुप्त</i> (lupta). By surface analysis, <i>lup</i> (root) +&lrm; <i>-ta</i>. Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀮𑀼𑀢𑁆𑀢</i> (lutta).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>talk</li><li>(<i>bird</i>) quail</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>लाप</i> (lāpa, “talking”).</p><p>The connection between 'talk' and 'quail' is that quails seem to talk a lot. The form corresponding to Sanskrit <i>लाब</i> (lāba, “quail”) would have merged with <i>&#42;lāpa</i> (“talker”) in some eastern dialects.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>man</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>मर्त्य</i> (martya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fish</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मत्स्य</i> (mátsya), from Proto-Indo-Aryan <i>&#42;mátsyas</i>, from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;mátsyas</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shoal of fish</li></ol><p><i>maccha</i> +&lrm; <i>gumba</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>death</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मृत्यु</i> (mṛtyu), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;mr̥tyúš</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mer-</i> (“to die”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intoxication</li><li>infatuation; obsession</li><li>conceit; self-importance</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intoxication</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मदन</i> (madana). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀫𑀅𑀡</i> (maaṇa), <i>𑀫𑀬𑀡</i> (mayaṇa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crush, tread on, destroy, knead, jumble</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मर्दति</i> (mardati, “to pound, crush, tread on, squeeze, rub”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>softness, gentleness, mildness</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>soft, gentle, mild</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मार्दव</i> (mārdava). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀫𑀤𑁆𑀤𑀯</i> (maddava).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>softness, gentleness, mildness</li></ol><p>From <i>maddava</i> (“soft, gentle”, adjective) +&lrm; <i>-tā</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>honey</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मधु</i> (madhu).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bee</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मधुकर</i> (madhukara). Morphologically <i>madhu</i> +&lrm; <i>-kara</i>, literally “honey-maker”.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>weak and stiff</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intoxicating drink, spirit</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मदिरा</i> (madirā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>road, way, path</li></ol><p>Cognate with Ashokan Prakrit <i>𑀫𑀕</i> (maga).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>religious festival</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>great, big, massive</li><li>important, well-known</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>महत्</i> (mahat, “great”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to honour, to revere</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an ocean</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>महार्णव</i> (mahārṇava). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀫𑀳𑀡𑁆𑀡𑀯</i> (mahaṇṇava).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>buffalo (<i>Old World</i>)</li></ol><p>See <i>mahiṃsa</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>buffalo (<i>Old World</i>)</li></ol><p>Although the simplest form suggests that these words are simply inherited from Sanskrit <i>महिष</i> (mahiṣa), the other forms suggest some compounding or contamination with the root <i>is</i> (“to have power”) as in <i>īsa</i> (“lord”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Buddhism</i>) the Bo Tree, i.e. the specific fig tree under which the Buddha sat when he attained enlightenment; the descendant of that tree on the same spot at Bodh Gaya.</li><li>Male name, especially of monks</li></ol><p><i>mahā</i> +&lrm; <i>bodhi</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Buddhism</i>) Maha Kassapa, one of Ten Principal Disciples of Gautama Buddha</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an epithet of Gautama Buddha</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Sanskrit <i>महामुनि</i> (mahāmuni, literally “the Great Sage”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>great man</li></ol><p><i>mahā</i> +&lrm; <i>purisa</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>great king</li></ol><p><i>mahant</i> (“great”) +&lrm; <i>rājan</i> (“king”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the great ocean</li></ol><p><i>mahā</i> +&lrm; <i>samudda</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>luxurious bed</li></ol><p><i>mahant</i> (big) +&lrm; <i>sayana</i> (bed)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intoxicant</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मद्य</i> (madya, “intoxicating drink”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drinking of intoxicating liquors</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मद्यपान</i> (madyapāna). Compare Prakrit <i>𑀫𑀚𑁆𑀚𑀧𑀸𑀡</i> (majjapāṇa). By surface analysis, tatpuruṣa compound of <i>majja</i> +&lrm; <i>pāna</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wipe, to polish, to clean</li><li>to be intoxicated</li><li>to be exultant</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>माद्यति</i> (mādyati, “to be drunk”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>middle</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मध्य</i> (mádhya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>middle, medium, mediocre</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) In combination with the word for 'person', the meaning is "second".</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मध्यम</i> (madhyama, “middlemost”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>majjhimapurisa</i> (“second person”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) second person</li></ol><p>Calque of Sanskrit <i>मध्यमपुरुष</i> (madhyamapuruṣa), composed as <i>majjhima</i> (“middle”) +&lrm; <i>purisa</i> (“person”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>middle finger</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) ring finger</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cat</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मार्जार</i> (mārjāra). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀫𑀚𑁆𑀚𑀸𑀭</i> (majjāra), <i>𑀫𑀁𑀚𑀸𑀭</i> (maṃjāra).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fly</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मक्षिका</i> (makṣikā). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀫𑀓𑁆𑀔𑀺𑀆</i> (makkhiā), <i>𑀫𑀘𑁆𑀙𑀺𑀆</i> (macchiā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crest, crown, coronet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dravidian, compare Modern Tamil <i>முகடு</i> (mukaṭu), Sanskrit <i>मुकुट</i> (mukuṭa, “crown”) (a borrowing).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the letter 'm'</li></ol><p>Possibly from Sanskrit <i>मकार</i> (makāra). By surface analysis, translingual <i>ම</i> (the letter) +&lrm; <i>kāra</i> (term designating a letter or sound) etc.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>impurity</li><li>stain</li><li>rust</li><li>dirt</li><li>dung</li></ol><p>Related to Sanskrit <i>मल</i> (mala).</p><p><i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wrestler</li><li>a man of the Malla clan</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a vital spot of the body</li><li>nerve center</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Interpretation of many of the inflectional forms of <i>manas</i> (“mind”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mind</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>मनस्</i> (manas, “mind”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a little</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>slow</li><li>dull</li><li>lazy</li><li>foolish</li><li>giving a little</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slowness</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mind made</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>charm, spell, incantation</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>मन्त्र</i> (mantra).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bearer of Vedic charms</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>counsel, consultation, advice, command</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>counsellor</li><li>minister</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मन्त्रिन्</i> (mantrín).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who imagines</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>मन्तु</i> (mantu).</p></html>:/manus.sa/
<h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>man, human being</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मनुष्य</i> (manuṣya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manhood</li><li>humanity</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मनुष्यत्व</i> (manuṣyatvá) (compare <i>मनुष्यता</i> (manuṣyatā)). Cognate with Prakrit <i>मणुस्सत्त</i> (maṇussatta). By surface analysis, <i>manussa</i> +&lrm; <i>-tta</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ablative/vocative singular of <i>manas</i></li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pleasing, charming</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to die</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मरति</i> (marati).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>death</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मरण</i> (maraṇa). Cognate with Ashokan Prakrit <i>𑀫𑀮𑀦</i> (malana, Kalsi), <i>𐨨𐨪𐨞</i> (maraṇa, Mansehra, Shahbazgarhi) / <i>𑀫𑀭𑀡</i> (maraṇa, Girnar), (whence Prakrit <i>𑀫𑀭𑀡</i> (maraṇa)). By surface analysis, <i>mar</i> (root) +&lrm; <i>-ana</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>black pepper</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deity</li><li>sand</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) sandy waste</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soot; charcoal dust</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beard</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dead</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>opinion, view</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मृत</i> (mṛta).</p><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मत</i> (mata).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mind</li><li>opinion, thought</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मति</i> (mati).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intoxicated</li><li>exultant</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मत्त</i> (mattá). Cognate to Prakrit <i>𑀫𑀢𑁆𑀢</i> (matta).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>head, forehead</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मस्त</i> (masta). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀫𑀢𑁆𑀣</i> (mattha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>head</li><li>top, summmit</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brain</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of clay</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clay, soil</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>मृत्तिका</i> (mṛttikā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>charming; lovely</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>मञ्जु</i> (mañju).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a casket (<i>used for keeping important documents</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मञ्जूषा</i> (mañjūṣā), ultimately of Dravidian origin. Compare Prakrit <i>𑀫𑀁𑀚𑀽𑀲𑀸</i> (maṃjūsā, “box, chest”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to think</li><li>to have an opinion</li><li>to deem</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flesh, meat</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मांस</i> (māṃsa). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀫𑀁𑀲</i> (maṃsa), <i>𑀫𑀸𑀲</i> (māsa), <i>𑀫𑀁𑀰</i> (maṃśa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>auspicious, royal, lucky, festive</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>festivity; good omen; ceremony; prosperity</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मङ्गल</i> (maṅgala).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>silent, downcast．</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jewel; gem</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मणि</i> (maṇi). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀫𑀡𑀺</i> (maṇi).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) wrist</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मणिबन्ध</i> (maṇibandha). By surface analysis, <i>maṇi</i> +&lrm; <i>bandha</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jeweler</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मणिकार</i> (maṇikārá). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀫𑀡𑀺𑀆𑀭</i> (maṇiāra), <i>𑀫𑀡𑀺𑀬𑀸𑀭</i> (maṇiyāra). By surface analysis, <i>maṇi</i> +&lrm; <i>-kāra</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of or consisting of jewels</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मणिमय</i> (maṇimaya). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀫𑀡𑀺𑀫𑀬</i> (maṇimaya). By surface analysis, <i>maṇi</i> +&lrm; <i>-maya</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a valuable gem</li></ol><p><i>maṇi</i> +&lrm; <i>ratana</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scum, cream</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मण्ड</i> (maṇḍa, “scum of boiled rice”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>circle</li><li>disc</li><li>round platform</li><li>circus ring</li><li>round flat surface</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>मण्डल</i> (maṇḍala).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pavilion</li></ol><p><i>maṇḍala</i> +&lrm; <i>māḷa</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adornment</li><li>decoration</li></ol><p><i>maṇḍ</i> (adorn) +&lrm; <i>-ana</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>desirous of adornment</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>temporary shed</li><li>pavilion</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>मण्डप</i> (maṇḍapa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to adorn</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fat</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मेदस्</i> (medas).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intellectual</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मेध</i> (médha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wisdom</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मेधा</i> (medhā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cloud</li><li>rain</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मेघ</i> (meghá), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;Hmaygʰás</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃moygʰ-ós</i> (“fog, cloud”), from <i>&#42;h₃meygʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>male or female organ</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pure; fit for sacrifice</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मेध्य</i> (medhya, “fit for sacrifice; pure”). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀫𑁂𑀚𑁆𑀛</i> (mĕjjha), <i>𑀫𑀺𑀚𑁆𑀛</i> (mijjha). Related to <i>medha</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fermented liquor</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ram</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goodwill, loving-kindness</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>मैत्री</i> (maitrī).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ram, sheep</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>wrong, wrongly</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>मिथ्या</i> (mithyā, “falsedom”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misconduct</li></ol><p><i>micchā</i> +&lrm; <i>cāra</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wrong effort</li></ol><p><i>micchā</i> +&lrm; <i>vāyāma</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>torpor, drowsiness</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beast</li><li>quadruped</li><li>deer</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मृग</i> (mṛga, “wild beast”), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;mr̥gás</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a pair of a male and a female</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>मिथुन</i> (mithuna).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>friend</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) friendship</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मित्र</i> (mitrá), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;mitrás</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>friendship</li></ol><p><i>mitta</i> +&lrm; <i>-tā</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plantain tree</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rejoiced</li><li>to be satisfied</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stupidity</li><li>delusion</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मोह</i> (moha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>release, freedom, deliverance, liberation</li></ol><p>Cognate with Ashokan Prakrit <i>𑀫𑁄𑀔</i> (mokha /⁠mokkha⁠/).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wisdom</li><li>self-possession</li><li>silence</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peacock</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मयूर</i> (mayūra).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>theft</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>untruth</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>foolish</li><li>bewildered</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मुग्ध</i> (mugdha, “gone astray; foolish”), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;mugdʰás</i>. Related to <i>mūḷha</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>head</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मूर्धन्</i> (mūrdhan).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>properly anointed (king)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seal</li><li>stamp</li><li>impression</li><li>gesture</li><li>printing</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मुद्रा</i> (mudrā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a small drum</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>supple</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>मृदु</i> (mṛdu); see there for more.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>supple, malleable</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gladness</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a moment, an instant; a very brief or fleeting period of time</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मुहूर्त</i> (muhūrta).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mouth</li><li>face</li><li>entrance</li><li>opening</li><li>front</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मुख</i> (mukha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>entrance of the mouth</li></ol><p><i>mukha</i> (“face”) +&lrm; <i>dvāra</i> (“door”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>monk, sage</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Sanskrit <i>मुनि</i> (muni, “sage, ascetic”), from <i>मन्</i> (man, “think”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chief of sages (an epithet of the Buddha)</li></ol><p><i>muni</i> (“sage”) +&lrm; <i>inda</i> (“lord”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pestle</li><li>club (<i>weapon</i>)</li><li>crowbar</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मुसल</i> (musala, “pestle”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to steal, rob, plunder</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मूषति</i> (mūṣati, “to steal”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to forget</li><li>to pass into oblivion</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मृष्यति</i> (mṛṣyati, “forgets”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mers-</i> (“to forget”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>lyingly</li><li>lie, falsehood</li><li>falsely, wrongly</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मृषा</i> (mṛṣā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>telling lies</li></ol><p><i>musā</i> (“lying”) +&lrm; <i>vāda</i> (“speech”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a small drum</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>freed</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>urine</li></ol><p>See the entry.</p><p>From Sanskrit <i>मूत्र</i> (mū́tra, “urine”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>release</li><li>freedom</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pearl</li></ol><p>These words and forms are all applications of the past participle of <i>muñcati</i> (“to release”); the nouns are specific applications. The specific meaning 'pearl' is shared with and might be derived from Sanskrit <i>मुक्ता</i> (muktā) (see there for more information).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to loose</li><li>to release</li><li>to dismiss, to give up, to abandon, to omit</li><li>to send forth</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मुञ्चति</i> (muñcati).</p><p>The use of the active form with passive meaning has been attributed to a similarity at some point of writings of the conjuncts 'cc' and 'ñc'.</p><p>Although printed Pali uses touching letters for the most part and restricts conjuncts to those with <i>ය</i> (ya) and <i>ර</i> (ra) and those which remove the right hand portion of <i>ක</i> (ka), <i>ත</i> (ta) and <i>න</i> (na), much greater use of conjuncts has been made in manuscripts. Thus it is reported that there is a ligature for 'cc' (but <i>ඩ</i> (ḍa)) as well as for 'ñc' namely <i>ඤ්‍ච</i> (ñca). Now, the latter is formed by prefixing sanyaka, which can denote a nasal sound or else gemination, and it is possible that we have a conflict in usage. For consonants other than palatals, sanyaka seems to denote a nasal only for the voiced unaspirated stop.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of grass used in making slippers, etc.</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the fist</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मुष्टि</i> (muṣṭí), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;muštís</i> (“fist”). Cognate with Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀫𑀼𑀝𑁆𑀞𑀺</i> (muṭṭhi), Sauraseni Prakrit <i>𑀫𑀼𑀝𑁆𑀞𑀺</i> (muṭṭhi).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>prohibitive</i>) do not</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mother</li><li>moon</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मा</i> (mā, “do not”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Magadhan</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nominative singular masculine of <i>māgadha</i> (“Magadhan”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(~māsa) the month of Margashirsha, corresponding to November–December</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(~māsa) the month of Magha, corresponding with January–February</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>circular enclosure, round yard</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>garland, wreath</li><li>a string of flowers</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pride</li><li>conceit</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>मान</i> (māna).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>human</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who is proud</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>killer</li><li>one who frustrates other's benefaction</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mara, the Evil One, the tempter, death personified</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the realm of Mara</li></ol><p><i>māra</i> +&lrm; <i>dheyya</i> (“realm”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a month</li><li>mungo bean, black matpe bean, <i>Vigna mungo</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मास</i> (māsa, “month”).</p><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>माष</i> (māṣa, “mungo bean”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mother</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मातृ</i> (mātṛ).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elephant</li><li>low-caste man</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water course</li><li>table of contents</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>womankind</li></ol><p><i>mātar</i> (“mother”) +&lrm; <i>gāma</i> (“village”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>maternal uncle; mother's brother</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मातुल</i> (mātula). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀫𑀸𑀉𑀮</i> (māula).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>mātula</i> (“maternal uncle; mother's brother”)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मातुलक</i> (mātulaka).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mother and father</li><li>parents</li></ol><p>Dvanda compound <i>mātar</i> +&lrm; <i>pitar</i>. The precise formation can be traced back to Sanskrit <i>मातापितृ</i> (mātāpitṛ)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fraud</li><li>deceit</li><li>magic</li><li>jugglery</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>माया</i> (māyā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>young man</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>मानव</i> (mānava).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a young man</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fish</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मीन</i> (mīna).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dumb</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dumb person</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मूक</i> (mūka).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>root</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>मूल</i> (mū́la).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crucible</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>foolish</li><li>bewildered, confused</li><li>gone astray, erring</li><li>infatuated, blinded</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>मूढ</i> (mūḍhá). See the Sanskrit root <i>मुह्</i> (muh) for more. Related to <i>muddha</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>no, not</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>demonstrative</i>) that</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>demonstrative</i>) that</li><li>him, it</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) them</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>न</i> (na).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mist, vapour; clouds</li><li>sky</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>नभः</i> (nabhaḥ). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀡𑀳</i> (ṇaha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dancing (for an audience)</li><li>a play</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>river</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>नदी</i> (nadī).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bathe</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sinew, tendon</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Indo-Aryan <i>&#42;snáHwar</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;snéh₁wr̥</i>. Compare Sanskrit <i>स्नायु</i> (snāyu), <i>स्नावन्</i> (snāvan).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nail</li><li>claw</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>नख</i> (nakhá).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>constellation (esp. lunar mansion)</li><li>celebration</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>नक्षत्र</i> (nakṣatra).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mongoose</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>नकुल</i> (nakula).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pay honour to</li><li>to venerate</li></ol><p>Denominative from <i>namo</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend</li><li>to bow down</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>नमति</i> (namati, “to bend”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>salutation</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>नमः</i> (namaḥ). The Pali root is <i>nam</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>man</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>नर</i> (nára).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>purgatory, hell</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>night</li></ol><p>Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀡𑀢𑁆𑀢</i> (ṇatta).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>there is not</li></ol><p><i>na</i> (“not”) +&lrm; <i>atthi</i> (“is”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>nine</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>नव</i> (náva).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>new works</li></ol><p><i>nava</i> (“new”) +&lrm; <i>kamma</i> (“work”), the first compoment from Sanskrit <i>नव</i> (náva, “new”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>ninth</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>नवम</i> (navamá).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fresh butter</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>नवनीत</i> (navanīta).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>leading</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>method</li><li>plan</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eye</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Sanskrit <i>नयन</i> (nayana).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>neti</i> (“to lead”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plough</li></ol><p>Compare Sanskrit <i>लाङ्गल</i> (lāṅgala) and Hindi <i>नांगल</i> (nāṅgal).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>several</li><li>many</li></ol><p>Aphetic form of aneka</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Buddhism</i>) Renunciation, one of the Ten Perfections.</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>नैष्काम्य</i> (naiṣkāmya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leader, guide</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>नेतृ</i> (netṛ). By surface analysis, <i>nī</i> (root) +&lrm; <i>-tar</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lead, conduct, show the way</li><li>to deduce, understand</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>नयति</i> (náyati). Cognate with Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀡𑁂𑀇</i> (ṇei), Sauraseni Prakrit <i>𑀡𑁂𑀤𑀺</i> (ṇedi).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>nahāru</i> (“sinew”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unravel</li><li>to explain</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fearless, unafraid</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>निर्भय</i> (nirbhaya). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀡𑀺𑀩𑁆𑀪𑀬</i> (ṇibbhaya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have enough of</li><li>to be disgusted with</li><li>to turn away from</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Buddhism</i>) nirvana</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>निर्वाण</i> (nirvāṇa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put out, extinguish</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>constant</li><li>continuous</li><li>permanent</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>permanence</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>नित्य</i> (nítya, “everlasting”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>perpetually</li><li>constantly</li><li>always</li></ol><p>The adverb is regularly derived from the adjective, being identical with its neuter singular.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>description, analytic explanation</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>निर्देश</i> (nirdeśa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sleep, slumber</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>निद्रा</i> (nidrā). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀡𑀺𑀤𑁆𑀤𑀸</i> (ṇiddā), <i>𑀡𑁳𑀤𑁆𑀤𑀸</i> (ṇĕddā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sleep</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>निद्रायति</i> (nidrāyati). Synchronically a denominative from <i>niddā</i> (“sleep”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>treasure</li><li>receptacle</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>निधि</i> (nidhi). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀡𑀺𑀳𑀺</i> (ṇihi).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>source, cause, origin</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>निदान</i> (nidāna).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The nasal consonant ṃ in the Pali language.</li></ol><p>The noun is a specific application of the adjective.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The nasal consonant ṃ in the Pali language.</li></ol><p>The noun is a specific application of the adjective.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>niggaṇhāti</i> (“to restrain, to rebuke”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to restrain</li><li>to rebuke</li></ol><p><i>ni-</i> +&lrm; <i>gaṇhāti</i> and <i>ni-</i> +&lrm; <i>gaṇhati</i></p><p>Although there is a Vedic Sanskrit verb <i>निगृह्णाति</i> (nigṛhṇāti, “to hold back”), the gemination of the initial of the simplex after prefixes, as in the aorist <i>aggahi</i>, indicates an intermediate stage with the full grade of the root rather than the zero grade.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a dictionary of synonyms</li></ol><p>Compare Sanskrit <i>निघण्टु</i> (nighaṇṭu).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>banyan tree</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>न्यग्रोध</i> (nyagrodha); see there for more. Cognate with Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀡𑀕𑁆𑀕𑁄𑀳</i> (ṇaggoha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go out, to go forth</li><li>to leave the worldly life</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>निष्क्रामति</i> (niṣkrāmati) but rather with the zero grade seen in the middle <i>निष्क्रमते</i> (niṣkramate) with synchronic support as <i>nis-</i> +&lrm; <i>kamati</i>, via which source however it is also a doublet of <i>nikkamati</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>turned upside down</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>group</li><li>collection</li><li>(<i>Buddhism</i>) sect or chapter of monks</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>निकाय</i> (nikāya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plundering</li><li>plunder</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>निर्लोप</i> (nirlopa, “plundering, spoil”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sign, omen, portent, prognostication</li><li>outward appearance, mark, characteristic, attribute, phenomenon</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Sanskrit <i>निमित्त</i> (nimitta​).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>clear, spotless, clean, pure</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>निर्मल</i> (nirmala). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀡𑀺𑀫𑁆𑀫𑀮</i> (ṇimmala).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crush</li><li>to subdue</li><li>to humiliate</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sink down</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diving, sinking, ducking</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blame, censure, reproach</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>निन्दा</i> (nindā́). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀡𑀺𑀁𑀤𑀸</i> (ṇiṃdā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>having bowed down</li></ol><p><i><i>ni-</i> &#43;‎ <i>pat</i></i> +&lrm; <i>-ya</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>humbleness</li><li>obedience</li><li>respect</li></ol><p><i>nipacca</i> (“having bowed down”) +&lrm; <i>ākāra</i> (“manner”). The alternative form nipaccakāra is formally <i>nipacca</i> (“having bowed down”) +&lrm; <i>kāra</i> (“deed; act of homage”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>clever</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall down</li><li>to descend</li><li>to convene</li></ol><p><i>ni-</i> +&lrm; <i>patati</i> (“to fall down”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Purgatory; hell</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>निरय</i> (niraya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cessation</li></ol><p>From the root <i>rudh</i>, formed as <i>ni-</i> +&lrm; <i>rudh</i> +&lrm; <i>-a</i>. Cognate or parallel to Sanskrit <i>निरोध</i> (nirodha, “confinement;destruction”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cease, to perish, to be destroyed</li></ol><p><i>ni-</i> +&lrm; <i>rujjhati</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>restraining, holding back</li><li>prohibition; protection</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>निषेध</i> (niṣedha). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀡𑀺𑀲𑁂𑀳</i> (ṇiseha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sitting down, seated</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sharp</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>निशित</i> (niśita). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀡𑀺𑀲𑀺𑀅</i> (ṇisia).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>support</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lean on, to rest on</li><li>to have as a foundation</li></ol><p>Formed from the root <i>si</i> (“to lean on”) (as in Sanskrit <i>श्रयते</i> (śrayate)) by adding the prefix <i>ni-</i>. Formally corresponds to post-Vedic Sanskrit <i>निश्रयते</i> (niśrayate), but this appears to be a parallel development.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>close to, near, by</li><li>on account of, by reason of, through, for</li></ol><p>The meaning has expanded by metaphor from its basic meaning as the absolutive of <i>nissayati</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>night</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>निशा</i> (niśā). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀡𑀺𑀲𑀸</i> (ṇisā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sit down</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>settlement, house</li></ol><p>Compare with Sanskrit <i>निवेशन</i> (niveśana).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sown</li><li>thrown (<i>said of food</i>)</li><li>shaven</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>न्युप्त</i> (nyùpta).</p><p><i>ni</i> +&lrm; <i>vutta</i> (“shaven”), from Sanskrit <i>व्युप्त</i> (vyupta), <i>उप्त</i> (upta).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>going out, departure</li><li>release, deliverance</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>leading to salvation</li><li>profitable</li></ol><p><i>niyyāna</i> +&lrm; <i>-ika</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nest</li><li>place, seat</li></ol><p>From Old Indo Aryan <i>&#42;niḍḍʰá-</i> (compare attested Vedic Sanskrit <i>नीड</i> (nīḍá) and <i>नीळ</i> (nīḷá)), from Proto-Indo-Aryan <i>&#42;niẓḍás</i>, from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;niždás</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;nisdós</i> (“nest”). Doublet of <i>nīḷa</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>surely not</li><li>indeed not</li><li>indeed, then, now</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>नः</i> (naḥ, “us”).</p><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>नो</i> (no, “and not”).</p><p>Emphatic form of <i>nu</i> (“then, now”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>noce</i> (“unless”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>if not, unless</li></ol><p><i>no</i> (not) +&lrm; <i>ce</i> (if). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>नो चेद्</i> (no ced, “if not”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>navanīta</i>; fresh butter</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>नवनीत</i> (navanīta).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>then, now</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>नु</i> (nu), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;nu</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to push</li><li>to expel, to push away, to reject</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>that</li></ol><p>Synchronically, feminine pronoun from the demonstrative na (“that”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>navel</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>नाभि</i> (nā́bhi).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>serpent</li><li>cobra</li><li>naga, dragon</li><li>elephant</li><li>ironwood tree</li><li>chief</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>नाग</i> (nāga).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the naga realm</li></ol><p><i>nāga</i> (naga) +&lrm; <i>loka</i> (world)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citizen</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>नागर</i> (nāgara); related to <i>nagara</i>. Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀡𑀸𑀬𑀭</i> (ṇāyara).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heaven; firmament</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>नाक</i> (nāka).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>name</li><li>immaterial factors such as consciousness and perception</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) substantive, i.e. noun or adjective</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>नामन्</i> (nāman). Cognate with Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀡𑀸𑀫</i> (ṇāma).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a woman, a female</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>नारी</i> (nā́rī).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nose</li><li>trunk (of an elephant)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>नासा</i> (nāsā), nominative dual of Sanskrit <i>नस्</i> (nas, “nostril, nose”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>protector</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>नाथ</i> (nātha), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃neh₂-</i> (“to help, support”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ship, boat</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>नौ</i> (náu), <i>नावा</i> (nāvā́). Cognate with Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀡𑀸𑀯𑀸</i> (ṇāvā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leader, master</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>नायक</i> (nāyaka).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coconut palm</li></ol><p>Of a common origin with Sanskrit <i>नारिकेल</i> (nārikela), ultimately from Proto-Dravidian. Compare Tamil <i>நாரி</i> (nāri, “fibre”) and <i>கெளி</i> (keḷi, “coconut palm”). The tree was not cultivated where the Buddha preached, and the word seems to be a recent loanword in Pali.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hollow stalk</li><li>tube, pipe</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blue</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>नील</i> (nīla).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>नीर</i> (nīra).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>free from disease, healthy, well</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>नीरोग</i> (nīroga).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>led, guided</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>नीत</i> (nīta, “led”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>law, guidance</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>obstacle</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nest</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>नीळ</i> (nīḷá). Doublet of <i>niḍḍha</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dancing girl</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>ab-; away, off from</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;Háwa</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ew</i> (“off, away, down”). Cognate with Avestan <i>𐬀𐬎𐬎𐬀</i> (auua), Latin <i>au-</i> and Sanskrit <i>अव-</i> (ava-).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>light</li><li>appearance</li></ol><p>From <i>obhāsati</i> (“to shine”). Compare with Sanskrit <i>अवभास</i> (avabhāsa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shine, glow</li><li>to speak to</li><li>to speak to inopportunely</li><li>to rail at</li></ol><p><i>ava-</i> +&lrm; <i>bhāsati</i> (“to shine”)</p><p><i>apa-</i> +&lrm; <i>bhāsati</i> (“to speak”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make shine</li></ol><p>Causative of <i>obhāsati</i> (“to shine”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boiled rice</li></ol><p>Cognate with Sanskrit <i>ओदन</i> (odaná).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ending in 'o'.</li></ol><p><i>o</i> (“letter 'o'”) +&lrm; <i>anta</i> (“end”). The -d- is an arbitrary hiatus breaker, whose primary exemplar may be the Indo-European neuter singular ending -d.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>object of meditation which is white</li></ol><p><i>odāta</i> +&lrm; <i>kasiṇa</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>clad in white</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flood</li><li>that which sweeps a man away from emancipation</li><li>torrent</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>ओघ</i> (ogha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plunge or enter into</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strength, sap of life, vitality, nutritive essence, juice</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>ओजस्</i> (ójas).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>folly, vanity</li><li>the letter 'o'</li></ol><p><i>o-</i> +&lrm; <i>kāra</i></p><p>Possibly from Sanskrit <i>ओकार</i> (okāra). By surface analysis, translingual <i>ඔ</i> (o, the letter) +&lrm; <i>kāra</i> (term designating a letter or sound) etc.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>permission</li></ol><p><i>okāsa</i> +&lrm; <i>kamma</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>all broken up, falling to pieces</li></ol><p><i>olugga</i> +&lrm; <i>vi-</i> +&lrm; <i>lugga</i>. The element <i>vilugga</i> is not attested as an independent word.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall to pieces</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अवरुज्यते</i> (avarujyate) with the eastern tendency to change <i>r</i> to <i>l</i> and the usual change of voice from middle to active. By surface analysis, <i>ava-</i> +&lrm; <i>lujjati</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>leading to</li><li>leading to nirvana</li><li>bringing near</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lower</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अवर</i> (avara), a comparative formation whose base survives as <i>ava-</i> or <i>o-</i> (“down”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>on this side</li></ol><p>Superlative formation from <i>ora</i> (“below”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to descend, climb down</li></ol><p>From <i>o-</i> +&lrm; <i>rohati</i>, cognate with Sanskrit <i>अवरोहति</i> (avarohati).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>medicine</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>औषध</i> (auṣadha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>end</li><li>ceasing</li><li>conclusion</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>advice</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>अववाद</i> (avavāda).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lip</li><li>an unknown domesticated animal, possibly a camel</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>ओष्ठ</i> (oṣṭha).</p><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>औष्ट्र</i> (auṣṭra), vṛddhi derivative of <i>उष्ट्र</i> (úṣṭra, “camel, buffalo”). Compare Ardhamagadhi Prakrit <i>𑀉𑀝𑁆𑀝</i> (uṭṭa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mountain</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पर्वत</i> (párvata).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to act like a mountain</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make go out or away, to drive out, to banish</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>variety, cleavage</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>प्रभेद</i> (prabheda).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>light, radiance, shine</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>प्रभा</i> (prabhā́). By surface analysis, <i>pa-</i> +&lrm; <i>bhā</i>. Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀧𑀪𑀸</i> (pabhā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>morning, daybreak</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>प्रभात</i> (prabhāta). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀧𑀳𑀸𑀬</i> (pahāya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shine forth; gleam, glitter</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>प्रभाति</i> (prabhāti).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>might, power, strength</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>प्रभाव</i> (prabhāva). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀧𑀳𑀸𑀯</i> (pahāva), <i>𑀧𑀳𑀸𑀬</i> (pahāya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>awake, awakened, enlightened, roused</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>प्रबुद्ध</i> (prabuddha). By surface analysis, <i>pa-</i> +&lrm; <i>buddha</i>. Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀧𑀩𑀼𑀤𑁆𑀥</i> (pabuddha), <i>𑀧𑀩𑁆𑀩𑀼𑀤𑁆𑀥</i> (pabbuddha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to awaken</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>प्रबुध्यते</i> (prábudhyate). By surface analysis, <i>pa-</i> +&lrm; <i>bujjhati</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shaking, trembling, wavering</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>प्रचल</i> (pracala). The Pali root is <i>cal</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cooking</li></ol><p>From the root <i>pac</i> +&lrm; <i>-ana</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cook</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पचति</i> (pácati); see there for more.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swear back at</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>प्रत्याक्रोशति</i> (pratyākrośati).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to experience, realize</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>प्रत्यनुभवति</i> (pratyanubhavati), from <i>प्रति-</i> (prati-) +&lrm; <i>अनुभवति</i> (anubhavati).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>individual, separate</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>प्रत्यात्मन्</i> (pratyātman), from <i>प्रति-</i> (prati-) +&lrm; <i>आत्मन्</i> (ātman, “soul, self”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enemy, opponent, adversary</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look upon, consider, review, realize, contemplate</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>प्रत्यवेक्षति</i> (pratyavekṣati), from <i>प्रति-</i> (prati-) +&lrm; <i>अव-</i> (ava-) +&lrm; <i>ईक्षति</i> (īkṣati).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cause, motive</li><li>requisite</li><li>means</li><li>support</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) affix</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>each one, single, by oneself, separate</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>प्रत्येक</i> (pratyeka).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>basket</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>latest, hindmost, lowest, western</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पश्चिम</i> (paścima).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>afterwards</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पश्चा</i> (paścā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to descend</li><li>to dismount</li></ol><p><i>paṭi-</i> +&lrm; <i>orohati</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>existent</li><li>present (of time)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>प्रत्युत्पन्न</i> (pratyutpanna).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall back on, return again, to go back to</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>returned； come back； withdrawn</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foot</li><li>footstep</li><li>path, way</li><li>constituent</li><li>word, quarter verse, verse, stanza, sentence</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) ending</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पद</i> (pada, “foot”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lotus</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>पद्म</i> (padma).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>giver, bestower</li><li>distributor</li><li>spendthrift</li></ol><p>Possibly directly as <i>pa-</i> +&lrm; <i>dātar</i>, and possibly indirectly by compounding at the verbal level to give <i>padadāti</i> and <i>padāti</i> (“to bestow”) as <i>pa-</i> +&lrm; <i>dadāti</i>. The prefix intensifies the amount of giving.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>light</li><li>lamp</li></ol><p>Cognate with Epic Sanskrit <i>प्रदीप</i> (pradīpa)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>To ooze, trickle, drip</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Indo-Aryan <i>&#42;pragẓʰárati</i> (“flows, oozes”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A dripping, trickling, oozing</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strike, hit, beat</li></ol><p>From <i>pa</i> +&lrm; <i>harati</i>. Cognate with Sanskrit <i>प्रहरति</i> (praharati).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a present fit to be sent to someone</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blow</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>प्रहार</i> (prahāra).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abandoned</li><li>eliminated</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give up</li><li>to renounce</li><li>to forsake</li></ol><p><i>pa-</i> +&lrm; <i>jahati</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>progeny</li><li>race</li><li>mankind</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>प्रजा</i> (prajā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to know, understand</li><li>to discern, distinguish</li><li>to find out</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Vedic religion,&#32;Hinduism) Prajapati</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>प्रजापति</i> (prajāpati). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀧𑀬𑀸𑀯𑀇</i> (payāvaï). By surface analysis, tatpuruṣa compound of <i>pajā</i> +&lrm; <i>pati</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>done, made</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>original form; origin; natural state</li></ol><p>Sanskrit <i>प्रकृति</i> (prakṛti)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eyelash</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पक्ष्म</i> (pakṣma, “eyelash”). Doublet of <i>pamha</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>side (of the body), flank</li><li>wing</li><li>feathers</li><li>side, faction</li><li>half a lunar month, fortnight</li><li>alternative (in a debate)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>visible, clear</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पक्ष</i> (pakṣá).</p><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>प्रख्य</i> (prakhyá, “clear”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bird; winged one</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>winged</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पक्षिन्</i> (pakṣín). By surface analysis, <i>pakkha</i> +&lrm; <i>-i</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to betray, to deceive</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>grey</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पलित</i> (palita).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>throne; sofa; couch</li><li>cross-legged sitting</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>पर्यङ्क</i> (paryaṅka, “couch, palanquin”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a heap of straw</li></ol><p><i>palāla</i> +&lrm; <i>puñja</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leaf</li><li>foliage</li><li>bastard teak, i.e. <i>Butea monosperma</i>, formerly known as <i>Butea frondosa</i></li><li>alternative form of <i>paḷāsa</i> (“mercilessness”)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>green</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पलाश</i> (palāśa, “leaf, foliage; bastard teak”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>running away, fleeing</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पलायन</i> (palāyana). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀧𑀮𑀸𑀬𑀡</i> (palāyaṇa), <i>𑀧𑀮𑀸𑀡</i> (palāṇa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>running away, taking to flight, fleeing</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पलायिन्</i> (palāyin).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>crushing</li><li>overcoming</li><li>defeating</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crush down</li><li>to destroy</li><li>to overcome</li><li>to defeat</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eyelash</li></ol><p>From Old Indo-Aryan <i>&#42;paṣma</i>, a colloquial form of Sanskrit <i>पक्ष्म</i> (pakṣma, “eyelash”). Doublet of <i>pakhuma</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>greatly delighted</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>joyfully, joyously</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>chief, first, foremost, prominent</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eyebrow</li></ol><p><i>pa</i> (“front”) +&lrm; <i>mukha</i> (“mouth”), literally “in front of the face”; compare Sanskrit <i>प्रमुख</i> (pramukha, “chief, first”), which is formed from etymologically identical components.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carelessness</li><li>indifference</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>प्रमाद</i> (pramā́da).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>measure, size, amount</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>प्रमाण</i> (pramāṇa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>but, yet, on the other hand</li><li>moreover</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jackfruit</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dravidian. Compare Telugu <i>పనస</i> (panasa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>path, road, way</li></ol><p>Full-grade variant of patha, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pent-</i> (“path”). Cognate with Vedic Sanskrit <i>पन्थाः</i> (panthāḥ), English <i>find</i>, and English <i>path</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>obstacle, impediment</li><li>(<i>Theravada&#32;Buddhism</i>) proliferation of thought or reactions, or the expansion of thoughts, reactions, and diffuseness, as an obstacle to spiritual progress</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the lungs</li></ol><p>Obscurely related to Sanskrit <i>पुप्फुस</i> (pupphusa)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a place for supplying water: a shed by the roadside to provide travellers with water; a well or cistern</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>प्रपा</i> (prapā́).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>other, another</li><li>alien</li><li>outsider</li><li>further (on)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पर</i> (para), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;per-</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>somebody else's wife</li></ol><p><i>para</i> +&lrm; <i>dāra</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to commit adultery</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>welfare of others</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superior, best, excellent</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) active ending</li></ol><p>Calque of Sanskrit <i>परस्मैपद</i> (parasmaipada), built as <i>parassa</i> (“for another”) +&lrm; <i>pada</i> (“ending”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>after</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a wandering ascetic (not necessarily Buddhist)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eat</li><li>to clean</li></ol><p>From <i>pari-</i> +&lrm; <i>bhuñjati</i> (“to eat”).</p><p>From <i>pari-</i> +&lrm; <i>bhuñjati</i> (“to purify”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰu-né-g-ti ~ &#42;bʰu-n-g-énti</i> (“to flee”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wailing, lamentation</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to keep up</li><li>to protect</li><li>to carry about</li><li>to avoid</li></ol><p><i>pari-</i> +&lrm; <i>harati</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loss</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>retinue</li></ol><p><i>pari-</i> (“around”) +&lrm; <i>jana</i> (“people”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arrangement, preparation, preliminary action, plastering</li></ol><p><i>pari-</i> (“around”) +&lrm; <i>kamma</i> (“doing”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Buddhism</i>) eight prescribed articles for use by Buddhist monks, i.e., the 3 robes, almsbowl, razor, needle, girdle, and water strainer.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>परिष्कार</i> (pariṣkāra).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rubbing</li><li>crushing</li><li>subduing</li><li>massaging</li></ol><p><i>pari-</i> +&lrm; <i>madd</i> +&lrm; <i>-ana</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set free</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>edge of the road (a place of ambush)</li><li>danger</li><li>obstacle</li></ol><p><i>pari-</i> +&lrm; <i>pantha</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in ambush (as a metaphor from the locative singular form above)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to interrogate</li><li>to inquire</li><li>to question</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(quite) full, fulfilled, complete, finished, satisfied</li><li>complete, perfect, sound, healthy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>परिपूर्ण</i> (paripūrṇa). By surface analysis, <i>pari-</i> +&lrm; <i>puṇṇa</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>completion</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>To drench</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>To cleanse</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>assembly, collection</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trembling, fear</li><li>hesitation</li><li>excitement</li></ol><p><i>paritassati</i> (“to tremble”) +&lrm; <i>-anā</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tremble, to be afraid</li><li>to be excited</li></ol><p>Semantically as though <i>pari-</i> +&lrm; <i>tasati</i>, but the 's' is doubled for some reason. It may be a by-form from a different way of forming the verb, or metathesis of the forerunner Sanskrit <i>त्रसति</i> (trasati).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>round about, around, on every side, everywhere</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>परितस्</i> (parítas). Cognate with Niya Prakrit <i>𐨤𐨪𐨁𐨡𐨅</i> (paride), Maharastri Prakrit <i>परिओ</i> (pario), Sauraseni Prakrit <i>परिदो</i> (parido). By surface analysis, <i>pari-</i> +&lrm; <i>-to</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>safeguard</li><li>(protective) charm</li></ol><p>From <i>pari</i> +&lrm; <i>trā</i>, related to Sanskrit <i>परि-</i> (pari-, “peri-”) + <i>त्रा</i> (trā, “protector”) (from <i>त्रै</i> (trai, “to protect”)).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to live among</li><li>to live under probation</li></ol><p><i>pari-</i> +&lrm; <i>vasati</i> (“to dwell”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having stayed for a period of time</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पर्युष्ट</i> (paryuṣṭa), with reinforcement (if not reformation) from the past participle <i>vuttha</i> of <i>vasati</i> (“to dwell”). Compare Prakrit <i>𑀧𑀭𑀺𑀯𑀼𑀢𑁆𑀣</i> (parivuttha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>search, quest</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>defence</li></ol><p><i>pari-</i> +&lrm; <i>yodha</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to arise</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>order, course, turn</li><li>method</li><li>quality</li><li>synonym</li><li>figurative language</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leader, guide, adviser</li></ol><p><i>pari-</i> (“around”) +&lrm; <i>ni</i> (“to lead”) +&lrm; <i>-a</i> +&lrm; <i>-ka</i>. The elements from which this word is built may be grouped in several ways, e.g. <i>pari</i> +&lrm; <i>nāyaka</i> or derived from <i>pariṇeti</i> as <i>nāyaka</i> is derived from <i>neti</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>invisible</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>परोक्ष</i> (parokṣa), <i>परोऽक्ष</i> (paroʼkṣa), from <i>परस्</i> (paras, “beyond”) + <i>अक्ष</i> (akṣa, “eye”, form of <i>अक्षि</i> (akṣi) in compounds).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dependent on others</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become allayed, to cease, to fade away</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>प्रशाम्यति</i> (praśāmyati). By surface analysis, <i>pa-</i> +&lrm; <i>sammati</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>One who sees</li><li>side, flank</li></ol><p>See <i>passati</i>.</p><p>Possibly inherited from Sanskrit <i>पश्य</i> (paśya) but also possibly a byform of Pali <i>passant</i> (“seeing”).</p><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पार्श्व</i> (pārśva).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>To quiet, to calm down</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to see</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पश्यति</i> (páśyati).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>urine</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>प्रस्रव</i> (prasrava, “trickling forth; gushing; flowing out; urine”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cattle</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पशु</i> (paśu). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀧𑀲𑀼</i> (pasu).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brightness, clearness</li><li>favour, grace</li><li>refreshment, joy, serenity of mind</li><li>faith</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to teach</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be serene, to be tranquil</li><li>to be content, to be satisfied, to be pleased, to be glad</li><li>to be favourable, to be gracious</li><li>to rejoice in, to take pleasure in</li><li>to have faith in, to believe, to be converted</li></ol><p><i>pa-</i> +&lrm; <i>sīdati</i> (“to subside”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall down</li><li>to alight</li><li>to jump</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>पतति</i> (patati).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wing</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>path, road, way</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पथ</i> (patha); see there for more.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>paṭhavī</i></li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>husband</li><li>master</li><li>lord</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पति</i> (pati), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pótis</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>paṭi-</i></li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>genitive/dative singular of <i>pati</i> (“lord”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thoughtful</li></ol><p><i>pati-</i> +&lrm; <i>sata</i> (“mindful”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>past participle of <i>patati</i> (“to fall”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be established</li><li>to stand firmly</li><li>to find a support</li></ol><p>- <i>pati-</i> +&lrm;</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>standing firmly</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>प्रतिष्ठित</i> (prátiṣṭhita).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spur</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leaf</li><li>wing</li><li>feather</li><li>bowl</li><li>alms bowl</li></ol><p>Compare Sanskrit <i>पत्र</i> (patra).</p><p>Compare Sanskrit <i>पात्र</i> (pātra).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wish, desire, request</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>प्रार्थन</i> (prārthana).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arrival</li><li>attainment</li><li>gain</li><li>merit</li><li>a foot soldier (a soldier who fights on foot)</li><li>a pedestrian</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पत्ति</i> (patti, “the act of going, moving, walking”).</p><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पत्ति</i> (patti, “foot-soldier, infantryman”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>on foot, pedestrian</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pedestrian</li><li>foot soldier, infantryman</li></ol><p><i>patti</i> (“on foot”) +&lrm; <i>-ka</i>. The noun is implicit in the adjective, but is recorded separately because of the specialised military meaning.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flag, banner</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पताका</i> (patākā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>excellent, noble</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>प्रवर</i> (pravara).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to arise, to begin, to take place</li><li>to start, to set out</li><li>to roll or flow forwards</li><li>to become, to be, to exist</li><li>to go on, to proceed, to be kept up</li></ol><p><i>pa-</i> +&lrm; <i>vattati</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manifestation, wielding, execution, giving</li><li>happening, incident, news</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>प्रवृत्ति</i> (pravṛtti); by surface analysis, a nominal formation from <i>pa-</i> + <i>vṛt</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>To give</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>entrance</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enter, to go in.</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>talk</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cause to be carried by a stream</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>To offer, present, give</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sit beside, to attend on, to associate with, to serve, to honour</li><li>to visit (<i>for the above purposes</i>)</li></ol><p>Metathesis of very rare <i>pari-</i> +&lrm; <i>-y-</i> +&lrm; <i>upāsati</i>, evidenced only in the absolutive <i>pariyupāsitvā</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>means, instrument</li><li>preparation, undertaking, occupation, exercise, business, action, practice</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to undertake, engage in</li><li>to prepare, to apply, to employ</li></ol><p><i>pa-</i> +&lrm; <i>yojeti</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be applied</li></ol><p>passive of <i>payuñjati</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>harnessed</li><li>employed in</li><li>applicable</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to harness</li><li>to employ, to appoint</li><li>to practice, to behave</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>five</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पञ्च</i> (páñca) ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pénkʷe</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>fifth</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पञ्चम</i> (pañcama).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>fifty-five</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पञ्चपञ्चाशत्</i> (páñcapañcāśat).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>fifth</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>consisting of five parts.</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>question, asking, inquiry</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>प्रश्न</i> (praśna). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀧𑀡𑁆𑀳</i> (paṇha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wisdom</li><li>insight, understanding</li><li>(<i>Buddhism</i>) transcendental wisdom (MWB.)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>प्रज्ञा</i> (prajñā, “wisdom, intelligence”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>advising, assigning, appointing</li></ol><p><i>paññāpeti</i> (“to make known”) +&lrm; <i>-aka</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make a rule, to regulate</li><li>to make known, to declare</li><li>to prepare (something)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>fifty</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पञ्चाशत्</i> (pañcāśát).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dust</li><li>dirt, soil</li></ol><p>Compare Sanskrit <i>पांसु</i> (pāṃsu).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend</li><li>bow down</li><li>worship</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>प्रणमति</i> (praṇamati). By surface analysis, <i>pa-</i> +&lrm; <i>namati</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a small drum or cymbal</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>affection</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>प्रणय</i> (praṇaya). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀧𑀡𑀬</i> (paṇaya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>salutation</li><li>bending</li><li>adoration</li><li>bowing down.</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>brought out, performed</li><li>exalted, excellent</li><li>heaped up</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wise man</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Sanskrit <i>पण्डित</i> (paṇḍita).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pale-yellow, yellowish</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leaf</li><li>feather, quill</li><li>letter, book</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पर्ण</i> (parṇa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>first</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>primary, first</li><li>foremost</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) When qualifying <i>purisa</i> (“person”), it translates as “third”.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>प्रथम</i> (prathama).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>paṭhamapurisa</i> (“third person”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) third person</li></ol><p>Calque of Sanskrit <i>प्रथमपुरुष</i> (prathamapuruṣa, “third person”), compounded as karmadhāraya compound of <i>paṭhama</i> (“first”) +&lrm; <i>purisa</i> (“person”). The uncompounded form also exists.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>earth</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पृथिवी</i> (pṛthivī). Compare Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀧𑀼𑀳𑀯𑀻</i> (puhavī), Sauraseni Prakrit <i>𑀧𑀼𑀠𑀯𑀻</i> (puḍhavī).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>Verbal prefix:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>back (to)</li><li>towards</li><li>against, in opposition to, opposite</li></ol></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>प्रति-</i> (prati-).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>terrible, fearful</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fear, terror, fright</li></ol><p><i>paṭi-</i> +&lrm; <i>bhaya</i> (“fear; danger”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to argue back</li></ol><p><i>paṭi-</i> +&lrm; <i>bhaṇḍati</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>understanding</li></ol><p><i>paṭi-</i> +&lrm; <i>bhāna</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to awake</li></ol><p><i>paṭi-</i> +&lrm; <i>bujjhati</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>warding off</li><li>repulsion</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Buddhism</i>) pratītyasamutpāda, dependent origination</li></ol><p>From <i>paṭicca</i> (“following from, by reason of”) +&lrm; <i>samuppāda</i> (“arising, origination”). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>प्रतीत्यसमुत्पाद</i> (pratītyasamutpāda). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to redress</li><li>to repair</li><li>to make amends for a sin</li><li>to expiate</li></ol><p><i>paṭi-</i> +&lrm; <i>karoti</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>image, figure</li><li>counterpart</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>प्रतिमा</i> (pratimā́). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀧𑀟𑀺𑀫𑀸</i> (paḍimā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start along a path</li><li>to follow a method</li></ol><p><i>paṭi-</i> +&lrm; <i>-pajjati</i> (“to go”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having entered upon a path or course</li><li>going along</li><li>following a method</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to annoy in return, to tease back</li></ol><p><i>paṭi-</i> +&lrm; <i>rosati</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>retirement, seclusion.</li></ol><p><i>paṭi-</i> +&lrm; <i>saṃ-</i> +&lrm; <i>lī</i> +&lrm; <i>-ana</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reincarnation</li><li>metempsychosis, the supposed transmigration at death of the soul of a human being or animal into a new body of the same or a different species.</li><li>conception</li></ol><p>Buddhist application of what was quite possibly inherited from Sanskrit <i>प्रतिसंधि</i> (pratisaṃdhi, “reunion”), but may also have been formed as suggested by surface analysis, <i>paṭi-</i> +&lrm; <i>sandhi</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reincarnation</li><li>conception</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reflect, consider</li></ol><p><i>paṭi-</i> +&lrm; <i>saṃ-</i> +&lrm; <i>cikkhati</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to announce</li></ol><p><i>paṭi-</i> +&lrm; <i>vedeti</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>repression</li></ol><p><i>paṭi-</i> +&lrm; <i>vinaya</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>promise</li><li>vow</li><li>consent</li><li>permission</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>प्रतिज्ञा</i> (pratijñā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strip, slip, riband, tablet, plate, slab</li><li>cloth, bandage, turban</li></ol><p>Cognate with late Sanskrit <i>पट्ट</i> (paṭṭa) of the same meaning.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seaport</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>peyyāla</i></li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>love, affection</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>प्रेमन्</i>&nbsp;m or n (premán). Cognate with Gandhari <i>𐨤𐨿𐨪𐨅𐨨</i> (prema) and Prakrit <i>𑀧𑀺𑀫𑁆𑀫</i> (pimma), 𑀧𑁂𑀫 (pema), 𑀧𑁂𑀫𑁆𑀫 (pĕmma).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sender, employer</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>beautiful, pleasant, amiable, well-behaved</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पेशल</i> (peśala).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a sending</li><li>message</li><li>service</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>messenger</li><li>servant</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ghost</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>प्रेत</i> (preta).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>words omitted, et cetera</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>repetition</li><li>discourse, formula</li><li>abbreviation mark</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phalguna</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>फाल्गुन</i> (phālguna). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀨𑀕𑁆𑀕𑀼𑀡</i> (phagguṇa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(~māsa) the month of Phalguna, corresponding to February-March</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fruit (<i>of a tree</i>)</li><li>result, benefit</li><li>Name of the Pali root <i>phal</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>फल</i> (phala).</p><p>Extracted from <i>phala</i>, <i>phallati</i> and <i>nipphatti</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bear fruit</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tremble</li><li>to throb</li><li>to stir (intransitive)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to agitate</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make agitate</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>harsh</li><li>rough</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>परुष</i> (paruṣá, “variegated, rough”), which irregular aspiration of the initial <i>p</i> akin to that seen in <i>pharasu</i> (“hatchet, axe”) (the latter from Sanskrit <i>परशु</i> (paraśú)).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A joint or knot</li></ol><p>Compare Sanskrit <i>फडु</i> (phaḍu).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scum, foam, froth</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>फेन</i> (phena).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a swelling, a boil, a blister</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>स्फोट</i> (sphoṭa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>touch</li><li>contact</li></ol><p>From the verb <i>phusati</i> (“to touch”); formally identical with a possible gerundive in <i>-tabba</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>speckled</li></ol><p>Derived from phusati (“to spatter”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Pali (Capitalized: Phussa)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(~māsa) the month of Pausha, corresponding to December-January</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>topaz</li></ol><p><i>phussa</i> (“speckled”) +&lrm; <i>rāga</i> (“colour”). Compare Sanskrit <i>पुष्पराग</i> (puṣparāga), which is <i>पुष्प</i> (puṣpa, “topaz, flower”) +&lrm; <i>राग</i> (rāga, “colour”); some mutual influence is to be suspected.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ploughshare</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>फाल</i> (phāla).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rib</li></ol><p>Obscurely related to Sanskrit <i>पर्शु</i> (parśu, “rib”), plausibly via synonymous Pali <i>phāsukā</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>an emphatic particle</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>also, even so</li><li>even</li></ol><p>Enclitic form of <i>api</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cotton</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thirst</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पिपासा</i> (pipāsā́).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crush, grind</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Indo-Aryan <i>&#42;piṣáti</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peys-</i> (“to grind”). Compare Sanskrit <i>पिनष्टि</i> (pinaṣṭi, “to grind”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>demon, goblin</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पिशाच</i> (piśāca). Compare Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀧𑀺𑀲𑀸𑀅</i> (pisāa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>father</li></ol><p>From Vedic Sanskrit <i>पितृ</i> (pitṛ).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bile</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bilious</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>&#42;पित्तिक</i> (&#42;pittika) (compare <i>पैत्तिक</i> (paittika)). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀧𑀺𑀢𑁆𑀢𑀺𑀬</i> (pittiya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a father's sister; an aunt</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पितुःष्वसा</i> (pituḥṣvasā). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀧𑀺𑀉𑀘𑁆𑀘𑀸</i> (piuccā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drink</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>पिबति</i> (píbati). Cognate with Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀧𑀺𑀯𑀇</i> (pivaï).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dear, beloved</li><li>pleasant, agreeable</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>husband</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>प्रिय</i> (priya). The noun is a slightly divorced application of the adjective.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wife</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of a reddish colour, tawny</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पिञ्जर</i> (piñjara).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>reddish-yellow, brown, tawny</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पिङ्गल</i> (piṅgalá).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lump</li><li>lump of food</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a collection of alms．</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crushed seeds, flour</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पिष्ट</i> (piṣṭa, “crushed, ground; flour”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>back</li><li>upper side, top</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>immediately behind</li></ol><p>Reduplication of <i>piṭṭhito</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to float</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>प्लवते</i> (plavate).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pond, lotus pond, artificial pool</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>human</li><li>fit for a person</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>purisa</i></li></ol><p>From <i>purisa</i> (“man”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ancient, old, former</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) man</li></ol><p>Doublet of <i>purisa</i>, comparable to the doublets <i>ayya</i> and <i>ariya</i> (“noble”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feed, nourish</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पोषति</i> (poṣati).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a cloth made of hemp fibre</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पवस्त</i> (pavasta, “garment, covering”) + Middle Indo-Aryan <i>-𑀓-</i> (-ka-).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(~māsa) the month of Bhadrapada, corresponding to August–September</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pus</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>former</li><li>earlier</li><li>eastern</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पूय</i> (pūya).</p><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पूर्व</i> (pūrva).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ancestor</li></ol><p><i>pubba</i> +&lrm; <i>purisa</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>morning</li></ol><p>Derived from weak forms of a compound such as Sanskrit <i>पूर्व</i> (pūrva, “start”) +&lrm; Sanskrit <i>अहन्</i> (ahan, “day”), with regular vowel shortening and metathesis of <i>-nh-</i> in oblique forms, with a change of gender.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>remembrance of one's former state of existence</li></ol><p><i>pubbe</i> +&lrm; <i>nivāsa</i> +&lrm; <i>anussati</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Buddhism</i>) the state of having been a loving couple in a previous life; the state of being a soulmate.</li></ol><p>From <i>pubbe</i> (literally “past; ago; foregoing, previous”) + <i>sannivāsa</i> (literally “sharing the same residence; living together”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tail</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पुच्छ</i> (puccha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ask</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>पृच्छति</i> (pṛcchati), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;pr̥šćáti</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>question, asking</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पृच्छा</i> (pṛcchā). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀧𑀼𑀘𑁆𑀙𑀸</i> (pucchā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>individual</li><li>person</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पुद्गल</i> (pudgala, “body, soul”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>individual</li><li>personal</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>again</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>again and again</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cleanse, purify</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पुनाति</i> (punā́ti, “he purifies”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flower</li><li>menstrual flux</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पुष्प</i> (púṣpa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eastern</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>former, earlier</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a man</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) person</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>&#42;पुरिष</i> (*puriṣa). The forms with vowel /i/ were not part of the attested Sanskrit languages. See Sanskrit <i>पुरुष</i> (puruṣa) for Prakrit cognates exhibiting /i/. Geiger proposes derivation of attested Pali, Prakrit and Sanskrit from *pūrṣa, whence Pali doublet <i>posa</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a person to be trained or converted</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>individual man</li><li>person</li></ol><p><i>purisa</i> (“man”) +&lrm; <i>puggala</i> (“individual”) Cf. Sanskrit <i>पुरुषपुद्गल</i> (puruṣapudgala)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>belonging to the past, former, old, ancient</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पुरातन</i> (purātana). Cognate with Maharastri Prakrit <i>पुराअण</i> (purāaṇa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>old, worn out</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पुराण</i> (purāṇá). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀧𑀼𑀭𑀸𑀡</i> (purāṇa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>paṭhavī</i></li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>paṭhavī</i></li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>son</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पुत्र</i> (putrá), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;putrás</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>children and wife</li></ol><p><i>putta</i> +&lrm; <i>dāra</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>merit</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पुण्य</i> (puṇya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accumulation of merit</li></ol><p><i>puñña</i> +&lrm; <i>abhisanda</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>white lotus</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually in compounds</i>) full</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पूर्ण</i> (pūrṇá).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the night of the full moon</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पूर्णमाः</i> (pūrṇamāḥ).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the night of the full moon</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पूर्णमास</i> (pūrṇámāsa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>paṭhavī</i></li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fed, nourished</li><li>asked</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पुष्ट</i> (pṛṣṭá).</p><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पृष्ट</i> (pṛṣṭá).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eastern</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>प्राचीन</i> (prācī́na). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀧𑀸𑀈𑀡</i> (pāīṇa), <i>𑀧𑀸𑀇𑀡</i> (pāiṇa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foot</li><li>foot of a mountain</li><li>foot of a verse; a verse typically contains four feet.</li><li>a coin worth a quarter of a larger valued one</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Indo-Aryan <i>&#42;pā́ts</i>, from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;pā́ts</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pṓts</i> (“foot”). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>पद्</i> (pád), Avestan <i>𐬞𐬀𐬛</i> (pad), Hittite <i>𒉺𒋫</i> (pa-ta), Ancient Greek <i>πούς</i> (poús), Latin <i>pēs</i>, Tocharian B <i>paiyye</i>, Lithuanian <i>pāda</i> (“sole (foot)”), Old Armenian <i>ոտն</i> (otn), Persian پا (pâ), German <i>Fuß</i>, Old English <i>fōt</i> (whence English <i>foot</i>).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enduring footprint of the Buddha</li></ol><p><i>pāda</i> +&lrm; <i>cetiya</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>pāhuṇa</i> (“guest”)</li><li>alternative form of <i>pāhuṇa</i> (“meal, gift”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>pāhuṇeyya</i> (“worthy of hospitality”)</li></ol><p><i>pāhuna</i> +&lrm; <i>-eyya</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guest</li><li>meal for a guest</li><li>gift</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>worthy of hospitality</li></ol><p><i>pāhuṇa</i> +&lrm; <i>-eyya</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rampart, encircling wall</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>प्राकार</i> (prākāra).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>keeper, guardian, protector</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>पाल</i> (pāla).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a line, row (<i>of text</i>)</li><li>the original text (<i>as opposed to a commentary</i>)</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pali (a middle Indo-Aryan language)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>chief, first, eminent</li></ol><p>A gerund form of <i>pamukha</i> (“first, foremost, chief”), with lengthened initial vowel <i>a</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water</li><li>a beverage, drink</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पानीय</i> (pānīya). Cognate with Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀧𑀸𑀡𑀻𑀅</i> (pāṇīa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>evil, sinful</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a sin, bad deed</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पाप</i> (pāpá), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;pāpás</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>evil</li></ol><p><i>pāpa</i> (“wicked”) +&lrm; <i>-ka</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attainment</li></ol><p>Derived from Sanskrit <i>प्रापण</i> (prāpaṇa) by deretroflection of the suffix of the unattested inherited form in response to the lack of /r/ in the Pali root, so from the root <i>ap</i> +&lrm; <i>-ana</i>. The corresponding Pali verbs are <i>pappoti</i> and <i>pāpuṇāti</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>evil</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पापिन्</i> (pāpin). By surface analysis, <i>pāpa</i> +&lrm; <i>-in</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attainment</li></ol><p><i>pāpuṇāti</i> +&lrm; <i>-ana</i>. This exists beside the historically regular formation from the root, <i>pāpana</i>, but is formed from the present stem rather than the root.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reach</li><li>to attain</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>प्राप्नोति</i> (prāpnoti).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>beyond, across</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>beyond, across</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the other side</li><li>the other bank</li></ol><p>Vrddhied form from <i>para</i> (“other”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gone beyond, passed, crossed</li><li>accomplished, versed</li></ol><p><i>pāra</i> (“beyond”) +&lrm; <i>gū</i> (“gone”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>completeness, perfection</li></ol><p>Abstract noun of <i>parama</i> (“most excellent”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>farther</li></ol><p>Superlative from <i>pāra</i> (“beyond; the other side”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sling, snare, tie, fetter</li><li>spear</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पाश</i> (pāśa).</p><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>प्रास</i> (prāsa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a lofty platform, a terrace, a palace</li></ol><p>From <i>pra-</i> +&lrm; <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>sād</i> (“sit”, root). Parallel to Sanskrit <i>प्रासाद</i> (prāsāda).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rock, stone</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>पाषाण</i> (pāṣāṇa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>early in the morning</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>प्रातर्</i> (prātár).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>morning meal</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fell</li><li>to throw off</li><li>to kill, to destroy</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>pātī</i> (“bowl”)</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to keep watch</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>evident(ly), manifest(ly)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manifestation</li></ol><p><i>pātu-</i> +&lrm; <i>kamma</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bowl, vessel, dish</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rain, rainy season</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>प्रावृष</i> (prāvṛṣa). Cognate with Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀧𑀸𑀉𑀲</i> (pāusa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to proceed, to advance, to go forward</li><li>to set out</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>pāli</i></li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breath</li><li>life</li><li>living being</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>प्राण</i> (prāṇa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>living being</li></ol><p><i>pāṇa</i> (“life”) +&lrm; <i>bhūta</i> (“being”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>pāṇamatipāteti</i> (“to kill a living being”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kill a living being</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>miracle, marvel</li></ol><p>Compare Sanskrit <i>प्रातिहार्य</i> (prātihārya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rules of conduct for Buddhist monks</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>drunk, imbibed</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पीत</i> (pīta).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>joy, delight, pleasure</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>प्रीति</i> (prīti). Cognate with Ashokan Prakrit <i>𐨤𐨿𐨪𐨁𐨟𐨁</i> (priti, Shahbazgarhi), <i>𑀧𑀻𑀢𑀻</i> (pītī, Girnar), <i>𑀧𑀺𑀢𑀺</i> (piti, Kalsi).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bother, bully, distress</li><li>to mistreat, mishandle</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>&#42;पीळयति</i> (*pīḷayati), <i>पीडयति</i> (pīḍayati, “squeezes, presses, torments”), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;pižd-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pisd-</i> (“to press, squeeze”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chair</li></ol><p>Probably from Sanskrit <i>पीठ</i> (pīṭha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to honour</li><li>to respect</li><li>to make an offering with respect</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पूजयति</i> (pūjayati).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>veneration</li><li>homage</li><li>devotional offering</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पूजा</i> (pūjā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>full</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>पूर</i> (pūra).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Pali letter 'r'</li></ol><p>Probably from the pronunciation of a syllable consisting only of the letter.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lake</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a secret, mystery</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>रहस्य</i> (rahasya), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;raȷ́ʰ-</i> (“secret”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>enticing</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dust</li><li>dirt</li><li>pollen</li><li>moral defilement</li><li>impurity</li><li>menstrual flow</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kingship, rule, reign</li><li>kingdom</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>राज्य</i> (rājya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>guarding, to be guarded</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>रक्ष्</i> (rakṣ).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>demon, fiend</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>राक्षस</i> (rākṣasa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to protect</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>रक्षति</i> (rákṣati), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;Hrákšati</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂lékseti</i>. Cognate with Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀭𑀓𑁆𑀔𑀇</i> (rakkhaï).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>protection</li><li>observance</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>रक्षण</i> (rakṣaṇa). By surface analysis, <i>rakkh</i> +&lrm; <i>-ana</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>protection, shelter, safety</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>रक्षा</i> (rakṣā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to play, recreate, to amuse oneself</li><li>to frolic, delight</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>रमति</i> (ramati).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>delightful</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an opening</li><li>cleft</li><li>weak spot, flaw, defect</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>रन्ध्र</i> (randhra). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀭𑀁𑀥</i> (raṃdha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>taste, flavor</li><li>juice</li><li>mercury</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>रस</i> (rása).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>short</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jewel</li><li>treasure</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>रत्न</i> (ratna).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a chariot, a car</li><li>delight, pleasure</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>रथ</i> (rátha), from Proto-Indo-Aryan <i>&#42;Hrátʰas</i>, from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;Hrátʰas</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;Hrót-h₂-os</i>, from <i>&#42;Hret-</i> (“to roll”). Cognate with Latin <i>rota</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>street</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>love, attachment, pleasure, liking for, fondness of</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>रति</i> (ráti, “repose, pleasure”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>night</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>रात्रि</i> (rātri). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀭𑀢𑁆𑀢𑀺</i> (ratti).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>delighting, finding pleasure or delight [in]</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>रञ्जन</i> (rañjana). By surface analysis, <i>rañj</i> (root) +&lrm; <i>-ana</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dye, colour, paint</li><li>stage, theatre, dancing place, playhouse</li></ol><p>Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀭𑀁𑀕</i> (raṃga).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dyer</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>battle</li><li>intoxication, desire</li><li>sin, fault</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>रण</i>&nbsp;n (raṇa, “battle”), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;ránam</i> (“battle”).</p><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>रण</i>&nbsp;m (ráṇa, “pleasure, delight, etc.”), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;ran-</i> (“pleasure, joy”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>country, reign, kingdom</li><li>people in the country, population, inhabitant</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>राष्ट्र</i> (rāṣṭrá).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blank, vacant, empty</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>रिक्त</i> (rikta), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;riktás</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leykʷ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leave</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to please</li><li>to find pleasure in</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crying, weeping</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>रोदन</i> (rodana). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀭𑁄𑀅𑀡</i> (roaṇa), <i>𑀭𑁄𑀯𑀡</i> (rovaṇa). By surface analysis, <i>rud</i> (root) +&lrm; <i>-ana</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cry, weep</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>रोदिति</i> (roditi), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;HrewdH-</i> (“to cry”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>illness, disease</li></ol><p>The Pali root is <i>ruj</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hair (of the body)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>रोमन्</i> (róman). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀭𑁄𑀫</i> (roma).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>locative singular of <i>roma</i> (“hair”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>instrumental singular of <i>roma</i> (“hair”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plant, to sow</li><li>to erect</li><li>to direct towards</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anger</li><li>quarrel</li></ol><p>From the root <i>rus</i>. For the noun, inherited from Sanskrit <i>रोष</i> (roṣa, “anger”). For the verb, see <i>rosati</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enrage</li><li>to displease</li><li>to irritate, exasperate, vex</li><li>to provoke</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>रोषयति</i> (roṣayati).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blood</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cause pain, to hurt</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>रुजति</i> (rujati).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pain; disease</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>रुजा</i> (rujā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tree</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वृक्ष</i> (vṛkṣá), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;wr̥ćšás</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to restrain, to obstruct</li><li>to conceal</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>रुणद्धति</i> (ruṇaddhati, “to restrain”), with generalisation of the weak stem, parallel to Sanskrit <i>रुन्धति</i> (rundhati, “to restrain”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>angry</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>रुष्ट</i> (ruṣṭa, “angry, vexed”). Compare Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀭𑀼𑀝𑁆𑀞</i> (ruṭṭha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>colour; hue; dye</li><li>lust; attachment</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rahula, son of the Buddha.</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>combining form of <i>rājan</i> (“king”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a royal gift</li></ol><p><i>rājan</i> +&lrm; <i>dāya</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>royal messenger, (<i>government</i>) envoy</li></ol><p><i>rājan</i> (“king”) +&lrm; <i>dūta</i> (“messenger”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>king, ruler</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>राजन्</i> (rājan).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the government service</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>king's son, prince</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>राजपुत्र</i> (rājaputrá).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>minister (<i>of a king</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>delight</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nominative singular of <i>rāma</i> (“delight”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow</li><li>to heal</li><li>to be effective</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>form, figure, material form</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>रूप</i> (rūpa). Compare Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀭𑀽𑀅</i> (rūa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Buddhism</i>) subsistently derivative matter, compositively derivative matter</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>silver</li></ol><p>Sanskrit <i>रूप्य</i> (rūpya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>grown</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>रूढ</i> (rūḍhá).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>he, it</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>whole</li><li>all</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>सर्व</i> (sarva).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>everywhere</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>always</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in every respect</li></ol><p><i>sabba</i> +&lrm; <i>-so</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>polite</li><li>honorable</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>सभ्य</i> (sabhya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a hall, assembly room</li><li>a public rest house, hostelry</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>सभा</i> (sabhā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>truth</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>true</li><li>real</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>सत्य</i> (satya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>solemn declaration</li><li>truthfulness</li></ol><p><i>sacca</i> +&lrm; <i>vajja</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>if</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sound, word</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>शब्द</i> (śabda).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>grassy</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>शाद्वल</i> (śādvala).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>believing</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>together</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>faith, confidence</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>श्रद्धा</i> (śraddhā). Cognate with Prakrit <i>सद्धा</i> (saddhā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>trustworthy</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with gods</li></ol><p><i>sa-</i> + <i>deva</i> + <i>-ka</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rich, wealthy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>सधन</i> (sadhana).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>always</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>सदा</i> (sádā), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;sádaH</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a place of happiness; heaven</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>स्वर्ग</i> (svargá), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;suHargás</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>together with</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>सह</i> (saha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gadling</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>co-religionist</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>thousand</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>सहस्र</i> (sahasra), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;saȷ́ʰásram</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sm̥ǵʰéslom</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>companion</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be attached</li><li>to cling</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>silversmith</li></ol><p><i>sajjha</i> +&lrm; <i>kāra</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>study, rehearsal</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>one's own</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Pali roots <i>sak</i> and <i>sakk</i>.</li></ol><p>Extracted from Pali <i>sakkoti</i> and <i>sakkati</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>whole, entire</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>सकल</i> (sakala).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>identity</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having a mole</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thorny (having thorns)</li><li>dangerous</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>सकण्टक</i> (sakaṇṭaka). By surface analysis, <i>sa-</i> +&lrm; <i>kaṇṭaka</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Indra</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>able</li><li>possible</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>शक्र</i> (śakra)</p><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>शक्य</i> (śakya). The Pali root is <i>sak</i> or <i>sakk</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to honour</li></ol><p>From pre-<i>सत्करोति</i> (satkaroti, “to treat with respect”), which in Pali terms is <i>sat</i> (“good”) +&lrm; <i>karoti</i> (“to do”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Sanskrit</li></ol><p>In addition to the derivation as the past participle of <i>sakkaroti</i>, composed <i>sat</i> (“good”) +&lrm; <i>kata</i> (“done”), in the sense of 'Sanskrit' it is mostly also a calque of Sanskrit <i>संस्कृत</i> (saṃskṛta) as <i>sa-</i> +&lrm; <i>kata</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sakkoti</i></li><li>Alternative morphological interpretation of the verb above.</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gravel</li><li>sugar</li><li>granule, grain, crystal</li><li>potsherd</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>seen face to face.</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be able, can</li><li>Alternative interpretation of the verb. See above for forms, meanings and usage.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>शक्नोति</i> (śaknoti), with <i>sakkoti</i> and <i>sakkuṇāti</i> being mostly alternative phonetic developments (assimilation v. anaptyxis), with the gemination of /k/ extended to the anaptyctic form, making the gemination universal in verb forms where it is not assimilated away. The anaptyctic form without change of suffix lingers on as the rare <i>sakkuṇoti</i>. The form <i>sakkati</i> formally corresponds to the passive Sanskrit <i>शक्यते</i> (śakyate), but its use as active Sanskrit <i>शक्यति</i> (śakyati) is also seen in Epic Sanskrit, so the development may be parallel rather than common inheritance.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sakkoti</i></li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sakkoti</i></li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>possible</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>honor</li><li>hospitality</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bird</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>शकुन</i> (śakuna).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moth</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>शलभ</i> (śalabha). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀲𑀮𑀪</i> (salabha), <i>𑀲𑀮𑀳</i> (salaha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dart, javelin</li><li>arrow</li><li>stake, splinter,</li><li>porcupine quill</li><li>porcupine</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>शल्य</i> (śalya, “dart”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>porcupine</li></ol><p><i>salla</i> (“dart”) +&lrm; <i>-ka</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>friendly talk</li></ol><p><i>saṃ-</i> +&lrm; <i>lāpa</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>even, level</li><li>like, equal, the same</li><li>impartial</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>सम</i> (samá).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>united, being in harmony</li></ol><p><i>sam-</i> +&lrm; <i>agga</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>followed by, possessed of</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>endowed with</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>approving</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>brimful</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>time, occasion</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>designation, technical term</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) samana, sramana, a person who has abandoned society and property for religious reasons but does not follow the Vedas</li><li>(<i>religion</i>) ascetic, mendicant, an adherent of similarly austere religious practices in other contexts</li><li>recluse, any person who similarly abandons society</li><li>peace; tranquillity</li><li>extinguishment</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>श्रमण</i> (śramaṇa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the ascetic Gautama (a common appellation of the Buddha)</li></ol><p><i>samaṇa</i> (“ascetic”) + <i>gotama</i> (“Gautama”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>many</li></ol><p><i>saṃ-</i> +&lrm; <i>bahula</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mixing up</li><li>confusion</li></ol><p><i>saṃ-</i> +&lrm; <i>bheda</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enlightenment</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>understood clearly, known perfectly</li><li>understanding thoroughly, enlightened</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Omniscient One</li></ol><p><i>saṃ-</i> +&lrm; <i>buddha</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oyster</li><li>shell</li></ol><p>Compare Sanskrit <i>शम्बूक</i> (śambūka).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>success</li><li>prosperity</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>समृद्धि</i> (sámṛddhi).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>To succeed</li></ol><p><i>saṃ-</i> +&lrm; <i>ijjhati</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vocative</i>) friend, my dear</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to assent</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be appeased, to be calmed</li><li>to cease</li><li>to rest</li><li>to dwell</li><li>to be tired</li><li>to work</li><li>to be satisfactory</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>शाम्यति</i> (śāmyati, “to cease”).</p><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>श्राम्यति</i> (śrāmyati, “to be weary”).</p><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>शम्यति</i> (śamyati, “to toil at”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to agree with</li><li>to delight</li><li>to be friendly with</li><li>to exchange friendly greetings</li></ol><p><i>saṃ-</i> +&lrm; <i>modati</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>confusion</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>properly, rightly, thoroughly</li></ol><p>Related to Sanskrit <i>सम्यक्</i> (samyak, “connected, in one”), a derivative of <i>सम्-</i> (sam-, “with, together”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the fully enlightened one</li></ol><p><i>sammā</i> +&lrm; <i>sambuddha</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>right concentration</li></ol><p><i>sammā</i> +&lrm; <i>samādhi</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>infatuated</li></ol><p><i>sa-</i> +&lrm; <i>moha</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn out, to happen</li><li>to succeed</li><li>to be obtained</li></ol><p><i>saṃ-</i> +&lrm; <i>-pajjati</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>successful, complete</li><li>endowed with, rich</li><li>sweet, delicious</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>future state; the next world</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>success, attainment</li><li>fortune</li><li>excellence, magnificence, beauty</li><li>prosperity, splendour</li></ol><p><i>saṃ-</i> +&lrm; <i>patti</i> (attainment). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>सम्पत्ति</i> (sampatti)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>instigator</li></ol><p><i>saṃ-</i> +&lrm; <i>pavattar</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>associated</li><li>connected</li></ol><p><i>saṃ-</i> +&lrm; <i>payutta</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>concomitant</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>concomitant</li></ol><p><i>sampayutta</i> +&lrm; <i>-ka</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to procure</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>collection</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>समुच्चय</i> (samuccaya). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀲𑀫𑀼𑀘𑁆𑀘𑀬</i> (samuccaya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the ocean, a large body of water</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) a large quantity of something</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>समुद्र</i> (samudrá).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rising</li><li>origination</li><li>cause</li></ol><p><i>saṃ-</i> +&lrm; <i>uṭṭhāna</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>year</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>समा</i> (samā, “year”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>produced by concentration</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take with oneself</li><li>to take upon oneself</li></ol><p><i>saṃ-</i> +&lrm; <i>ādiyati</i> (“to take”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring together, to put together</li><li>to assemble</li><li>to compare</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attainment</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>compound, combination</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>समास</i> (samāsa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>collection, multitude, aggregation</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>समूह</i> (samūha). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀲𑀫𑀽𑀳</i> (samūha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dog</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>श्वन्</i> (śvan).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dancing</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>perpetual</li><li>primeval</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flow</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>स्यन्दते</i> (syandate, “to move fast, to flow”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>union</li><li>junction</li><li>joint</li><li>connection</li><li>agreement</li><li>sandhi</li></ol><p>Derived from Sanskrit <i>सन्धि</i> (sandhi) or <i>संधि</i> (saṃdhi).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>house-breaker</li><li>house-breaking</li><li>one who breaks the cycle of rebirths</li></ol><p><i>sandhi</i> +&lrm; <i>cheda</i>, agent and action nouns of <i>sandhiṃ chindati</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make a break in something</li><li>to break in (to a house etc.)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>visible</li><li>belonging to this life</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sounding</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>To mix</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to assemble</li><li>to come together</li></ol><p><i>saṃ-</i> +&lrm; <i>ni-</i> +&lrm; <i>patati</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sitting down together</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to settle, to subside</li><li>to become quiet</li></ol><p><i>saṃ-</i> +&lrm; <i>nisīdati</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>true</li><li>good</li><li>calmed</li><li>tranquil</li><li>tired, exhausted</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>सन्त्</i> (sant).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to restrain oneself</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spreading</li></ol><p>Related to <i>santharati</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acquaintance, intimacy, friendship</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peace</li><li>tranquillity</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>शान्ति</i> (śānti).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>making peace, making tranquillity</li></ol><p><i>santi</i> +&lrm; <i>-kara</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tranquil</li></ol><p><i>santi</i> +&lrm; <i>indriya</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contentment</li><li>joy, pleasure</li></ol><p><i>saṃ</i> +&lrm; <i>tuṣ</i>. Compare Sanskrit <i>संतोष</i> (saṃtoṣa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be content</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>carrying to the other shore</li></ol><p><i>saṃ-</i> +&lrm; <i>taraṇa</i> with vowel lengthening.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an oath</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swear (<i>an oath</i>)</li><li>to revile</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a co-wife</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>सपत्नी</i> (sapátnī), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;sapátniH</i>. Cognate with Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀲𑀯𑀢𑁆𑀢𑀻</i> (savattī).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>co-wife</li></ol><p><i>sa-</i> +&lrm; <i>pati</i> + <i>adoption of feminine gender</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snake</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>सर्प</i> (sarpá). Cognate with Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀲𑀧𑁆𑀧</i> (sappa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having a cause</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>सप्रत्यय</i> (sapratyaya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snakeling</li></ol><p>Tatpuruṣa compound of <i>sappa</i> +&lrm; <i>potaka</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wise</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dangerous, beset with fear</li></ol><p><i>sa-</i> +&lrm; <i>paṭibhaya</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ghee</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of reed used for arrows</li><li>arrow (originally made of aforementioned reed)</li><li>sound</li><li>vowel</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>शर</i> (śara, “reed, arrow”).</p><p>From Sanskrit <i>स्वर</i> (svara, “sound”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lake</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>सरस्</i> (sáras), from Proto-Indo-Aryan <i>&#42;sáras</i>, from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;sáras</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sélos</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>protection, refuge</li><li>a shelter</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>शरण</i> (śaraṇa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>passion</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>body (of human)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>शरीर</i> (śárīra). Cognate with Niya Prakrit <i>𐨭𐨪𐨁𐨪</i> (śarira), Gandhari <i>𐨭𐨪𐨁𐨪</i> (śarira), Prakrit <i>𑀲𑀭𑀻𑀭</i> (sarīra).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hare</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>शश</i> (śaśa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with an army</li></ol><p><i>sa-</i> (“with”) +&lrm; <i>senā</i> (“army”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corn, crop</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>सस्य</i> (sasya), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)h₁es-</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>including religious teachers and brahmans</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eternal</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>शश्वत्</i> (śaśvat).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a mother-in-law</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>श्वश्रू</i> (śvaśrū́), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;swaćrúH</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sweḱrúh₂</i>. Cognate with Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀲𑀸𑀲𑀽</i> (sāsū), Sauraseni Prakrit <i>𑀲𑀸𑀲𑀼𑀏</i> (sāsue).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a father-in-law</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>श्वशुर</i> (śváśura), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;swáćuras</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swéḱuros</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>hundred</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>शत</i> (śatá), from Proto-Indo-Aryan <i>&#42;śatám</i>, from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;ćatám</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱm̥tóm</i>. Compare Avestan <i>𐬯𐬀𐬙𐬆𐬨</i> (satəm), Latin <i>centum</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>memory, recognition, consciousness</li><li>intentness of mind, wakefulness of mind, lucidity of mind</li><li>mindfulness, alertness</li><li>self-possession, self-consciousness</li></ol><p>From Vedic Sanskrit <i>स्मृति</i> (smṛti), from Proto-Indo-European.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>seven</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>creature, living being</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>able, strong</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>सप्त</i> (saptá), from Proto-Indo-Aryan <i>&#42;saptá</i>, from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;saptá</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;septḿ̥</i>.</p><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>सत्त्व</i> (sattvá).</p><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>शक्त</i> (śakta, “able”). The Pali root is <i>sak</i> or <i>sakk</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>seventh</li><li>superlative degree of <i>sant</i>: best, most excellent</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>सप्तम</i> (saptama). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀲𑀢𑁆𑀢𑀫</i> (sattama).</p><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>सत्तम</i> (sattama).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the seventh day</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) locative case</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) conditional mood</li></ol><p>Absolute use of the feminine of the adjective <i>sattama</i> (“seventh”).</p><p>Semantic loan from Sanskrit <i>सप्तमी</i> (saptamī, literally “seventh”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seven kinds of precious things (<i>collectively</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inflection of <i>satta</i> (“living being”):## locative singular## accusative plural</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>seventh</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sword</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teacher</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>शास्तर्</i> (śāstar).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thigh</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>सक्थि</i> (sakthi).</p></html>:/sɐtti/
<h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ability, strength, power</li><li>spear, javelin, dagger</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>शक्ति</i> (śakti).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enemy</li><li>barley meal</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>शत्रु</i> (śatru). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀲𑀢𑁆𑀢𑀼</i> (sattu).</p><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>सक्तु</i> (saktu). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀲𑀢𑁆𑀢𑀼</i> (sattu).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a period of seven days: week</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>सप्ताह</i> (saptāha). By surface analysis, <i>satta</i> (“seven”) +&lrm; <i>aha</i> (“day”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hearing, listening</li><li>ear</li><li>flowing</li></ol><p>Derived from Sanskrit <i>श्रवण</i> (śravaṇa, “hearing”), but reformed from the consonants surviving in Pali.</p><p>From the root <i>su</i> +&lrm; <i>-ana</i>.</p><p>Derived from Sanskrit <i>स्रवण</i> (sravaṇa, “flowing”), but reformed from the consonants surviving in Pali.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a river</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>स्रवन्ती</i> (srávantī).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of a liquid</i>) to flow, run</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>स्रवति</i> (sravati), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;srew-</i> (“to flow”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>accompanied by reasoning</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sleeping</li><li>bed</li></ol><p><i>sī</i> (sleep) +&lrm; <i>-ana</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go about, to wander</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to joke, to jest</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evening</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>संध्या</i> (saṃdhyā́). Cognate with Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀲𑀁𑀛𑀸</i> (saṃjhā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>saṃyojana</i> (“fetter”)</li></ol><p>From <i>saṃyojana</i> by assimilation of the cluster.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>awareness</li><li>intellect</li><li>sign, gesture</li><li>name</li></ol><p><i>saṃ</i> +&lrm; <i>ñā</i> (“to know”). See Sanskrit <i>सम्-</i> (sam-) + <i>ज्ञा</i> (jñā) for more on the elements.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contact</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nominative singular of <i>saṃsagga</i> (“contact”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doubt</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>संशय</i> (saṃśaya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>transmigration</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a year</li></ol><p>Cognate with Ashokan Prakrit <i>𑀲𑀁𑀯𑀙𑀭</i> (saṃvachara).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>restraint</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>संवर</i> (saṃvara). By surface analysis, <i>var</i> (root) +&lrm; <i>-a</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to restrain</li><li>to shut</li><li>to cover</li></ol><p><i>saṃ-</i> +&lrm; <i>varati</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>covering</li><li>obstruction</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>संवरण</i> (saṃvaraṇa). By surface analysis, <i>saṃ-</i> +&lrm; <i>var</i> +&lrm; <i>-ana</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to divide</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>restrained</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>restraint, self-control, abstinence</li><li>niggardliness</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bond, fetter</li><li>metaphorical bond that ties man to the wheel of transmigration</li></ol><p><i>saṃ-</i> +&lrm; <i>yuj</i> +&lrm; <i>-ana</i>. With assimilation, this yields the alternative form <i>saññojana</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attachment</li><li>clinging</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>multitude, assemblage of people</li><li>sangha</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to collect</li><li>to scrutinise</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intimation, agreement, engagement, appointed place, rendezvous</li><li>hint, signal</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>संकेत</i> (saṃketa, “agreement, hint, signal”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chank</li></ol><p>Compare Sanskrit <i>शङ्ख</i> (śaṅkha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fetter</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>embellished</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>संस्कृत</i> (saṃskṛta), though synchronically derivable by the etymological relationship <i>saṅkhata</i> : <i>kata</i> :: <i>saṅkharoti</i> : <i>karoti</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abridgement</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to collect，heap together</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>concise</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>form of talk, trend of talk</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enumeration</li></ol><p>Derived from Sanskrit <i>सङ्ख्या</i> (saṅkhyā). The form without -y- is the expected inherited form; the form with -y- appears to be a Sanskitisation.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doubt, uncertainty, fear</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Sanskrit <i>शङ्का</i> (śaṅkā, “doubt”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of hemp</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>शण</i> (śaṇá).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>smooth</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>loose</li></ol><p>From Old Indo-Aryan <i>&#42;śṛthilá</i>. Compare Sanskrit <i>शिथिर</i> (śithirá), <i>शिथिल</i> (śithilá), Prakrit <i>𑀲𑀠𑀺𑀮</i> (saḍhila), <i>𑀲𑀺𑀠𑀺𑀮</i> (siḍhila). Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱr̥th₂-ró-s</i>. Also compare <i>sithila</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sweat</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>स्वेद</i> (sveda, “sweat”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cause to sweat; to heat</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>स्वेदयति</i> (svedayati). Synchronically analysed as the causative of <i>sijjati</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sprinkling</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>garland for the crest．</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>phlegm</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eagle, hawk or falcon</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>श्येन</i> (śyena, “falcon, eagle”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>army</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>सेना</i> (senā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sleeping place</li><li>lodging place</li></ol><p><i>sayana</i> (“sleeping (place)”) +&lrm; <i>āsana</i> (“sitting (place)”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>regulator of lodging-places</li></ol><p><i>senāsana</i> +&lrm; <i>paññāpaka</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inflection of <i>serin</i> (“independent”):## vocative singular masculine## nominative/vocative singular neuter</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>independent</li><li>free of obligation</li><li>of one's own free will</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>स्वैरिन्</i> (svairin, “going where one likes, free, independent, unrestrained”), from <i>स्वैर</i> (svaira​, “going where one likes”) +&lrm; <i>-इन्</i> (-in, “doer”), the former from <i>स्व</i> (sva, “self”) +&lrm; <i>ईर</i> (īra, “driving, chasing”).​</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inflection of <i>serin</i> (“independent”):## nominative singular masculine## nominative/vocative/accusative plural masculine/neuter</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the remainder</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>remaining</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>शेष</i> (śeṣa, “remainder”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>white</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>श्वेत</i> (śveta, “white”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sleep</li><li>to lie down</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>शेते</i> (śete), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱey-</i> (“to lie down”). The forms <i>शयते</i> (śayate) and <i>शयति</i> (śayati) are found in Vedic and Epic Sanskrit.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ridge, embankment, causeway</li><li>bridge</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>सेतु</i> (setu).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>indulge in, pursue</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>just as</li></ol><p>From <i>seyyathā</i> (“just as, as”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>best, excellent</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>श्रेष्ठ</i> (śréṣṭha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sew, stitch</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>सीव्यति</i> (sī́vyati). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀲𑀺𑀯𑁆𑀯𑀇</i> (sivvaï).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be sprinkled</li></ol><p>Passive verb from <i>sic</i> (“to sprinkle”, root).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>accomplished, completed</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>success</li><li>magician</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>सिद्ध</i> (siddha). The noun derives from the adjective, which is in origin a past participle.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accomplishment</li><li>success</li></ol><p>Cognate with Sanskrit <i>सिद्धि</i> (siddhi)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nominative singular masculine of <i>siddha</i> (“accomplished”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jackal</li></ol><p>Literally "howling," from Proto-Indo-Iranian. Or from Proto-Sino-Tibetan <i>&#42;s-k-jwal</i> (“wild canine”). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>सृगाल</i> (sṛgāla).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sweat</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>स्विद्यति</i> (svidyati), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sweyd-</i> (“to sweat”). Cognate with Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀲𑀺𑀚𑁆𑀚𑀇</i> (sijjaï).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Buddhism</i>) a young woman undergoing training to be a nun</li></ol><p>Feminine of <i>sikkhamāna</i> (“learning, undergoing training”, present middle participle), from Sanskrit <i>शिक्षमाण</i> (śikṣamāṇa). Compare Chinese <i>式叉摩那</i> (shìchàmónà), borrowed directly from Sanskrit.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to learn</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>शिक्षति</i> (śikṣati), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;ćikš-</i> (“to learn”). Cognate with Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀲𑀺𑀓𑁆𑀔𑀇</i> (sikkhaï).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>study, discipline</li><li>(<i>as one of the six Vedāngas</i>) phonetics</li></ol><p>From Vedic Sanskrit <i>शिक्षा</i> (śikṣā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a set of precepts, a code; a precept</li></ol><p><i>sikkhā</i> +&lrm; <i>pada</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a string</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>शिक्य</i> (śikyá).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fame</li><li>verse, stanza</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>श्लोक</i> (śloka).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>optative active third-person singular of <i>sinoti</i> (“to bind”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>covered in sweat, wet with perspiration</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>स्विन्न</i> (svinna).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bind</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>&#42;सिनोति</i> (&#42;sinoti), attested in the form of असिनोत् (asinot, imperfect).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bathed, bathing</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>स्नात</i> (snāta). Cognate with Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀡𑁆𑀳𑀸𑀅</i> (ṇhāa), Sauraseni Prakrit <i>𑀡𑁆𑀳𑀸𑀤</i> (ṇhāda). Doublet of <i>nahāta</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bathe</li><li>alternative form of <i>sinoti</i> (“to bind”)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>सिनाति</i> (sināti).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>art, branch of knowledge, craft</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>शिल्प</i> (śilpa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a branch of knowledge； a craft</li></ol><p><i>sippa</i> +&lrm; <i>ṭhāna</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>head</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>luck, glory, wealth, splendour</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>श्री</i> (śrī).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) a blood vessel, vein, nerve, tendon</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>सिरा</i> (sirā́). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀲𑀺𑀭𑀸</i> (sirā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smile</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>white</li><li>past participle of <i>sinoti</i> (“to bind”)</li><li>clinging to, devoted to</li><li>resting upon</li><li>sharp</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>स्मित</i> (smita, “smile”).</p><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>सित</i> (sita, “white”).</p><p>From the root <i>si</i> (“to bind”) +&lrm; <i>-ta</i></p><p>From the root <i>si</i> (“to rest on”) +&lrm; <i>-ta</i></p><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>शित</i> (śita, “whetted”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>loose</li><li>lax</li><li>yielding</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>शिथिल, शिथिर</i> (śithila, śithira).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>looseness</li></ol><p><i>sithila</i> +&lrm; <i>-tta</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sprinkled</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>सिक्त</i> (siktá).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a lump of boiled rice</li><li>beeswax</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>सिक्थ</i> (siktha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>watering</li><li>one who waters</li></ol><p>From <i>siñcati</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sprinkle</li></ol><p>Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀲𑀺𑀁𑀘𑀇</i> (siṃcaï).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) horn (hard growth of keratin on certain animals' heads)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>शृङ्ग</i> (śṛ́ṅga). Cognate with Prakrit <i>सिंग</i> / <i>𑀲𑀺𑀁𑀕</i> (siṃga).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>siṅghāṇikā</i> (“nasal mucus”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mucus of the nose, nose dirt, snot</li></ol><p>Probably from Sanskrit <i>शिङ्घाणिका</i> (śiṅghāṇikā), ultimately derived from the root of siṅghati (“to sniff, to smell”), but Sanskrit exhibits many variations of form - ś/s/śr, gh/h/kh and -aka v. -ikā, and it is possible that folk etymology has affected this word.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ginger</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dravidian. Compare Tamil <i>இஞ்சிவேர்</i> (iñcivēr), இஞ்சி (iñci).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gold</li><li>ginger</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dravidian. Compare Tamil <i>இஞ்சிவேர்</i> (iñcivēr), இஞ்சி (iñci).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>left over</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>शिष्ट</i> (śiṣṭa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>affection, love</li><li>oil, fat</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>he, it</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shine</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>शोभति</i> (śobhati).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grieve</li><li>to mourn</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>शोचति</i> (śocati).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grief, sorrow, mourning</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>शोक</i> (śoka).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sorrowful</li></ol><p>From <i>soka</i> +&lrm; <i>-in</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gladness</li><li>delight</li><li>happiness</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>joyful</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>helpful</li><li>useful</li></ol><p><i>sa-</i> +&lrm; <i>upakāra</i> (“support”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sleep</li><li>dream</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>स्वप्न</i> (svápna), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swépnos</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gentleness</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ear</li><li>stream</li><li>current</li><li>flood</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>श्रोत्र</i> (śrotra).</p><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>स्रोतस्</i> (srotas), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;sráwtas</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;srew-</i> (“to flow”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stream</li><li>current</li><li>flood</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>well-being, safety, happiness</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>स्वस्ति</i>&nbsp;f or n (svasti). The neuter gender is represented only by the isolated case form <i>sotthinā</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>safely</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>स्वस्तिना</i> (svastinā), instrumental singular of <i>स्वस्ति</i> (svasti), which also yields the feminine noun Pali <i>sotthi</i> (“safety”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blessing, welfare</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>sixteen</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>षोडश</i> (ṣóḍaśa), from Proto-Indo-Aryan <i>&#42;swáẓḍaśa</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a dog</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>श्वन्</i> (śvan). Compare also <i>श्वान</i> (śvāna).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>good, well</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>सु-</i> (su-).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lucky, fortunate</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>सुभग</i> (subhaga). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀲𑀼𑀪𑀕</i> (subhaga), <i>𑀲𑀼𑀳𑀬</i> (suhaya). By surface analysis, <i>su-</i> +&lrm; <i>bhaga</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pure, clean, white</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>शुचि</i> (śuci).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>restrained, exercising self-restraint</li></ol><p><i>su</i> (“well”) +&lrm; <i>danta</i> (“trained”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a person of the Sudra caste</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>शूद्र</i> (śūdra).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pure of heart</li><li>mere, nothing but</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fragrance, good odour</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fragrant</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>सुगन्ध</i> (sugandha). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀲𑀼𑀅𑀁𑀥</i> (suaṃdha). By surface analysis, <i>su-</i> +&lrm; <i>gandha</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>faring well</li><li>happy</li></ol><p><i>su-</i> +&lrm; <i>gata</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become clean or pure</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>शुध्यति</i> (śúdhyati, “to be cleansed”), from the root&nbsp;<i>शुध्</i> (śudh).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parrot</li></ol><p>Compare Sanskrit <i>शुक</i> (śuka).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>easy</li><li>agreeable, pleasant</li><li>happy</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>happiness, comfort</li></ol><p>Apparently inherited from Sanskrit <i>सुख</i> (sukha), but the Sanskrit word itself (q.v.) is strongly suspected to itself be derived from Prakrit.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>deemed a pleasure</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>happy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>सुखिन्</i> (sukhin). By surface analysis, <i>sukha</i> +&lrm; <i>-in</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>happy</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>white</li><li>bright</li><li>pure</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the planet Venus</li><li>semen</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>शुक्र</i> (śukra, “white, bright”), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;ćukrás</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱewk-</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>waxing fortnight (i.e. from new moon to full moon)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>sukkapakkha</i> (“waxing fortnight”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intelligent</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dog</li></ol><p>Compare Sanskrit <i>शुनक</i> (śunaka, “dog”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>well prepared or polished</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>entered upon the right path</li></ol><p><i>su-</i> +&lrm; <i>paṭipanna</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dream</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>स्वप्न</i> (svápna), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swépnos</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very pleasing or enjoyable</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>name of a palace</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>सुरम्य</i> (suramya). By surface analysis, <i>su-</i> +&lrm; <i>ramma</i>. Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀲𑀼𑀭𑀫𑁆𑀫</i> (suramma).</p></html>:/sʊɻɪjɐ/
<h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sun</li><li>the sun as a god</li></ol><p>From Vedic Sanskrit <i>सूर्य</i> (sūrya, “sun”). Also see Sanskrit <i>स्वर्</i> (svar, “heaven, light”). Compare Latin <i>sōl</i>, Ancient Greek <i>ἥλιος</i> (hḗlios).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>distilled liquor</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>सुरा</i> (súrā), from Proto-Indo-Aryan <i>&#42;súraH</i>, from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;súraH</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fermented drink that causes heedlessness</li><li>intoxicating drinks and drugs which lead to carelessness</li></ol><p><i>surā</i> (“a class of liquor”) +&lrm; <i>meraya</i> (“a class of liquor”) +&lrm; <i>majja</i> (“intoxicant”) +&lrm; <i>pamāda</i> (“heedlessness”) +&lrm; <i>ṭhāna</i> (“state”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to listen</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cemetery</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>श्मशान</i> (śmaśāna). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀫𑀲𑀸𑀡</i> (masāṇa), <i>𑀲𑀼𑀲𑀸𑀡</i> (susāṇa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>learned, taught</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sacred lore</li><li>learning</li><li>son</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>श्रुत</i> (śruta).</p><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>सुत</i> (suta, “son”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>learned catechesis</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>सुतमाम्</i> (sutamām), consisting of सुत and माम्.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>learned (in religious lore)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hearing</li><li>tradition</li><li>inspiration</li><li>knowledge of the Vedas</li><li>rumor: <i>sutivasena</i> by hearsay, as a story, through tradition, etc.</li><li>a sound, tone</li></ol><p>Likely from Sanskrit <i>श्रुति</i> (śruti, “hearing”), as opposed to the traditionally-suggested <i>स्मृति</i> (smṛti, “reminiscence”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>easy to endure</li></ol><p>From <i>su-</i> +&lrm; <i>titikkhā</i> (“endurance”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thread, string</li><li>sutra</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>asleep</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>सूत्र</i> (sū́tra).</p><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>सुप्त</i> (suptá).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>discourse (especially one of several parts of the Tipitaka)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>good saying</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>सूक्ति</i> (sūkti).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>daughter</li></ol><p>Feminine of <i>suta</i> (“son”).</p><p>Forms of this word <i>suta</i> are also included under this heading.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gold</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>beautiful</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>सुवर्ण</i> (suvarṇa, “gold”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>land of gold; a name for a country in Southeast Asia.</li></ol><p><i>suvaṇṇa</i> +&lrm; <i>bhūmi</i>. Compare with Sanskrit <i>सुवर्णभूमि</i> (suvarṇabhūmi).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goldsmith</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>सुवर्णकार</i> (suvarṇakāra). By surface analysis, <i>suvaṇṇa</i> +&lrm; <i>-kāra</i>. Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀲𑀼𑀯𑀡𑁆𑀡𑀆𑀭</i> (suvaṇṇaāra), <i>𑀲𑀼𑀡𑁆𑀡𑀆𑀭</i> (suṇṇaāra), <i>𑀲𑀼𑀡𑁆𑀡𑀸𑀭</i> (suṇṇāra).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>sāmī</i> (“master”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dog</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>श्वन्</i> (śvan). Cognate with Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀲𑀼𑀯𑀸𑀡</i> (suvāṇa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>empty</li><li>void</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>शून्य</i> (śūnyá).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crocodile</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>daughter-in-law</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>स्नुषा</i> (snuṣā́), from Proto-Indo-Aryan <i>&#42;snuṣáH</i>, from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;snušáH</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;snusós</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hear</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>शृणोति</i> (śṛṇoti, “to hear”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>welcome</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>well-preached</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Siam (an ancient kingdom in Southeast Asia, approximately coextensive with modern Thailand)</li></ol><p>Compound of a name of unknown origin, yielding English <i>Siam</i>, and a classificatory second element <i>raṭṭha</i> (“kingdom”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>effecting, accomplishing</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>proof</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>well</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>साधु</i> (sādhu, “well, proper”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ocean</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>सागर</i> (sāgara). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀲𑀸𑀬𑀭</i> (sāyara), <i>𑀲𑀸𑀕𑀭</i> (sāgara).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>branch</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>शाखा</i> (śākhā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rice</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>शालि</i> (śāli). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀲𑀸𑀮𑀺</i> (sāli).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hall (<i>covered and enclosed</i>), large room, house</li><li>outhouse</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>शाला</i> (śālā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>novice monk</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>श्रामणेर</i> (śrāmaṇera).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>owner</li><li>master</li><li>lord</li><li>husband</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>स्वामिन्</i> (svāmín).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a member of an assembly</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>सामाजिक</i> (sāmājika).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>propriety</li><li>respectful duty</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>following the proper course</li></ol><p><i>sāmīci</i> (“propriety”) +&lrm; <i>paṭipanna</i> (“following a route”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>charioteer</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>सारथि</i> (sārathi, “charioteer”), formed from Sanskrit <i>सरथ</i> (saratha, “with a chariot”), itself <i>स-</i> (sa-, “with”) +&lrm; <i>रथ</i> (ratha, “chariot”), thus synchronously visible as a derivative of <i>ratha</i> (“chariot”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>impassioned</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teaching</li><li>order</li><li>message</li><li>doctrine</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>शासन</i> (śāsana, “teaching, instruction, rule, order”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dangerous, suspicious</li></ol><p><i>sa-</i> +&lrm; <i>āsaṅkā</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lethargic</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>holy disciple</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>श्रावक</i> (śrāvaka).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Savatthi, also known as Shravasti, the one-time capital of the kingdom of Kosala.</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(~māsa) the month of Shraavana, corresponding to July-August</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tell, to announce</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>This word appears to exist only in dictionaries, where it was claimed to be masculine. An alternative citation form for this word is <i>sāyo</i>. For the attested word, see <i>sāyaṃ</i> (“at night”).</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in the evening</li><li>at night</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>सायम्</i> (sāyam, “in the evening”), ultimately formed from the absolutive of सा (sā, root), cognate with Vedic <i>सायम्</i> (sāyam). From this was back-formed Sanskrit <i>साय</i>&nbsp;n (sāya, “evening”), and the original Pali lexicographers similarly backformed Pali *<i>sāya</i>&nbsp;m (“evening”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sink, to subside, to settle</li><li>to be dejected, to be despondent, to yield, to give way</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>सीदति</i> (sīdati, “to sit”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lion</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>सिंह</i> (siṃha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Sinhalese</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>सिंहल</i> (siṃhala).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nature; habit</li><li>moral practice; code of morality</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>शील</i> (śīla).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>all that belongs to moral practices</li></ol><p><i>sīla</i> +&lrm; <i>khandha</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moral transgression．</li></ol><p><i>sīla</i> +&lrm; <i>vipatti</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boundary; limit</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>सीमा</i> (sīmā), from &#42;sh₂ey-.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>head</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>शीर्ष</i> (śīrṣá).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cold, cool</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>शीत</i> (śītá).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cold, cool</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>शीतल</i> (śītala).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cool water</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>furrow (trench cut in the soil)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>सीता</i> (sītā).</p><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>शीता</i> (śītā́).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>needle</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cook</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pig</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>सूकर</i> (sūkara).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>valiant; courageous</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sun</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>शूर</i> (śūra).</p><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>सूर</i> (sūra).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wise</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>demonstrative</i>) that</li><li>that one</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>he, it, that one</li><li>it</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>स</i> (sa), <i>तद्</i> (tad, “this, that”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skin</li><li>bark</li><li>hide</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to do carpentry, woodwork</li><li>to square, frame</li></ol><p>Probably denominated from <i>taccha</i>. Compare Sanskrit <i>तक्षन्</i> (takṣan, “carpenter”), Ancient Greek <i>τέκτων</i> (téktōn), Latin <i>texo</i> (“weave”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>buttermilk</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>तक्र</i> (takra, “buttermilk”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Taxila (a city in Rawalpindi district, Punjab, Pakistan)</li><li>Taxila (an ancient city)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>तक्षशिला</i> (takṣaśilā, “City of Cut Stone”). Compare Gandhari <i>𐨟𐨐𐨿‫𐨮𐨭𐨁𐨫</i> (tak‫ṣaśila‬).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>young</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>तरुण</i> (taruṇa), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;terunos</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>betel, betel leaf</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>ताम्बूल</i> (tāmbūla).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thread, cord, string, wire</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>तन्तु</i> (tantu). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀢𑀁𑀢𑀼</i> (taṃtu).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Pali root tar</li></ol><p>Part of or extracted from the verb <i>tarati</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cross over</li><li>to swim</li><li>to float</li><li>to hurry</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>तरति</i> (tarati). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀢𑀭𑀇</i> (taraï).</p><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>त्वरते</i> (tvarate). By an irregular process known as samprasarana, the root form 'tvar' is reduced to 'tur' in the past participle and aorist middle and in the Sanskrit by-form <i>तुरति</i> (turati).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crossing over, traversing</li></ol><p><i>tar</i> +&lrm; <i>-ana</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nominative singular of <i>tara</i> (“the Pali root tar”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tree</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>तरु</i> (taru).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>young</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>तरुण</i> (taruṇa), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;tárunas</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;terunos</i>. See also <i>taluṇa</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>movable</li><li>trembling</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tremble, to be afraid</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>त्रसति</i> (trasati, “to tremble”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>therefore</li></ol><p>Case form of demonstrative stem <i>ta</i> (“that”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>demonstrative</i>) to him, to it, to that</li><li>(<i>demonstrative</i>) his, its, of that</li><li>therefore</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>so, thus</li><li>in this way</li><li>likewise</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>तथा</i> (tathā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Buddhism</i>) tathagata</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>such</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>third</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>तृतीय</i> (tṛtīya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>for the third time</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>there</li><li>This word serves as a locative singular of <i>ta</i></li><li>then, for the time being</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Sanskrit <i>तत्र</i> (tátra).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hot, heated, glowing</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>truth</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>तप्त</i> (taptá). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀢𑀢𑁆𑀢</i> (tatta).</p><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>तत्व</i> (tatva). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀢𑀢𑁆𑀢</i> (tatta).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>there</li><li>This word serves as a locative singular of <i>ta</i></li><li>then, for the time being</li></ol><p>Like Sanskrit <i>तत्र</i> (tatra), from <i>ta</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tód</i>. Cognate with Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀢𑀢𑁆𑀣</i> (tattha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>due to that</li></ol><p><i>tato</i> (from that) +&lrm; <i>assa</i> (for this)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>triad</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>there</li><li>therefore</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>taṃyathā</i> (“as follows”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>this is how, thus, as follows</li></ol><p>Calque of Sanskrit <i>तद्यथा</i> (tadyathā), interpretable as <i>taṃ</i> (“that one”) +&lrm; <i>yathā</i> (“as”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Buddhism</i>) thirst, desire, craving, identified as the principal cause of dukkha</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>तृष्णा</i> (tṛ́ṣṇā, “thirst, desire”), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;tŕ̥šnas</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ters-</i> (“dry”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Interpretation of many of the inflectional forms of <i>tejas</i> (“brilliance”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flame, heat, fire</li><li>light, brilliance, splendour</li><li>majesty, prestige, fame</li><li>influence, efficacy, power</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>तेजस्</i> (téjas). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀢𑁂𑀅</i> (tea).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oil (<i>particularly from sesame</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>तैल</i> (tailá), itself from Sanskrit <i>तिल</i> (tila), cognate with Pali <i>tila</i> (“sesame”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wet, to moisten</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>there</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>thirteen</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>त्रयोदश</i> (trayodaśa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>thirteenth</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>त्रयोदशम</i> (trayodaśama).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>thirty-three</li></ol><p>From early Middle-Indo-Aryan <i>&#42;tayattiṃsa</i>, from Sanskrit <i>त्रयस्त्रिंशत्</i> (tráyastriṃśat).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>possessing three superhuman knowledges</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hard, rigid, firm</li><li>stubborn, callous, selfish</li><li>slow</li></ol><p>Cognate with Sanskrit <i>स्तब्ध</i> (stabdha, “fixed, firm”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>land</li><li>dry ground</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the breast of a woman</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>स्तन</i> (stana).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>builder, master carpenter</li><li>overseer, officer</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>स्थपति</i> (sthapati, “master of building”); compare <i>स्था</i> (sthā, “to build, establish”) and <i>पति</i> (pati, “master, lord”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Name of the Pali root <i>thar</i></li></ol><p>Extracted from reflexes of formations from Sanskrit <i>स्‌तॄ</i> (s‌tṝ), such as <i>tharaṇa</i> and <i>pattharati</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spreading, strewing</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>स्तरण</i> (staraṇa, “spreading”). By surface analysis, <i>thar</i> (root) +&lrm; <i>-ana</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elder, senior</li><li>a monk who has spent ten (10) years from his upasampadā</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>old, elder</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>स्थविर</i> (sthavira).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Theravada</li></ol><p><i>thera</i> (“elder”) +&lrm; <i>vāda</i> (“doctrine”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>reliable</li><li>trustworthy</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>firm</li><li>solid</li><li>lasting</li></ol><p>Compare Sanskrit <i>स्थिर</i> (sthira).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>small, little</li><li>short</li><li>unimportant, insignificant</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a trifle</li></ol><p>Compare Sanskrit <i>स्तोक</i> (stoka).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>thūla</i></li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chaff</li><li>husk of grain</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>तुष</i> (tuṣa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>praise, commendation, adulation</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>स्तुति</i> (stutí). Cognate with Sauraseni Prakrit <i>𑀣𑀼𑀤𑀺</i> (thudi), Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀣𑀼𑀇</i> (thui), Gandhari <i>𐨯𐨿𐨟𐨂𐨡𐨁</i> (studi).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dish</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>immovable</li><li>long-lasting</li></ol><p>Derived from Sanskrit <i>स्थावर</i> (sthāvara).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woman</li></ol><p>Doublet of <i>itthī</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unwieldiness, impalpability</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>big, bulky, strong, stout</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>स्थूल</i> (sthūlá). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀣𑀽𑀮</i> (thūla), <i>𑀣𑀼𑀮𑁆𑀮</i> (thulla).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tope</li><li>pagoda</li><li>cairn</li><li>a monument erected over the ashes of a holy person</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>स्तूप</i> (stūpa). See also Old Chinese 塔 (<i>OC *tʰuːb</i>, “tower, pagoda”), which was borrowed from a Middle Indo-Aryan relative of the Pali.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a pillar, prop, support</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>स्थूणा</i> (sthū́ṇā), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;stʰuHnáH</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>three</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>त्रि</i> (tri).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>triad</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>consisting of three (things)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sesame seed</li><li>sesame</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Buddhism</i>) the three worlds in Buddhist cosmology</li></ol><p>Derived from Sanskrit <i>त्रैलोक्य</i> (trailokya)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wet</li><li>moist</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sharp</li><li>piercing</li><li>acute</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lead (metal)</li><li>tin</li><li>tin plate</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lead (metal)</li><li>tin</li><li>tin plate</li><li>many kinds of vines in Convolvulaceae family.</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pumpkin</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>animal, beast</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>तिरश्च</i> (tiraśćá, “crosswise, horizontal”), in reference to animals' walking on all fours rather than erect and upright. Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀢𑀺𑀭𑀺𑀘𑁆𑀙</i> (tiriccha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>concealment, disappearance</li></ol><p><i>tiro-</i> +&lrm; <i>bhāva</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cover, lid, screen</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>further side of a mountain or rock</li></ol><p><i>tiro-</i> +&lrm; <i>pabbata</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The three refuges, i.e. the Buddha, the dharma and sangha taken collectively</li></ol><p><i>ti</i> (“three”) +&lrm; <i>saraṇa</i> (“refuge”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lunar day</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>तिथि</i> (tithi).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endurance</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>तितिक्षा</i> (titikṣā). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀢𑀺𑀢𑀺𑀓𑁆𑀔𑀸</i> (titikkhā), <i>𑀢𑀺𑀇𑀓𑁆𑀔𑀸</i> (tiikkhā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>satisfaction, contentment</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>तृप्ति</i> (tṛpti). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀢𑀺𑀢𑁆𑀢𑀺</i> (titti), <i>𑀢𑀢𑁆𑀢𑀺</i> (tatti).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>partridge</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>thirty</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>thirty</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grass</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>तृण</i> (tṛṇa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spear, lance (for driving elephants)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>joy, satisfaction</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>तोष</i> (toṣa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to weigh, to compare, to measure</li><li>to match, to equal</li></ol><p>Denominative from <i>tulā</i> (“balancing pole”). Compare however Sanskrit <i>तुलयति</i> (tulayati).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>tulya</i> (“to be weighed”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>matched, equal, comparable</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grammatical agreement</li></ol><p><i>tulya</i> (“equal”) +&lrm; <i>adhikaraṇa</i> (“relationship”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beam or pole for balancing and carrying or supporting a load</li><li>rafter</li><li>scales, balance</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>तुला</i> (tulā, “balance, weight”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>An abstraction of the forms of <i>tarati</i> (“to hurry”) in which the stem appears as 'tur'.</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hastening, hurried, eager, swift</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>hastily, quickly</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be satisfied, to be happy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>तुष्यति</i> (tuṣyati). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀢𑀼𑀲𑁆𑀲𑀇</i> (tussaï), <i>𑀢𑀽𑀲𑀇</i> (tūsaï).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>silently</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>तूष्णीम्</i> (tūṣṇīm).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>silent</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>satisfied</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>तुष्ट</i> (tuṣṭa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enjoyment, pleasure</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>तुष्टि</i> (tuṣṭi).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>thou, you</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>त्वम्</i> (tvam).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>demonstrative</i>) that</li><li>they, them</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>palm (<i>Borassus flabellifer</i>)</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>ताल</i> (tāla). There is no reason to believe this word comes from 'Sanskrit' in the normal sense. Magadhi Prakrit exhibits both <i>𑀢𑀸𑀮</i> (tāla) and <i>𑀢𑀸𑀟</i> (tāḍa), so Sanskrit has probably borrowed this word from some Prakrit, possibly even Pali.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>palate</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rendered groundless； uprooted</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the sky</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fear, terror</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>त्रास</i> (trāsa). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀢𑀸𑀲</i> (tāsa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>protector, saviour</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>त्रातृ</i> (trātṛ).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>so much</li><li>so long</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>तावत्</i> (tāvat).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to protect</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>त्रायते</i> (trāyate).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>protection</li></ol><p>Compare with Sanskrit <i>त्राण</i> (trāṇa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shore, riverbank</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>तीर</i> (tīra). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀢𑀻𑀭</i> (tīra).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>excitement</li><li>agitation</li><li>fright</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>both</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>high, noble</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>उच्च</i> (ucca).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rubbing of the body</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>high bed</li></ol><p><i>uccā</i> (high) +&lrm; <i>sayana</i> (bed)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>Verbal prefix:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>out of</li><li>upwards</li><li>awry</li><li>exceptionally</li></ol></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>उद्-</i> (ud-).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>उदक</i> (udaká). Cognate with Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀉𑀅𑀅</i> (uaa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lake of water</li></ol><p><i>udaka</i> +&lrm; <i>rahada</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flood of water</li></ol><p><i>udaka</i> +&lrm; <i>aṇṇava</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>belly</li><li>cavity, interior</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>उदर</i> (udara).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stomach contents</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>udeti</i> (“to come out”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>indication</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>list, a table of contents；a cluster</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go out, to come out</li><li>to go up, to rise</li><li>to increase</li></ol><p><i>ud-</i> (“out”) +&lrm; <i>eti</i> (“to go”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>past participle of <i>udeti</i> (“to come out”)</li><li>risen</li><li>elevated</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>उदित</i> (udita).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mortar (<i>something in or on which to grind items</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>huge, mighty</li><li>fierce</li></ol><p>Cognate with Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀉𑀕𑁆𑀕</i> (ugga).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to laugh aloud</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Indo-Aryan <i>&#42;udȷ́ʰagẓʰáti</i>, from <i>&#42;ud</i> (“out, outward”) + <i>&#42;ȷ́ʰagẓʰ-</i>, reduplication of the root <i>&#42;ȷ́ʰas-</i> (“to laugh”); compare Sanskrit <i>हसति</i> (hasati, “laughs”). By surface analysis, <i>ud-</i> +&lrm; <i>jagghati</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>straight, right</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>ऋजु</i> (ṛjú, “straight, right”), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;Hr̥ȷ́úš</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃r̥ǵéws</i>. Cognate with Ardhamagadhi Prakrit <i>𑀉𑀚𑁆𑀚𑀼</i> (ujju), Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀉𑀚𑁆𑀚𑀼</i> (ujju), Old Marathi <i>उजु</i> (uju).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>high, prominent</li><li>excellent</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>उत्कृष्ट</i> (utkṛṣṭa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hold up, to take up</li><li>to suspend (e.g. a monk for misconduct)</li></ol><p><i>ud-</i> +&lrm; <i>khipati</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>torch</li><li>meteor</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dung, excrement</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dirt</li></ol><p>Doublet of <i>ukkalāpa</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the letter 'u'</li></ol><p>Possibly from Sanskrit <i>उकार</i> (ukāra). By surface analysis, translingual <i>උ</i> (the letter) +&lrm; <i>kāra</i> (term designating a letter or sound) etc.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>owl</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>उलूक</i> (ulūka).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>underground watercourse, conduit</li><li>side track, wrong way, devious way</li></ol><p><i>ud</i> +&lrm; <i>magga</i> (“way, path”), literally “off-track”.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>emerging and diving</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>To emerge</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accumulation</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>advice; indication; instruction</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>उपदेश</i> (upadeśa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to approach</li><li>to undergo</li><li>to undertake</li></ol><p><i>upa-</i> +&lrm; <i>gacchati</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spiritual teacher, spiritual preceptor</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>उपाध्याय</i> (upādhyāya). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀉𑀯𑀚𑁆𑀛𑀸𑀅</i> (uvajjhāa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>help, support, favour</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>upa-</i> +&lrm; <i>kāra</i>; possibly from Sanskrit <i>उपकार</i> (upakāra).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scratch</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>simile; parable; comparison</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>उपमा</i> (upamā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tie up, to wrap up</li><li>to bear enmity or a grudge against someone, to scorn</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>उपनह्यति</i> (upanahyati, “to bind up”). By surface analysis, <i>upa-</i> +&lrm; <i>-nayhati</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be reborn in</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rebirth</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) crown prince; heir apparent; viceroy</li></ol><p><i>upa-</i> +&lrm; <i>rājan</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow calm</li><li>to cease</li><li>to be settled</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>उपशाम्यति</i> (upaśāmyati). By surface analysis, <i>upa-</i> +&lrm; <i>sammati</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to approach</li><li>to go to</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blameworthy</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>existing near</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come to pass, to take place</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>finding fault； blaming</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>patient</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Buddhism</i>) Equanimity, one of the Ten Perfections.</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>उपेक्षा</i> (upekṣā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Buddhism</i>) uposatha, a day of fasting, devotion, or other religious observation</li><li>(Buddhism,&#32;figurative) special observance of the Five, Eight, or Ten Precepts, as on an uposatha</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>उपवसथ</i> (upavasatha, “dwelling with [the gods], a fasting day, especially before a soma sacrifice”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to arise</li></ol><p><i>ud-</i> +&lrm; <i>-pajjati</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water lily</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>उत्पल</i> (utpala).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>subsisting on something else, clinging</li><li>Derived things, derivative</li></ol><p>Contraction of <i>upādāya</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agitation through clinging</li></ol><p><i>upādā</i> +&lrm; <i>paritassanā</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>out of, as, for</li><li>compared with, according to</li></ol><p>Formally <i>upa-</i> +&lrm; <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>dā</i> +&lrm; <i>-ya</i>, the absolutive of *<i>upādāyati</i> or *<i>upādadāyati</i>, but the verb only exists in the passive <i>form</i> <i>upādiyati</i>, which has active meaning. This absolutive then serves as a preposition.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a shoe</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a lay devotee</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>state of a devotee, state of being a believing layman or a lay follower of the Buddha</li></ol><p><i>upāsaka</i> +&lrm; <i>-atta</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to attend, to honour, to serve, to revere</li></ol><p><i>upa-</i> +&lrm; <i>ās</i>, literally 'to sit around', as seen in many depictions of the Buddha and his disciples.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>way</li><li>means</li><li>resource</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>उपाय</i> (upāya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Interpretation of many of the inflectional forms of uras (“breast”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snake</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>उरग</i> (uraga).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breast, chest</li><li>base of a carriage pole</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bull</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>ऋषभ</i> (ṛṣabha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>usumā</i> (“heat”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>trying</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>procession, going or running about, tumult</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cause to move back</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heat</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>उष्मन्</i> (uṣman, “heat”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>utmost</li><li>highest</li><li>best</li><li>noble</li><li>excellent</li></ol><p>Superlative of <i>ud-</i>. Also most likely derived from Sanskrit <i>उत्तम</i> (uttama).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>uttamapurisa</i> (“first person”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) first person</li></ol><p>Calque of Sanskrit <i>उत्तमपुरुष</i> (uttamapuruṣa), composed as <i>uttama</i> (“utmost”) +&lrm; <i>purisa</i> (“person”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>most important part of body</li><li>head</li><li>eye</li><li>penis</li></ol><p><i>uttama</i> +&lrm; <i>aṅga</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>higher, superior</li><li>further, subsequent</li><li>northern</li><li>over, beyond (object is in prior element of a bahuvrihi)</li><li>crossing over</li><li>to be crossed</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the north; northern direction</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>उत्तर</i> (úttara), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;údteros</i>.</p><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>उत्तर</i> (uttara). Synchronically, clearly related to the root <i>tar</i> and the verb <i>uttarati</i> (“to cross over”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bridge</li></ol><p>Tatpuruṣa compound of <i>uttara</i> (“crossing”) +&lrm; <i>setu</i> (“embankment”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cross over</li><li>to escape from</li><li>to emerge from water at its edge</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>उत्तरति</i> (uttarati). By surface analysis, <i>ud-</i> +&lrm; <i>tarati</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lintel</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>season, time</li><li>season (<i>one of the three seasons of the year</i>)</li><li>menses</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>ऋतु</i> (ṛtu, “appointed time”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>star; planet</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) constellation</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hot (having or giving off a high temperature)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>उष्ण</i> (uṣṇá). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀉𑀡𑁆𑀳</i> (uṇha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hot weather</li><li>summer (season)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>उष्णकाल</i> (uṣṇakāla). By surface analysis, <i>uṇha</i> +&lrm; <i>kāla</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wool</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>ऊर्णा</i> (ūrṇā) and parallel to Pali <i>ऊर्ण</i> (ūrṇa). Doublet of <i>uṇṇā</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wool</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>ऊर्णा</i> (ūrṇā). Doublet of <i>uṇṇa</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rising</li><li>origin</li><li>opportunity to arise</li></ol><p><i>ud-</i> +&lrm; <i>ṭhāna</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(poetic,&#32;enclitic) like, as if</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Pali letter 'v'</li></ol><p>From <i>iva</i>.</p><p>Probably from the pronunciation of a syllable consisting only of the letter.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pali root <i>vac</i> (root)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>utterance</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वचन</i> (vacaná). Cognate with Ashokan Prakrit <i>𑀯𑀘𑀦</i> (vacana), Gandhari <i>𐨬𐨕𐨣</i> (vacana), Niya Prakrit <i>𐨬𐨕𐨣</i> (vacana), Prakrit <i>𑀯𑀬𑀡</i> (vayaṇa). By surface analysis, <i>vac</i> (root) +&lrm; <i>-ana</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>words, saying</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वच</i> (vaca) or Sanskrit <i>वचस्</i> (vacas).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to say</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The parts of the verb <i>vadati</i> formed from the root <i>vac</i> (root).</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>excrement</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animal</i>) calf</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वत्स</i> (vatsa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>affectionate</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वत्सल</i> (vatsala). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀯𑀘𑁆𑀙𑀮</i> (vacchala).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a weaned calf, bullock</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वत्सतर</i> (vatsatará).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to say</li></ol><p>This verb is built from two roots, <i>vac</i> descended from Sanskrit <i>वच्</i> (vac), and <i>vad</i> descended from Sanskrit <i>वद्</i> (vad).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>liberal</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strike, to punish</li><li>to kill</li></ol><p>Ultimately from √<i>वध्</i> (vadh).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>company</li><li>section (e.g. of a book)</li><li>group</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) group of consonants with a property in common, such as place of articulation</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>separated</li><li>incomplete</li><li>dissentious</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वर्ग</i> (varga, “group”).</p><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>व्यग्र</i> (vyagra).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thunderbolt</li><li>diamond</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वज्र</i> (vajra).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having a diamond mace in his hand.</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>having a diamond mace in his hand</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>वज्रपाणि</i> (vajrapāṇi), a compound of <i>वज्र</i> (vajra, “a kind of crossed mace; a thunderbolt”) + <i>पाणि</i> (pāṇi, “hand”, noun).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kidney</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>crooked</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वृक्क</i> (vṛkka, “kidney”).</p><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वक्र</i> (vakra, “crooked”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The letter 'v'</li></ol><p>Possibly from Sanskrit <i>वकार</i> (vakāra). By surface analysis, translingual <i>ව</i> (the letter) +&lrm; <i>kāra</i> (term designating a letter or sound) etc.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forest</li><li>desire</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वन</i> (vana, “forest”).</p><p>Formations from the root <i>van</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jungle thicket</li></ol><p><i>vana</i> +&lrm; <i>saṇḍa</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to desire, to seek</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वनति</i> (vanati) and Sanskrit <i>वनोति</i> (vanoti).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to salute</li><li>to pays homage</li><li>to honour</li><li>to adore</li></ol><p>Cognate with Sanskrit <i>वन्दते</i> (vándate)/<i>वन्दति</i> (vándati).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woman</li></ol><p>Either inherited from Sanskrit <i>वनिता</i> (vanitā, “woman”), or a common Middle Indic formation from the root <i>van</i> as the feminine of the unattested past participle of vanati (“to desire”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>vanati</i> (“to desire”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sow</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वपति</i> (vápati, “he sows”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>excellent</li><li>noble</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wish, boon, favour</li><li>The Pali root <i>var</i></li></ol><p>Extracted from <i>varati</i>, <i>vareti</i> and <i>vāreti</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hinder</li><li>to conceal</li><li>to protect</li><li>to wish, to desire</li></ol><p>Derived from Sanskrit <i>वृणोति</i> (vṛṇoti). A different formation is favoured as the precursor of the Pali word.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a boar, wild hog</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वराह</i> (varāha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Pali root <i>vas</i> (to dwell)</li><li>the Pali root <i>vas</i> (to clothe)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an outcaste, a low man</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वृषल</i> (vṛṣala).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>garment, clothing</li><li>dwelling, abode</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वसन</i> (vásana). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀯𑀲𑀡</i> (vasaṇa).</p><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वसन</i> (vasana). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀯𑀲𑀡</i> (vasaṇa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spring</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>वसन्त</i> (vasantá).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dwell</li><li>to stay</li><li>to clothe</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वसति</i> (vasati).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rain</li><li>year</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वर्ष</i> (varṣa).</p><p>See <i>va</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rain, shower, pour</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वर्षति</i> (várṣati). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀯𑀭𑀺𑀲𑀇</i> (varisaï), <i>𑀯𑀲𑁆𑀲𑀤𑀺</i> (vassadi), <i>𑀯𑀸𑀲𑀇</i> (vāsaï).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wealth</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>earth</li></ol><p><i>vasu-</i> (“wealth”) +&lrm; <i>dharā</i> (“bearer”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grease</li><li>fat</li><li>tallow</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वसा</i> (vasā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>surely, certainly, indeed, alas!</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>religious duty</li></ol><p>Probably inherited from Sanskrit <i>वत</i> (vata), though Vedic Sanskrit used <i>बत</i> (bata) instead.</p><p>In form inherited from Sanskrit <i>वृत</i> (vṛta) but with meaning corresponding to Sanskrit <i>व्रत</i> (vrata, “vow”); both derived from the same root, Sanskrit <i>वृ</i> (vṛ).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>duty</li><li>service</li><li>function</li><li>religious observance, vow</li><li>mouth</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>opened wide</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वृत्त</i> (vṛtta).</p><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>व्यात्त</i> (vyātta).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) present</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वर्तमान</i> (vartamāna, present middle participle of <i>वर्तते</i> (vartate)).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>present tense</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>vattamānā</i> <i>vibhatti</i> (“present tense”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who says or tells</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dutiful</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>exist</li><li>happen</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वर्तति</i> (vartati). Doublet of <i>vaṭṭati</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make go on</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cloth, garment</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वस्त्र</i> (vastra).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>object; thing; reason; ground; story</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>वस्तु</i> (vastu).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to say</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The parts of the verb <i>vadati</i> formed from the root <i>vac</i> (root).</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वक्ति</i> (vakti).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cheating; fraud</li><li>illusion; delusion</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वञ्चन</i> (vañcana).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deceive</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carpenter</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>vaḍḍhaki</i> (“carpenter”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>growing</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>augmentation, enlargement, extension, prolongation</li><li>growth, increase, development</li><li>upbringing, fostering</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>वर्धन</i> (vardhana).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>increase, growth</li><li>welfare happiness</li><li>interest on money lent</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वृद्धि</i> (vṛddhi, “growth, increase, interest, profit”). Doublet of <i>vuddhi</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mare</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वडवा</i> (vaḍavā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bamboo</li><li>lineage</li><li>race</li><li>family</li><li>dynasty</li><li>history</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वंश</i> (vaṃśa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wound</li><li>sore</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pauper</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trade; trading</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>वणिज्या</i> (vaṇijyā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>colour</li><li>appearance</li><li>class</li><li>quality</li><li>beauty</li><li>praise</li><li>letter, vowel, syllable</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वर्ण</i> (varṇa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prostitute (woman who engages in sexual activity for payment)</li></ol><p>Tatpuruṣa compound of <i>vaṇṇa</i> (“beauty”) +&lrm; <i>dāsī</i> (“slavess”), literally “slavess of beauty”.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>road, path</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वर्त्म</i> (vártma), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wértmn̥</i>. Cognate with Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀯𑀝𑁆𑀝</i> (vaṭṭa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quail</li></ol><p>Compare Sanskrit <i>वर्तक</i> (vartaka).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knowledge</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वेद</i> (veda). Cognate with Prakrit <i>वेअ</i> (vea), <i>वेय</i> (veya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>perception, feeling, sensation</li><li>pain, suffering</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वेदना</i> (vedanā). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀯𑁂𑀅𑀡𑀸</i> (veaṇā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>vedeti</i> (“to know”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Videhan, of the country Videha</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वैदेह</i> (vaideha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>present active participle of <i>vedeti</i> (“to know”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to know</li><li>to experience</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cornice, ledge, railing</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>वेदि</i> (vedi).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>which is to be known</li><li>which is to be understood</li></ol><p>Gerundive in <i>-tabba</i> of <i>vedeti</i>, formed from the root in the same way as the infinitive <i>veditum</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>time</li><li>shore</li><li>boundary</li></ol><p>Probably inherited from Sanskrit <i>वेला</i> (velā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loom, shuttle</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वेम</i> (véma, “loom, shuttle”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>difference, distinction, discrepancy, disproportion</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>विमत</i> (vimata).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>full development, abundance</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>वैपुल्य</i> (vaipulya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hatred</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वैर</i> (vaira).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abstinence</li></ol><p>From <i>viramaṇa</i> (“abstinence”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prostitute (woman who engages in sexual activity for payment)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वेश्या</i> (veśyā). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀯𑁂𑀲𑀺𑀬𑀸</i> (vesiyā), <i>𑀯𑁂𑀲𑁆𑀲𑀸</i> (vĕssā), <i>𑀯𑁂𑀲𑀸</i> (vesā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Vaishya</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>वैश्य</i> (vaiśya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the month of Vaisakha, corresponding to April–May</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prostitute (woman who engages in sexual activity for payment)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वेश्या</i> (veśyā). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀯𑁂𑀲𑀺𑀬𑀸</i> (vesiyā), <i>𑀯𑁂𑀲𑁆𑀲𑀸</i> (vĕssā), <i>𑀯𑁂𑀲𑀸</i> (vesā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>common rattan (<i>Calamus rotang</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वेतस</i> (vetasá).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>twig, rod, staff</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वेत्र</i> (vetra).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>distinction, lucidity, accomplishment</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>वियाति</i> (viyāti).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>answer, explanation, exposition</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>वैयाकरण</i> (vaiyākaraṇa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bamboo</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वेणु</i> (veṇu). Doublet of <i>veṇu</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lapis lazuli, a precious stone</li></ol><p>The Sanskrit word <i>वैडूर्य</i> (vaiḍūrya) is traditionally explained (Pāṇini, <i>Aṣṭādhyāyī</i>, 4.3.84) as a mineral being named after the city of Vidūra (from <i>विदूरात्यः</i> (Vidūrāt syaḥ)). The Sanskrit word is a derivation of <i>veḷuriya</i> (“beryl”), itself borrowed from Dravidian, an adjectival form of <i>veḷur</i> (“white town”) - compare Telugu <i>వెలి</i> (veli, “white”) and <i>ఊరు</i> (ūru, “town”). Vēḷur (modern-day Bēlūr) was the beryl mining capital city known as Vidūra to Pāṇini. The original meaning of the South Indian word was "white crystals of quartz and beryl" (preserved in Arabic and Persian borrowing), and later "coloured (blue and green) beryl", as retained in Pali and Sanskrit.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bamboo</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वेणु</i> (veṇu). Doublet of <i>veḷu</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>division, partition</li><li>distinction, modification</li><li>(<i>paintings</i>) detail</li><li>inflectional ending of noun or verb</li><li>declension, conjugation</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) tense</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>विभक्ति</i> (vibhakti) or reconstituted as <i>vi-</i> +&lrm; <i>bhaj</i> (“to divide”) +&lrm; <i>-ti</i>. In the grammatical senses, a semantic loan from Sanskrit <i>विभक्ति</i> (vibhakti).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move</li><li>to walk</li><li>to wander</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>विचरति</i> (vicarati). Cognate with Maharastri Prakrit <i>𑀯𑀺𑀅𑀭𑀇</i> (viaraï), <i>𑀯𑀺𑀬𑀭𑀇</i> (viyaraï), <i>𑀯𑀺𑀘𑀭𑀇</i> (vicaraï), Sauraseni Prakrit <i>𑀯𑀺𑀘𑀭𑀤𑀺</i> (vicaradi).</p><p>By surface analysis, <i>vi-</i> +&lrm; <i>carati</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>search, investigation, examination</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>विचय</i> (vicaya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scorpion</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वृश्चिक</i> (vṛścika).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seek, search</li><li>to examine, investigate</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to know</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>विदति</i> (vidati), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weyd-</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>span; a measure of length equal to 12 aṅgulas</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>demolition, destruction</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a widow</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>विधवा</i> (vidhavā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rule</li><li>method</li><li>luck, fate</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>विधि</i> (vidhi).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>knowing, wise, skilled in</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to depart</li><li>to go away</li><li>to disappears</li></ol><p><i>vi-</i> +&lrm; <i>gacchati</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rebuke, reproach</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gone away</li><li>disappeared</li><li>ceased</li><li>having lost</li><li>without</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to live, to abide, to stay</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a dwelling place</li><li>a monastery</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>विहार</i> (vihāra, “dwelling place”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to abandon</li></ol><p><i>vi</i> +&lrm; <i>jahati</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>victory, triumph</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>विजय</i> (vijaya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>vijeti</i></li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to conquer, to master, to triumph over</li></ol><p><i>vi-</i> +&lrm; <i>jeti</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>vijeti</i></li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pierce, perforate</li><li>to shoot with an arrow</li><li>to strike, hit, split</li></ol><p>Cognate with Sanskrit <i>विध्यति</i> (vídhyati).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lightning</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>विद्युत्</i> (vidyút).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>electronic switch.</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>higher knowledge, science</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>विद्या</i> (vidyā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tremble</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thought</li></ol><p><i>vi-</i> +&lrm; <i>kappa</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having intentions upon</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>changed, altered</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>विकृत</i> (vikṛta). Doublet of <i>vikaṭa</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a sort or kind; made of a shape</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>changed, altered</li><li>distorted</li><li>disgusting, filthy, foul</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>विकट</i> (vikaṭa). Doublet of <i>vikata</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disturb, confuse</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>upset, perplexed</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of confused or upset mind</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wrong time</li><li>afternoon and night</li></ol><p><i>vi-</i> +&lrm; <i>kāla</i></p><p>The meaning of 'afternoon and night' arises because healthy monks are not allowed to eat after noon.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eating in the afternoon or at night, an activity forbidden to healthy Buddhist monks</li></ol><p><i>vikāla</i> (wrong time) +&lrm; <i>bhojana</i> (food)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ointment</li><li>cosmetic</li></ol><p><i>vi-</i> +&lrm; <i>lepana</i> (smearing)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eye</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>विलोचन</i> (vilocana).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>astonishment, surprise</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>विस्मय</i> (vismaya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>release</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>release, deliverance, emancipation</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>विमुक्ति</i> (vimukti).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>removal</li><li>(<i>logic</i>) rule, terminology</li><li>norm of conduct</li><li>the code of monastic discipline</li></ol><p><i>vi-</i> +&lrm; <i>nī</i> (root) +&lrm; <i>-a</i>; see Sanskrit <i>विनय</i> (vinaya​) for more.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>vineti</i> (“to remove; to subdue; to instruct”), <i>which see for further details</i></li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remove, to put away</li><li>to subdue, to conquer, to restrain</li><li>to instruct, to educate, to train</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>विनयति</i> (vinayati). By surface analysis, <i>vi-</i> +&lrm; <i>neti</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a place of suffering</li><li>a bad falling</li></ol><p><i>vi-</i> +&lrm; <i>nipāta</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disentangle</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>separation</li></ol><p><i>vinā</i> (“without”) +&lrm; <i>bhāva</i> (“being”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>destruction</li></ol><p><i>vi-</i> +&lrm; <i>nāsa</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>destroying</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leader, guide, instructor</li><li>judge</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>विनायक</i> (vināyaka).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hostile</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Buddhism</i>) vipassana (insight into the true nature of reality)</li></ol><p>From <i>vi-</i> +&lrm; <i>passanā</i> or <i>vipassati</i> (verb) +&lrm; <i>-anā</i>. Cognate with Sanskrit <i>विपश्यना</i> (vipaśyanā). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>failure</li><li>misfortune</li><li>distress</li></ol><p><i>vi-</i> +&lrm; <i>patti</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to abandon</li><li>to give up</li></ol><p><i>vi-</i> +&lrm; <i>pajahati</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unfinished</li></ol><p><i>vi-</i> +&lrm; <i>pakata</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very clear, pure, bright</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>remorse</li></ol><p><i>vi-</i> +&lrm; <i>paṭisāra</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>remorseful</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abstinence</li></ol><p><i>vi-</i> (“away”) +&lrm; <i>rati</i> (“love, attachment, fondness of”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Buddhism</i>) Energy, enthusiasm; one of the Ten Perfections.</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>वीर्य</i> (vīrya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dispassionateness</li></ol><p><i>vi-</i> +&lrm; <i>rāga</i> (“attachment”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poison</li><li>venom</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>विष</i> (viṣa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>locality, region, sphere, object</li></ol><p><i>vi</i> (“spread about”) +&lrm; <i>sī</i> (“lie”) +&lrm; <i>a</i>. Contrast with meaning of the parallel Sanskrit formation <i>विशय</i> (viśaya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>distinction; difference; attainment</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>विशेष</i> (viśeṣa, “distinction, excellence”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>visodheti</i>, causative of <i>visujjhati</i> (“to be purified”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to purify, to clean</li></ol><p>Causative of <i>visujjhati</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stainless, sanctified</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be cleaned</li><li>to be cleansed</li><li>to be pure</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>separately, individually</li><li>apart</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>restless motion</li><li>wriggling</li><li>show (as in performance)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pleasure</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dispute</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wilderness</li></ol><p><i>vi-</i> +&lrm; <i>vana</i> (“forest”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to open</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move back, to go back, to revolve, to begin again</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>detachment, loneliness, separation, seclusion</li><li>discrimination (of thought)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>विवेक</i> (viveka).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>diverse, manifold, mixed</li><li>full of, gay with</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carrying yoke</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>विविध</i> (vividha); by surface analysis, <i>vi-</i> +&lrm; <i>vidha</i>.</p><p>From Sanskrit <i>विवध</i> (vivadha​). See <i>वि-</i> (vi-, “distinction prefix”) +&lrm; <i>वह्</i> (vah, “to carry”) for more.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>learned</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to separate</li></ol><p><i>vi-</i> +&lrm; <i>yojeti</i> (“to yoke”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to instruct, inform, teach</li></ol><p>Secondary causative of <i>vijānāti</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>consciousness</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>विज्ञान</i> (vijñāna).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wise, learned</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tree</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>expression, jurisprudence, current appellation</li><li>convention, concept</li><li>commerce</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>व्यवहार</i> (vyavahāra).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Pali root <i>var</i></li></ol><p>Possibly extracted from <i>saṃvuta</i>, or more likely from the expected but unattested Pali present stem development <i>&#42;vuṇāti</i> of Sanskrit <i>वृणोति</i> (vṛṇoti).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>increase, growth</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वृद्धि</i> (vṛddhi, “growth, increase, interest, profit”). Doublet of <i>vaḍḍhi</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Also used in active sense</li><li>shaven</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>उक्त</i> (ukta, “said”). Compare <i>utta</i>.</p><p>From an earlier <i>&#42;utta</i>, from Sanskrit <i>उप्त</i> (upta, “sown”).</p><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>व्युप्त</i> (vyùpta, “shaven”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conduct, habit, practice, usage, livelihood</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>वृत्ति</i> (vṛtti).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>old, aged</li><li>venerable</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वृद्ध</i> (vṛddha), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;wr̥dᶻdʰás</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>increase, growth</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वृद्धि</i> (vṛddhi, “growth, increase, interest, profit”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rain</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वृष्टि</i> (vṛṣṭi). Related to <i>vassati</i>. Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀯𑀺𑀝𑁆𑀞𑀺</i> (viṭṭhi), <i>𑀯𑀼𑀝𑁆𑀞𑀺</i> (vuṭṭhi).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rise</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sign</li><li>mark</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) letter</li><li>letter (as opposed to spirit)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>व्यञ्जन</i> (vyañjana). By surface analysis, <i>vi-</i> +&lrm; <i>añj</i> (“to daub”) +&lrm; <i>-ana</i>. The sense 'letter' is a shared innovation of the early Prakrits, taken up by Sanskrit.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heap, mass</li><li>military array; array of troops</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>व्यूह</i> (vyūha). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀯𑀽𑀳</i> (vūha), <i>𑀯𑀺𑀊𑀳</i> (viūha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>or (<i>always enclitic</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वा</i> (vā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>speech, talk</li><li>theory, doctrine</li><li>dispute</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;poetic</i>) sentence, saying</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Sanskrit <i>वाक्य</i> (vākya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hair of the tail</li><li>horsehair</li><li>tail</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>malicious, troublesome, difficult</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वाल</i> (vāla). Although both Pali and documented Sanskrit have a by-form with initial b- (<i>bāla</i> and <i>बाल</i> (bāla)), the by-forms need not be a common inheritance.</p><p>Apparently inherited from Sanskrit <i>व्याल</i> (vyāla). However, both these forms look like Prakritisations of <i>vāḷa</i> (“a type of savage animal”) and its apparent source Sanskrit <i>व्याड</i> (vyāḍa), so relationships may be more complex.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an archer who can hit a hair</li></ol><p><i>vāla</i> (“hair”) +&lrm; <i>vedhin</i> (“piercing”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dwarfish, small</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वामन</i> (vāmaná).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>monkey</li></ol><p>Formed from vana (“forest”) with the implication 'forest dweller'. Cognate with Sanskrit <i>वानर</i> (vānara).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prevent, to obstruct, to hinder</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वारयति</i> (vāráyati); on the surface, the regular long-vowelled causative of varati.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वारि</i> (vāri).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>restrained</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>avoidance</li></ol><p>From the root <i>var</i> +&lrm; <i>-itta</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>life</li><li>dwelling (both place and action)</li><li>sojourn</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वास</i> (vāsa). The Pali root is <i>vas</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that which remains in the mind</li><li>tendencies of the past</li><li>impression</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वासना</i> (vāsanā). By surface analysis, <i>vas</i> (root) +&lrm; <i>-anā</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take up residence</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wind</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>वात</i> (vāta).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blow</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to struggle, to strive</li></ol><p><i>vi-</i> +&lrm; <i>āyamati</i> (“to stretch”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snake</li><li>beast of prey</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>व्याड</i> (vyāḍa). Possibly a doublet of <i>vāla</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>merchant; trader</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>वाणिज</i> (vāṇija).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>brave; heroic</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hero</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>वीर</i> (vīra).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stainless</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>street</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>वीथि</i> (vīthi).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>relief, calmness, cessation</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>To appease</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relative</i>) who, which, that</li><li>(<i>relative</i>) which, that</li><li>(<i>relative</i>) (<i>duplicated</i>) whatever</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Pali letter 'y'</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>यद्</i> (yad).</p><p>Probably from the pronunciation of a syllable consisting only of the letter.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>if</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>यदि</i> (yadi), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;Hyádi</i>. Cognate with Ashokan Prakrit <i>𐨩𐨡𐨁</i> (yadi).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>i.e., namely</li></ol><p><i>yad</i> (“which”) +&lrm; <i>idaṃ</i> (“(is) this”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relative</i>) when</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>यदा</i> (yadā, “yadắ”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>liver</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>यकृत्</i> (yakṛt).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>demon, yaksha</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>यक्ष</i> (yakṣa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The letter 'y'</li></ol><p>Possibly from Sanskrit <i>यकार</i> (yakāra). By surface analysis, translingual <i>ය</i> (the letter) +&lrm; <i>kāra</i> (term designating a letter or sound) etc.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>double, twin</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>twin</li><li>pair, couple</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fame</li><li>success</li><li>honour</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>यशस्</i> (yaśas).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>as, like</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>यथा</i> (yathā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>as it was carried</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>according to the reality</li><li>rightly, truly, correctly</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grass, hay, fodder</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>यवस</i> (yávasa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sacrifice</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>यज्ञ</i> (yajña).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nominative singular of <i>yañña</i> (“sacrifice”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>mostly</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>where</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>youth</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>यौवन</i> (yauvana). Both Pali and Prakrit <i>𑀚𑁄𑀯𑁆𑀯𑀡</i> (jŏvvaṇa) show a change of long vowel + consonant to short vowel + geminate, with Pali then changing /vv/ to /bb/ as usual.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the pride of youth</li></ol><p><i>yobbana</i> +&lrm; <i>mada</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>warrior</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>योध</i> (yodha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>warrior</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rest after toiling and exertion</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>योगक्षेम</i> (yogakṣema, “rest after labour”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carriage, wagon</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>suitable, fit for</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>योग्य</i> (yogya, “fit to be yoked”). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀚𑁄𑀕𑁆𑀕</i> (jŏgga).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yojana</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>योजन</i> (yojana).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to yoke, to harness</li><li>to combine, to unite, to mix, to apply</li><li>to prepare</li><li>to urge</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>योजयति</i> (yojayati). Doublet of <i>yuñjati</i>, of which it has the form of a causative.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>womb</li><li>origin</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>योनि</i> (yoni).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tie (<i>of the yoke for a conveyance</i>)</li><li>string</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>योक्त्र</i> (yóktra), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;yáwktram</i> (“thong”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;yéwg-tro-m</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>battle, war, fight</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>युद्ध</i> (yuddha). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀚𑀼𑀤𑁆𑀥</i> (juddha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pair, couple</li><li>generation (period of time)</li><li>age of the world</li><li>yoke of a plough or carriage</li></ol><p>Cognate with Sanskrit <i>युग</i> (yuga), which see for further connections</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fight, make war</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>युध्यते</i> (yudhyate), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;Hyawdʰ-</i> (“to fight”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>able, fit for, sufficient</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) conjunction</li><li>junction</li></ol><p>This derivative of the verb <i>yuñjati</i> (“to join”) traces back to Sanskrit <i>युक्त</i> (yukta) and beyond.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>application, usage</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>युक्ति</i> (yukti). Cognate with Prakrit <i>जुत्ति</i> (jutti).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>young</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>young man, youth</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>युवन्</i> (yuvan). Doublet of <i>yūna</i>, which seems to have developed from the feminine.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>maiden, girl</li></ol><p>Feminine of the adjective <i>yuvan</i> used as a noun.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>young</li></ol><p>Thematicisation of <i>yuvan</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to join with</li><li>to engage in</li><li>to endeavour</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>युञ्जति</i> (yuñjati, “to join”), or independently thematicised from Sanskrit <i>युनक्ति</i> (yunakti). Doublet of <i>yojeti</i>, which has the form of a causative.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relative</i>) who, which, that</li></ol><p>feminine of <i>ya</i> (“who”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beggar</li></ol><p><i>yāca</i> +&lrm; <i>-ka</i>. This and Sanskrit <i>याचक</i> (yācaka) appear to be parallel developments.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>begging</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beg, ask for, entreat</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>याचति</i> (yā́cati).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>like which, like what, similar to</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>यादृश</i> (yādṛśa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rice-gruel</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nightwatch of three hours</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>याम</i> (yāma, “cessation”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>going, journey, motion</li><li>means of conveyance (including animals)</li><li>vehicle</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>यान</i> (yāna).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go, travel</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>याति</i> (yā́ti), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;yeh₂-</i>. Cognate with Ashokan Prakrit <i>𑀬𑀸𑀢𑀺</i> (yāti).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>as far as</li><li>until</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>as much as, as many as</li><li>as far as</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>going</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who goes</li></ol><p><i>yā</i> (“to go”) +&lrm; <i>-in</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>broth, soup</li><li>juice</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>यूष</i> (yūṣa), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;yuHs-</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>relative; kinsman</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>ज्ञाति</i> (jñātí). Cognate with Ashokan Prakrit <i>𑀜𑀸𑀢𑀺</i> (ñāti, Girnar), <i>𑀦𑀸𑀢𑀺</i> (nāti, Kalsi).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paternal home</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>&#42;ज्ञाति-घर</i> (&#42;jñāti-ghara). By surface analysis, tatpuruṣa compound of <i>ñāti</i> +&lrm; <i>ghara</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>method, system</li><li>right manner, propriety</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>न्याय</i> (nyāya). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀡𑀸𑀬</i> (ṇāya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>following the right path</li></ol><p><i>ñāya</i> (“right manner”) +&lrm; <i>paṭipanna</i> (“following a route”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A wisdom, insight</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>ज्ञान</i> (jñāna).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>perfect knowledge</li></ol><p><i>ñāṇa</i> +&lrm; <i>dassana</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the month of Aashaadha, corresponding to June-July</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>illness, sickness, affliction, disease</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>आबाध</i> (ābādha).</p><p>The Pali root is <i>bādh</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to oppress</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sick</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to practice, perform, indulge in, behave, practice, do</li><li>to step upon, pass through</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>आचरति</i> (ācárati).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teacher</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>आचार्य</i> (ācārya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pointing out</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) substitution</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>आदेश</i> (ādeśa, “advice, instruction”). It might be a semantic loan from Sanskrit <i>आदेश</i> (ādeśa, “substituted form or letter”), but the construction is different.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lordship, domination, power</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beginnings, origin</li><li>(<i>list</i>): and so on; from</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>initial, beginning</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>initially</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>आदि</i> (ādi).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sun</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>आदित्य</i> (āditya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>beautiful in the beginning</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>taking</li></ol><p><i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>dāna</i> (“giving”) as <i>ādadāti</i> (“to take”) is <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>dadāti</i> (“to give”); alternatively, just <i>ādā</i> +&lrm; <i>-ana</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mirror</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>आदर्श</i> (ādarśá, “mirror”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coming, arrival</li><li>that which arrives:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>income, influx</li><li>knowledge</li><li>sacred book</li><li>doctrine, religion</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) augment</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>come, arrived</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>आगत</i> (ā́gata).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anger, ill-will, malice, hatred</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>आघात</i> (āghāta). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀆𑀖𑀸𑀬</i> (āghāya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>coming back, returning</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>आगामिन्</i> (āgāmin). Morphologically <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>gāmin</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>removable</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>आहृत्य</i> (āhṛtya).</p><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>आहत्य</i> (āhatya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take</li><li>to bring, to fetch</li><li>to get, to acquire</li></ol><p><i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>harati</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bringing</li><li>accomplishing</li><li>plundering</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>आहरण</i> (āharaṇa). From the root <i>har</i> +&lrm; <i>-ana</i> By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>haraṇa</i>. Formally, the verbal noun of <i>āharati</i>, though there appear to be some divergences in attested meanings.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bringer</li></ol><p>Agent noun of <i>āharati</i>; from Sanskrit <i>आहर्तृ</i> (āhartṛ, “bringer”). The Pali root is <i>har</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adoration</li><li>oblation</li></ol><p>Ultimately from same root as English <i>god</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>worthy of adoration or offerings</li></ol><p><i>āhuna</i> +&lrm; <i>-eyya</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adoration</li><li>oblation</li></ol><p>Ultimately from same root as English <i>god</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>worthy of adoration or offerings</li></ol><p><i>āhuṇa</i> +&lrm; <i>-eyya</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>feeding, support, food, nutriment</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a multitude</li><li>mine, place of production</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>आकर</i> (ākara).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the letter 'ā'</li><li>state, condition</li><li>quality, attribute</li><li>appearance</li><li>manner</li></ol><p>Possibly from Sanskrit <i>आकार</i> (ākāra). By surface analysis, <i>ආ</i> (ā, the letter) +&lrm; <i>kāra</i> (term designating a letter or sound) etc.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>space, sky, atmosphere</li></ol><p>Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀆𑀓𑀸𑀲</i> (ākāsa), <i>𑀆𑀕𑀸𑀲</i> (āgāsa), <i>𑀆𑀆𑀲</i> (āāsa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aeroplane, aircraft, airplane.</li></ol><p>From <i>ākāsa</i> (“sky”) +&lrm; <i>yāna</i> (“vehicle”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>raw</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak to</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>आमन्त्रयते</i> (āmantrayate, “to address, to speak to”).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>illness, affliction</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>आमय</i> (āmaya).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>joy, bliss, happiness</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ananda (a disciple of the Buddha)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>आनन्द</i> (ānandá). Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀆𑀡𑀁𑀤</i> (āṇaṃda).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>freedom from debt</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring, to bring home</li><li>to fetch, to procure</li><li>to carry, to convey</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>static, imperturbable</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>profit</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>आपः</i> (āpaḥ).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enter, to meet with</li><li>to undergo</li><li>to make, to exhibit</li></ol><p><i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>-pajjati</i> (“to go”)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beginning, attempt</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>आरम्भ</i> (ārambha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>belonging to or residing in a forest</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an ascetic forest dwelling</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ārañña</i> (“forest”) +&lrm; <i>-ka</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>belonging to or living in a forest</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pride of health</li></ol><p><i>ārogya</i> +&lrm; <i>mada</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ascend, climb, to go up, to go on</li></ol><p>Cognate with Sanskrit <i>आरोहति</i> (ārohati).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pleasure</li><li>park</li><li>monastery</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>आराम</i> (ārāma). By surface analysis, <i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>rāma</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sitting, sitting down</li><li>seat, throne</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>आसन</i> (āsana).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spirit (as in liquor)</li><li>discharge from a sore</li><li>ideas which intoxicate the mind</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>आसव</i> (āsava).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flow</li></ol><p><i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>savati</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fear, doubt</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>आशङ्का</i> (āśaṅkā)</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mortification</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dwelling</li><li>rest-house</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>आवसथ</i> (āvasatha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weapon</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>आयुध</i> (āyudha).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>resident</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a pit, a well (a hole dug in the ground.)</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>income, profit</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>stretch, extend, stretch out</li></ol><p><i>ā-</i> +&lrm; <i>yamati</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inflow, that which brings income</li></ol><p><i>āya</i> +&lrm; <i>mukha</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>old, venerable</li></ol><p><i>āyus</i> +&lrm; <i>-mant</i></p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sphere</li><li>region</li><li>sense-organ</li><li>sensed object</li><li>position</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in the future</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>age</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>आयुस्</i> (āyus).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>request</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>आयाचन</i> (āyācana).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>name of a demon, Alavaka.</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rich, rich in grain or corn</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>आढ्य</i> (āḍhyá). Doublet of <i>aḍḍha</i>.</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>order, command, authority</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>आज्ञा</i> (ājñā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lord</li><li>ruler</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>ईश</i> (īśa).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pole of a plough or a carriage</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>ईषा</i> (īṣā).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thigh</li></ol><p>Inherited from Sanskrit <i>ऊरु</i> (ūru).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Devanagari script form of</i> aṃsa (“shoulder”)</li><li><i>Devanagari script form of</i> aṃsa (“part”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Devanagari script form of</i> aṃso, <i>which is</i> nominative singular of <i>अंस</i> &#x20;(aṃsa, “shoulder”)</li><li><i>Devanagari script form of</i> aṃso, <i>which is</i> nominative singular of <i>अंस</i> &#x20;(aṃsa, “part”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>Devanagari script form of</i> akarā, <i>which is</i> third-person singular imperfect active of <i>करोति</i> &#x20;(karoti, “to do”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Devanagari script form of</i> akāra (“letter 'a'”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Devanagari script form of</i> akārā, <i>which is</i> inflection of <i>अकार</i>:## ablative singular## nominative/vocative plural &#x20;(akāra, “letter a”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li><i>Devanagari script form of</i> akiñcana (“having nothing”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Devanagari script form of</i> akkhara (“letter”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Devanagari script form of</i> akkhi</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Devanagari script form of</i> agada</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li><i>Devanagari script form of</i> agada</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Devanagari script form of</i> agāra</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li><i>Devanagari script form of</i> agga</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a wooden bolt or pin for fastening a door</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a wooden bolt or pin for fastening a door</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Devanagari script form of</i> aggahaṇa (“seizure, taking”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>Devanagari script form of</i> aggahi, <i>which is</i> second/third-person singular aorist active of <i>गण्हाति</i> &#x20;(gaṇhāti, “to take”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Devanagari script form of</i> aggi</li></ol><p>Compare Sanskrit <i>अग्नि</i> (agni).</p></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li><i>Devanagari script form of</i> aghosa (“voiceless”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Devanagari script form of</i> aṅga</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Devanagari script form of</i> aṅguli (“finger”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Devanagari script form of</i> aṅgulī, <i>which is</i> inflection of <i>अङ्गुलि</i>:## nominative singular## nominative/vocative/accusative plural &#x20;(aṅguli, “finger”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Devanagari script form of</i> acci</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Devanagari script form of</i> accha</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Devanagari script form of</i> accharā (“celestial nymph”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Devanagari script form of</i> acchā, ablative singular & nominative/vocative plural of <i>अच्छ</i> &#x20;(accha, “bear”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Devanagari script form of</i> acche, locative singular & accusative plural of <i>अच्छ</i> &#x20;(accha, “bear”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Devanagari script form of</i> accho, nominative singular of <i>अच्छ</i> &#x20;(accha, “bear”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Devanagari script form of</i> aja</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Devanagari script form of</i> ajagara (“python”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Devanagari script form of</i> ajagaro, <i>which is</i> nominative singular of <i>अजगर</i> &#x20;(ajagara, “python”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Devanagari script form of</i> ajā</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Devanagari script form of</i> ajina (“antelope skin”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li><i>Devanagari script form of</i> ajjava (“straight, upright”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li><i>Devanagari script form of</i> añña (“other”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li><i>Devanagari script form of</i> aṭṭha (“eight”)</li></ol></html><h3>Pali</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li><i>Devanagari script form of</i> aṭ